\id GEN - Berean Standard Bible
\h Genesis
\toc2 Genesis
\toc1 Genesis
\mt1 Genesis
\c 1
\s1 The Creation
\r (John 1:1–5; Hebrews 11:1–3)
\m
\v 1 In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.
\b
\m
\v 2 Now the earth was formless and void, and darkness was over the surface of the deep. And the Spirit of God was hovering over the surface of the waters.
\s2 The First Day
\b
\pmo
\v 3 And God said, “Let there be light,” \f + \fr 1:3 \ft Cited in 2 Corinthians 4:6\f* and there was light.
\v 4 And God saw that the light was good, and He separated the light from the darkness.
\v 5 God called the light “day,” and the darkness He called “night.”
\b
\pmo And there was evening, and there was morning—the first day.\f + \fr 1:5 \ft Literally day one\f*
\s2 The Second Day
\b
\pmo
\v 6 And God said, “Let there be an expanse \f + \fr 1:6 \ft Or a canopy or a firmament or a vault; also in verses 7, 8, 14, 15, 17, and 20\f* between the waters, to separate the waters from the waters.”
\v 7 So God made the expanse and separated the waters beneath it from the waters above. And it was so.
\v 8 God called the expanse “sky.”
\b
\pmo And there was evening, and there was morning—the second day.
\s2 The Third Day
\b
\pmo
\v 9 And God said, “Let the waters under the sky be gathered into one place, so that the dry land may appear.” And it was so.
\v 10 God called the dry land “earth,” and the gathering of waters He called “seas.” And God saw that it was good.
\b
\pmo
\v 11 Then God said, “Let the earth bring forth vegetation: seed-bearing plants and fruit trees, each bearing fruit with seed according to its kind.” And it was so.
\v 12 The earth produced vegetation: seed-bearing plants according to their kinds and trees bearing fruit with seed according to their kinds. And God saw that it was good.
\b
\pmo
\v 13 And there was evening, and there was morning—the third day.
\s2 The Fourth Day
\b
\pmo
\v 14 And God said, “Let there be lights in the expanse of the sky to distinguish between the day and the night, and let them be signs to mark the seasons and days and years.
\v 15 And let them serve as lights in the expanse of the sky to shine upon the earth.” And it was so.
\b
\pmo
\v 16 God made two great lights: the greater light to rule the day and the lesser light to rule the night. And He made the stars as well.
\b
\pmo
\v 17 God set these lights in the expanse of the sky to shine upon the earth,
\v 18 to preside over the day and the night, and to separate the light from the darkness. And God saw that it was good.
\b
\pmo
\v 19 And there was evening, and there was morning—the fourth day.
\s2 The Fifth Day
\b
\pmo
\v 20 And God said, “Let the waters teem with living creatures, and let birds fly above the earth in the open expanse of the sky.”
\v 21 So God created the great sea creatures and every living thing that moves, with which the waters teemed according to their kinds, and every winged bird after its kind. And God saw that it was good.
\b
\pmo
\v 22 Then God blessed them and said, “Be fruitful and multiply and fill the waters of the seas, and let birds multiply on the earth.”
\b
\pmo
\v 23 And there was evening, and there was morning—the fifth day.
\s2 The Sixth Day
\b
\pmo
\v 24 And God said, “Let the earth bring forth living creatures according to their kinds: livestock, land crawlers, and beasts of the earth according to their kinds.” And it was so.
\v 25 God made the beasts of the earth according to their kinds, the livestock according to their kinds, and everything that crawls upon the earth according to its kind. And God saw that it was good.
\b
\pmo
\v 26 Then God said, “Let Us make man in Our image, after Our likeness, to rule over the fish of the sea and the birds of the air, over the livestock, and over all the earth itself \f + \fr 1:26 \ft MT; Syriac and over all the beasts of the earth\f* and every creature that crawls upon it.”
\b
\q1
\v 27 So God created man in His own image;
\q2 in the image of God He created him;
\q2 male and female He created them.\f + \fr 1:27 \ft Cited in Matthew 19:4 and Mark 10:6\f*
\b
\pmo
\v 28 God blessed them and said to them, “Be fruitful and multiply, and fill the earth and subdue it; rule over the fish of the sea and the birds of the air and every creature that crawls upon the earth.”
\b
\pmo
\v 29 Then God said, “Behold, I have given you every seed-bearing plant on the face of all the earth, and every tree whose fruit contains seed. They will be yours for food.
\v 30 And to every beast of the earth and every bird of the air and every creature that crawls upon the earth—everything that has the breath of life in it—I have given every green plant for food.” And it was so.
\b
\pmo
\v 31 And God looked upon all that He had made, and indeed, it was very good.
\b
\pmo And there was evening, and there was morning—the sixth day.
\c 2
\s1 The Seventh Day
\r (Exodus 16:22–30; Hebrews 4:1–11)
\m 
\v 1 Thus the heavens and the earth were completed in all their vast array. 
\v 2 And by the seventh day God had finished the work He had been doing; so on that day He rested from all His work.\f + \fr 2:2 \ft Cited in Hebrews 4:4\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Then God blessed the seventh day and sanctified it, because on that day He rested from all the work of creation that He had accomplished. 
\s1 Man and Woman in the Garden
\b
\m 
\v 4 This is the account of the heavens and the earth when they were created, in the day that the LORD \f + \fr 2:4 \ft LORD or GOD, with capital letters, represents the proper name of the God of Israel and the one true God, transliterated from the Hebrew as YHWH; here and throughout the Scriptures.\f* God made them. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Now no shrub of the field had yet appeared on the earth, nor had any plant of the field sprouted, for the LORD God had not yet sent rain upon the earth, and there was no man to cultivate the ground. 
\v 6 But springs \f + \fr 2:6 \ft Or mist\f* welled up from the earth and watered the whole surface of the ground. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 \zdate|Q70899:P569\*Then the LORD God formed \zwd man|Q70899\zwd* from the dust of the ground and breathed the breath of life into his nostrils, and \zwd the man|Q70899\zwd* became a living being.\f + \fr 2:7 \ft Or a living soul; cited in 1 Corinthians 15:45\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 8 And the LORD God planted a garden in Eden, in the east, where He placed \zwd the man|Q70899\zwd* He had formed. 
\v 9 Out of the ground the LORD God gave growth to every tree that is pleasing to the eye and good for food. And in the middle of the garden were the tree of life and the tree of the knowledge of good and evil. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Now a river flowed out of Eden to water the garden, and from there it branched into four headwaters: 
\b
\li1 
\v 11 The name of the first river is the Pishon; it winds through the whole land of Havilah, where there is gold. 
\v 12 And the gold of that land is pure, and bdellium and onyx are found there. 
\b
\li1 
\v 13 The name of the second river is the Gihon; it winds through the whole land of Cush. 
\b
\li1 
\v 14 The name of the third river is the Tigris; it runs along the east side of Assyria. 
\b
\li1 And the fourth river is the Euphrates. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 Then the LORD God took \zwd the man|Q70899\zwd* and placed him in the Garden of Eden to cultivate and keep it. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 And the LORD God commanded him, “You may eat freely from every tree of the garden, 
\v 17 but you must not eat from the tree of the knowledge of good and evil; for in the day that you eat of it, you will surely die.” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 The LORD God also said, “It is not good for \zwd the man|Q70899\zwd* to be alone. I will make for him a suitable helper.” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 And out of the ground the LORD God formed every beast of the field and every bird of the air, and He brought them to \zwd the man|Q70899\zwd* to see what he would name each one. And whatever \zwd the man|Q70899\zwd* called each living creature, that was its name. 
\v 20 \zwd The man|Q70899\zwd* gave names to all the livestock, to the birds of the air, and to every beast of the field. But for \zwd Adam|Q70899\zwd* \f + \fr 2:20 \ft Or the man, as in verses 19 and 21\f* no suitable helper was found. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 So the LORD God caused \zwd the man|Q70899\zwd* to fall into a deep sleep, and while he slept, He took one of \zwd the man|Q70899\zwd*’s ribs \f + \fr 2:21 \ft Or took part of the man’s side; similarly in verse 22\f* and closed up the area with flesh. 
\v 22 And from the rib that the LORD God had taken from \zwd the man|Q70899\zwd*, He made \zwd a woman|Q830183\zwd* and brought her to him. 
\v 23 And \zwd the man|Q70899\zwd* said: 
\b
\q1 “This is now bone of my bones 
\q2 and flesh of my flesh; 
\q1 she shall be called ‘woman,’ 
\q2 for out of man she was taken.” 
\b
\m 
\v 24 For this reason a man will leave his father and mother and be united to his wife, and they will become one flesh.\f + \fr 2:24 \ft LXX and the two will become one flesh; cited in Matthew 19:5, Mark 10:7–8, 1 Corinthians 6:16, and Ephesians 5:31\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 25 And \zwd the man|Q70899\zwd* and \zwd his wife|Q830183\zwd* were both naked, and they were not ashamed. 
\c 3
\s1 The Serpent’s Deception
\r (Romans 5:12–21)
\m 
\v 1 Now the serpent \f + \fr 3:1 \ft Hebrew nachash, translated in this chapter as serpent, is translated in most cases as snake.\f* was more crafty than any beast of the field that the LORD God had made. And he said to \zwd the woman|Q830183\zwd*, “Did God really say, ‘You must not eat from any tree in the garden?’” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 \zwd The woman|Q830183\zwd* answered the serpent, “We may eat the fruit of the trees of the garden, 
\v 3 but about the fruit of the tree in the middle of the garden, God has said, ‘You must not eat of it or touch it, or you will die.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 “You will not surely die,” the serpent told \zwd the woman|Q830183\zwd*. 
\v 5 “For God knows that in the day you eat of it, your eyes will be opened and you will be like God, knowing good and evil.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 When \zwd the woman|Q830183\zwd* saw that the tree was good for food and pleasing to the eyes, and that it was desirable for obtaining wisdom, she took the fruit and ate it. She also gave some to \zwd her husband|Q70899\zwd* who was with her, and he ate it. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 And the eyes of \zwd both of them|Q70899,Q830183\zwd* were opened, and they knew that they were naked; so they sewed together fig leaves and made coverings for themselves. 
\s1 God Arraigns Adam and Eve
\b
\m 
\v 8 Then \zwd the man|Q70899\zwd* and \zwd his wife|Q830183\zwd* heard the voice of the LORD God walking in the garden in the breeze \f + \fr 3:8 \ft Or at the breezy (time); Hebrew unto the Ruach\f* of the day, and they hid themselves from the presence of the LORD God among the trees of the garden. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 But the LORD God called out to \zwd the man|Q70899\zwd*, “Where are you?” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 “I heard Your voice in the garden,” he replied, “and I was afraid because I was naked; so I hid myself.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 “Who told you that you were naked?” asked the LORD God. “Have you eaten from the tree of which I commanded you not to eat?” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 And \zwd the man|Q70899\zwd* answered, “\zwd The woman|Q830183\zwd* whom You gave me, she gave me fruit from the tree, and I ate it.” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 Then the LORD God said to \zwd the woman|Q830183\zwd*, “What is this you have done?” 
\b
\m “The serpent deceived me,” she replied, “and I ate.” 
\s1 The Fate of the Serpent
\b
\m 
\v 14 So the LORD God said to the serpent: 
\b
\q1 “Because you have done this, 
\q2 cursed are you above all livestock 
\q2 and every beast of the field! 
\q1 On your belly will you go, 
\q2 and dust you will eat, 
\q2 all the days of your life. 
\q1 
\v 15 And I will put enmity between you and \zwd the woman|Q830183\zwd*, 
\q2 and between your seed and her seed. 
\q1 He will crush your head, 
\q2 and you will strike his heel.\f + \fr 3:15 \ft Or He will bruise your head, and you will bruise his heel. The same Hebrew root for crush, bruise, or strike appears twice in this verse.\f*” 
\s1 The Punishment of Mankind
\b
\m 
\v 16 To \zwd the woman|Q830183\zwd* He said: 
\b
\q1 “I will sharply increase your pain in childbirth; 
\q2 in pain you will bring forth children. 
\q1 Your desire will be for your husband,\f + \fr 3:16 \ft Or You will desire to control your husband\f* 
\q2 and he will rule over you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 And to \zwd Adam|Q70899\zwd* He said: 
\b
\q1 “Because you have listened to the voice of \zwd your wife|Q830183\zwd* 
\q2 and have eaten from the tree 
\q2 of which I commanded you not to eat, 
\q1 cursed is the ground because of you; 
\q2 through toil you will eat of it 
\q2 all the days of your life. 
\q1 
\v 18 Both thorns and thistles it will yield for you, 
\q2 and you will eat the plants of the field. 
\q1 
\v 19 By the sweat of your brow 
\q2 you will eat your bread, 
\q1 until you return to the ground—
\q2 because out of it were you taken. 
\q1 For dust you are, 
\q2 and to dust you shall return.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 And \zwd Adam|Q70899\zwd* named his wife \zwd Eve|Q830183\zwd*,\f + \fr 3:20 \ft Eve sounds like the Hebrew for giving life or living.\f* because she would be the mother of all the living. 
\s1 The Expulsion from Paradise
\b
\m 
\v 21 And the LORD God made garments of skin for \zwd Adam|Q70899\zwd* and \zwd his wife|Q830183\zwd*, and He clothed them. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 Then the LORD God said, “Behold, \zwd the man|Q70899\zwd* has become like one of Us, knowing good and evil. And now, lest he reach out his hand and take also from the tree of life, and eat, and live forever...” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 Therefore the LORD God banished him from the Garden of Eden to work the ground from which he had been taken. 
\v 24 So He drove out \zwd the man|Q70899\zwd* and stationed cherubim on the east side of the Garden of Eden, along with a whirling sword of flame to guard the way to the tree of life. 
\c 4
\s1 Cain and Abel
\r (Hebrews 11:4)
\m 
\v 1 And \zwd Adam|Q70899\zwd* had relations with \zwd his wife Eve|Q830183\zwd*, and she conceived and gave birth to \zwd Cain|Q205365\zwd*.\f + \fr 4:1 \ft Cain sounds like the Hebrew for acquired or brought forth.\f* 
\b
\m “With the help of the LORD I have brought forth a man,” she said. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 Later she gave birth to \zwd \zwd Cain|id="Q205365" t="2"\zwd*’s brother Abel|Q313421\zwd*. 
\b
\m Now \zwd Abel|Q313421\zwd* was a keeper of sheep, while \zwd Cain|Q205365\zwd* was a tiller of the soil. 
\v 3 So in the course of time, \zwd Cain|Q205365\zwd* brought some of the fruit of the soil as an offering to the LORD, 
\v 4 while \zwd Abel|Q313421\zwd* brought the best portions of the firstborn of his flock. 
\b
\m And the LORD looked with favor on \zwd Abel|Q313421\zwd* and his offering, 
\v 5 but He had no regard for \zwd Cain|Q205365\zwd* and his offering. So \zwd Cain|Q205365\zwd* became very angry, and his countenance fell. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 “Why are you angry,” said the LORD to \zwd Cain|Q205365\zwd*, “and why has your countenance fallen? 
\v 7 If you do what is right, will you not be accepted? But if you refuse to do what is right, sin is crouching at your door; it desires you,\f + \fr 4:7 \ft Or it desires to control you\f* but you must master it.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 Then \zwd Cain|Q205365\zwd* said to \zwd his brother Abel|Q313421\zwd*, “Let us go out to the field.” \f + \fr 4:8 \ft SP, LXX, Syriac, and Vulgate; Hebrew Then Cain spoke to his brother Abel.\f* And while they were in the field, \zwd Cain|Q205365\zwd* rose up against \zwd his brother Abel|Q313421\zwd* and killed him. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 And the LORD said to \zwd Cain|Q205365\zwd*, “Where is \zwd your brother Abel|Q313421\zwd*?” 
\b
\m “I do not know!” he answered. “Am I \zwd my brother|Q313421\zwd*’s keeper?” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 “What have you done?” replied the LORD. “The voice of \zwd your brother|Q313421\zwd*’s blood cries out to Me from the ground. 
\v 11 Now you are cursed and banished from the ground, which has opened its mouth to receive \zwd your brother|Q313421\zwd*’s blood from your hand. 
\v 12 When you till the ground, it will no longer yield its produce to you. You will be a fugitive and a wanderer on the earth.” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 But \zwd Cain|Q205365\zwd* said to the LORD, “My punishment \f + \fr 4:13 \ft Or guilt or sin\f* is greater than I can bear. 
\v 14 Behold, this day You have driven me from the face of the earth, and from Your face I will be hidden; I will be a fugitive and a wanderer on the earth, and whoever finds me will kill me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 “Not so!”\f + \fr 4:15 \ft LXX, Vulgate, and Syriac; Hebrew “Very well!” or “Therefore:”\f* replied the LORD. “If anyone slays \zwd Cain|Q205365\zwd*, then \zwd Cain|Q205365\zwd* will be avenged sevenfold.” And the LORD placed a mark on \zwd Cain|Q205365\zwd*, so that no one who found him would kill him. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 So \zwd Cain|Q205365\zwd* went out from the presence of the LORD and settled in the land of Nod,\f + \fr 4:16 \ft Nod means wandering.\f* east of Eden. 
\s1 The Descendants of Cain
\b
\m 
\v 17 And \zwd Cain|Q205365\zwd* had relations with \zwd his wife|Q134373856\zwd*, and she conceived and gave birth to \zwd Enoch|Q2495184\zwd*. Then \zwd Cain|Q205365\zwd* built a city and named it after \zwd his son Enoch|Q2495184\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 Now to \zwd Enoch|Q2495184\zwd* was born \zwd Irad|Q3364264\zwd*, and \zwd Irad|Q3364264\zwd* was the father of \zwd Mehujael|Q10329365\zwd*, and \zwd Mehujael|Q10329365\zwd* was the father of \zwd Methusael|Q10329289\zwd*, and \zwd Methusael|Q10329289\zwd* was the father of \zwd Lamech|Q155852\zwd*. 
\v 19 And \zwd Lamech|Q155852\zwd* married two women, one named \zwd Adah|Q1809123\zwd* and the other \zwd Zillah|Q203082\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 \zwd Adah|Q1809123\zwd* gave birth to \zwd Jabal|Q1676803\zwd*; he was the father of those who dwell in tents and raise livestock. 
\v 21 And his brother’s name was \zwd Jubal|Q1432204\zwd*; he was the father of all who play the harp and flute. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 And \zwd Zillah|Q203082\zwd* gave birth to \zwd Tubal-cain|Q1356181\zwd*, a forger of every implement of bronze and iron. And \zwd the sister|Q1542154\zwd* of \zwd Tubal-cain|Q1356181\zwd* was \zwd Naamah|Q1542154\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 Then \zwd Lamech|Q155852\zwd* said to \zwd his wives|Q1809123,Q203082\zwd*: 
\b
\q1 “\zwd Adah|Q1809123\zwd* and \zwd Zillah|Q203082\zwd*, hear my voice; 
\q2 \zwd wives of \zwd Lamech|id="Q155852" t="2"\zwd*|Q1809123,Q203082\zwd*, listen to my speech. 
\q1 For I have slain a man for wounding me, 
\q2 a young man for striking me. 
\q1 
\v 24 If \zwd Cain|id="Q205365" t="2"\zwd* is avenged sevenfold, 
\q2 then \zwd Lamech|Q155852\zwd* seventy-sevenfold.”\f + \fr 4:24 \ft Hebrew; LXX can be translated as either seventy times seven or seventy-sevenfold; see also Matthew 18:22.\f* 
\s1 Seth and Enosh
\b
\m 
\v 25 \zdate|Q107626:P569\*And \zwd Adam|Q70899\zwd* again had relations with \zwd his wife|Q830183\zwd*, and she gave birth to a son and named him \zwd Seth|Q107626\zwd*,\f + \fr 4:25 \ft Seth probably means granted or appointed.\f* saying, “God has granted me another seed in place of \zwd Abel|id="Q313421" t="2"\zwd*, since \zwd Cain|id="Q205365" t="2"\zwd* killed him.” 
\b
\m 
\v 26 And to \zwd Seth|Q107626\zwd* also a son was born, and he called him \zwd Enosh|Q682531\zwd*. 
\b
\m At that time men began to call upon \f + \fr 4:26 \ft Or to proclaim; some translators to invoke or to call themselves by or to profane \f* the name of the LORD. 
\c 5
\s1 The Descendants of Adam
\r (1 Chronicles 1:1–3)
\m 
\v 1 This is the book of the generations of \zwd Adam|Q70899\zwd*. In the day that God created man, He made him in His own likeness. 
\v 2 Male and female He created them,\f + \fr 5:2 \ft Cited in Matthew 19:4 and Mark 10:6\f* and He blessed them. And in the day they were created, He called them “man.”\f + \fr 5:2 \ft Hebrew Adam\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 3 \zdate|Q70899:P569+P130Y\*When \zwd Adam|Q70899\zwd* was 130 years old, he had \zwd a son|Q107626\zwd* in his own likeness, after his own image; and he named him \zwd Seth|Q107626\zwd*. 
\v 4 And after he had become the father of \zwd Seth|Q107626\zwd*, \zwd Adam|Q70899\zwd* lived 800 years and had other sons and daughters. 
\v 5 So \zwd Adam|Q70899\zwd* lived a total of 930 years, and then he died. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 \zdate|Q107626:P569+P105Y\*When \zwd Seth|Q107626\zwd* was 105 years old, he became the father of \zwd Enosh|Q682531\zwd*. 
\v 7 And after he had become the father of \zwd Enosh|Q682531\zwd*, \zwd Seth|Q107626\zwd* lived 807 years and had other sons and daughters. 
\v 8 So \zwd Seth|Q107626\zwd* lived a total of 912 years, and then he died. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 \zdate|Q682531:P569+P90Y\*When \zwd Enosh|Q682531\zwd* was 90 years old, he became the father of \zwd Kenan|Q844433\zwd*. 
\v 10 And after he had become the father of \zwd Kenan|Q844433\zwd*, \zwd Enosh|Q682531\zwd* lived 815 years and had other sons and daughters. 
\v 11 So \zwd Enosh|Q682531\zwd* lived a total of 905 years, and then he died. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 \zdate|Q844433:P569+P90Y\*When \zwd Kenan|Q844433\zwd* was 70 years old, he became the father of \zwd Mahalalel|Q10297002\zwd*. 
\v 13 And after he had become the father of \zwd Mahalalel|Q10297002\zwd*, \zwd Kenan|Q844433\zwd* lived 840 years and had other sons and daughters. 
\v 14 So \zwd Kenan|Q844433\zwd* lived a total of 910 years, and then he died. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 \zdate|Q10297002:P569+P65Y\*When \zwd Mahalalel|Q10297002\zwd* was 65 years old, he became the father of \zwd Jared|Q927410\zwd*. 
\v 16 And after he had become the father of \zwd Jared|Q927410\zwd*, \zwd Mahalalel|Q10297002\zwd* lived 830 years and had other sons and daughters. 
\v 17 So \zwd Mahalalel|Q10297002\zwd* lived a total of 895 years, and then he died. 
\s1 God Takes Up Enoch
\r (Hebrews 11:5)
\b
\m 
\v 18 \zdate|Q927410:P569+P162Y\*When \zwd Jared|Q927410\zwd* was 162 years old, he became the father of \zwd Enoch|Q213027\zwd*. 
\v 19 And after he had become the father of \zwd Enoch|Q213027\zwd*, \zwd Jared|Q927410\zwd* lived 800 years and had other sons and daughters. 
\v 20 So \zwd Jared|Q927410\zwd* lived a total of 962 years, and then he died. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 \zdate|Q213027:P569+P65Y\*When \zwd Enoch|Q213027\zwd* was 65 years old, he became the father of \zwd Methuselah|Q156290\zwd*. 
\v 22 And after he had become the father of \zwd Methuselah|Q156290\zwd*, \zwd Enoch|Q213027\zwd* walked with God \f + \fr 5:22 \ft LXX pleased God; also in verse 24\f* 300 years and had other sons and daughters. 
\v 23 So \zwd Enoch|Q213027\zwd* lived a total of 365 years. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 \zwd Enoch|Q213027\zwd* walked with God, and then he was no more, because God had taken him away.\f + \fr 5:24 \ft LXX and he was not found, because God had taken him away; cited in Hebrews 11:5\f* 
\s1 From Methuselah to Noah
\b
\m 
\v 25 \zdate|Q156290:P569+P187Y\*When \zwd Methuselah|Q156290\zwd* was 187 years old, he became the father of \zwd Lamech|Q10921342\zwd*. 
\v 26 And after he had become the father of \zwd Lamech|Q10921342\zwd*, \zwd Methuselah|Q156290\zwd* lived 782 years and had other sons and daughters. 
\v 27 So \zwd Methuselah|Q156290\zwd* lived a total of 969 years, and then he died. 
\b
\m 
\v 28 \zdate|Q10921342:P569+P182Y\*When \zwd Lamech|Q10921342\zwd* was 182 years old, he had \zwd a son|Q81422\zwd*. 
\v 29 And he named him \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd*,\f + \fr 5:29 \ft Noah sounds like the Hebrew for rest or comfort.\f* saying, “May this one comfort us in the labor and toil of our hands caused by the ground that the LORD has cursed.” 
\v 30 And after he had become the father of \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd*, \zwd Lamech|Q10921342\zwd* lived 595 years and had other sons and daughters. 
\v 31 So \zwd Lamech|Q10921342\zwd* lived a total of 777 years, and then he died. 
\b
\m 
\v 32 \zdate|Q81422:P569+P500Y\*After \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* was 500 years old, he became the father of \zwd Shem|Q200902\zwd*, \zwd Ham|Q229702\zwd*, and \zwd Japheth|Q200637\zwd*. 
\c 6
\s1 Corruption on the Earth
\r (Matthew 24:36–51)
\m 
\v 1 Now when men began to multiply on the face of the earth and daughters were born to them, 
\v 2 the sons of God saw that the daughters of men were beautiful, and they took as wives whomever they chose. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 So the LORD said, “My Spirit will not contend with man forever,\f + \fr 6:3 \ft LXX and Syriac My Spirit will not remain in man forever\f* for he is mortal; his days shall be 120 years.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 The Nephilim were on the earth in those days—and afterward as well—when the sons of God had relations with the daughters of men. And they bore them children who became the mighty men of old, men of renown. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Then the LORD saw that the wickedness of man was great upon the earth, and that every inclination of the thoughts of his heart was altogether evil all the time. 
\v 6 And the LORD regretted that He had made man on the earth, and He was grieved in His heart. 
\v 7 So the LORD said, “I will blot out man, whom I have created, from the face of the earth—every man and beast and crawling creature and bird of the air—for I am grieved that I have made them.” 
\s1 Noah’s Favor with God
\b
\m 
\v 8 \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd*, however, found favor in the eyes of the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 This is the account of \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd*. \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* was a righteous man, blameless in his generation; \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* walked with God. 
\v 10 And \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* had three sons: \zwd Shem|Q200902\zwd*, \zwd Ham|Q229702\zwd*, and \zwd Japheth|Q200637\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 Now the earth was corrupt in the sight of God, and full of violence. 
\v 12 And God looked upon the earth and saw that it was corrupt; for all living creatures \f + \fr 6:12 \ft Literally all flesh; similarly in verses 13, 17, and 19\f* on the earth had corrupted their ways. 
\s1 Preparing the Ark
\r (Hebrews 11:7)
\b
\m 
\v 13 Then God said to \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd*, “The end of all living creatures has come before Me, because through them the earth is full of violence. Now behold, I will destroy both them and the earth. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Make for yourself an ark of gopher wood;\f + \fr 6:14 \ft Gopher is an unknown kind of tree; possibly cypress or cedar.\f* make rooms in the ark and coat it with pitch inside and out. 
\v 15 And this is how you are to build it: The ark is to be 300 cubits long, 50 cubits wide, and 30 cubits high.\f + \fr 6:15 \ft The ark was approximately 450 feet long, 75 feet wide, and 45 feet high (137.2 meters long, 22.9 meters wide, and 13.7 meters high).\f* 
\v 16 You are to make a roof \f + \fr 6:16 \ft Or skylight or window\f* for the ark, finish its walls a cubit from the top,\f + \fr 6:16 \ft A cubit is approximately 18 inches or 45.7 centimeters.\f* place a door in the side of the ark, and build lower, middle, and upper decks. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 And behold, I will bring floodwaters upon the earth to destroy every creature under the heavens that has the breath of life. Everything on the earth will perish. 
\v 18 But I will establish My covenant with you, and you will enter the ark—you and your sons and \zwd your wife|Q107442921\zwd* and \zwd your sons’ wives|\zwd* with you. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 And you are to bring two of every living creature into the ark—male and female—to keep them alive with you. 
\v 20 Two of every kind of bird and animal and crawling creature will come to you to be kept alive. 
\v 21 You are also to take for yourself every kind of food that is eaten and gather it as food for yourselves and for the animals.” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 So \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* did everything precisely as God had commanded him. 
\c 7
\s1 The Great Flood
\r (2 Peter 3:1–7)
\m 
\v 1 Then the LORD said to \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd*, “Go into the ark, you and all your family, because I have found you righteous in this generation. 
\v 2 You are to take with you seven pairs of \f + \fr 7:2 \ft Or by sevens; also in verse 3\f* every kind of clean animal, a male and its mate; a pair of every kind of unclean animal, a male and its mate; 
\v 3 and seven pairs of every kind of bird of the air, male and female, to preserve their offspring on the face of all the earth. 
\v 4 For seven days from now I will send rain on the earth for forty days and forty nights, and I will wipe from the face of the earth every living thing I have made.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 And \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* did all that the LORD had commanded him. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 \zdate|Q81422:P569+P600Y>Iyyar!17+P-7D\*Now \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* was 600 years old when the floodwaters came upon the earth. 
\v 7 And \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* and his wife, with \zwd his sons|Q200902,Q229702,Q200637\zwd* and \zwd their wives|\zwd*, entered the ark to escape the waters of the flood. 
\v 8 The clean and unclean animals, the birds, and everything that crawls along the ground 
\v 9 came to \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* to enter the ark, two by two, male and female, as God had commanded \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 \zdate|Q81422:P569+P600Y>Iyyar!17\*And after seven days the floodwaters came upon the earth. 
\v 11 In the six hundredth year of \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd*’s life, on the seventeenth day of the second month, all the fountains of the great deep burst forth, and the floodgates of the heavens were opened. 
\v 12 And the rain fell upon the earth for forty days and forty nights. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 On that very day \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* entered the ark, along with his sons \zwd Shem|Q200902\zwd*, \zwd Ham|Q229702\zwd*, and \zwd Japheth|Q200637\zwd*, and \zwd his wife|Q107442921\zwd*, and \zwd the three wives of \zwd his sons|id="Q200902,Q229702,Q200637" t="2"\zwd*|\zwd*—
\v 14 they and every kind of wild animal, livestock, crawling creature, bird, and winged creature. 
\v 15 They came to \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* to enter the ark, two by two of every creature \f + \fr 7:15 \ft Literally of all flesh; similarly in verses 16 and 21\f* with the breath of life. 
\v 16 And they entered, the male and female of every living thing, as God had commanded \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd*. Then the LORD shut him in. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 For forty days the flood kept coming on the earth, and the waters rose and lifted the ark high above the earth. 
\v 18 So the waters continued to surge and rise greatly on the earth, and the ark floated on the surface of the waters. 
\v 19 Finally, the waters completely prevailed upon the earth, so that all the high mountains under all the heavens were covered. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 The waters rose and covered the mountaintops to a depth of fifteen cubits.\f + \fr 7:20 \ft 15 cubits is approximately 22.5 feet or 6.9 meters.\f* 
\v 21 And every living thing that moved upon the earth perished—birds, livestock, animals, every creature that swarms upon the earth, and all mankind. 
\v 22 Of all that was on dry land, everything that had the breath of life in its nostrils died. 
\v 23 And every living thing on the face of the earth was destroyed—man and livestock, crawling creatures and birds of the air; they were blotted out from the earth, and only \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* and \zwd those with him in the ark|\zwd* remained. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 \zdate|Q81422:P569+P600Y>Iyyar!17+P40D\*And the waters prevailed upon the earth for 150 days.\zdate|Q81422:P569+P600Y>Iyyar!17+P190D\* 
\c 8
\s1 The Ark Rests on Ararat
\m 
\v 1 But God remembered \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* and all the animals and livestock that were with him in the ark. And God sent a wind over the earth, and the waters began to subside. 
\v 2 The springs of the deep and the floodgates of the heavens were closed, and the rain from the sky was restrained. 
\v 3 The waters receded steadily from the earth, and after 150 days the waters had gone down. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 \zdate|Q81422:P569+P600Y>Iyyar!17>Tishri!17\*On the seventeenth day of the seventh month, the ark came to rest on the mountains of Ararat. 
\v 5 \zdate|Q81422:P569+P600Y>Iyyar!17>Tishri!17>Tevet!1\*And the waters continued to recede until the tenth month, and on the first day of the tenth month the tops of the mountains became visible. 
\s1 Noah Sends a Raven and a Dove
\b
\m 
\v 6 After forty days \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* opened the window he had made in the ark 
\v 7 and sent out a raven. It kept flying back and forth until the waters had dried up from the earth. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 Then \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* sent out \f + \fr 8:8 \ft Literally sent out from him or sent out from it\f* a dove to see if the waters had receded from the surface of the ground. 
\v 9 But the dove found no place to rest her foot, and she returned to him in the ark, because the waters were still covering the surface of all the earth. So he reached out his hand and brought her back inside the ark. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* waited seven more days and again sent out the dove from the ark. 
\v 11 And behold, the dove returned to him in the evening with a freshly plucked olive leaf in her beak. So \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* knew that the waters had receded from the earth. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 And \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* waited seven more days and sent out the dove again, but this time she did not return to him. 
\s1 Exiting the Ark
\b
\m 
\v 13 \zdate|Q81422:P569+P601Y>Nisan!1\*In \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd*’s six hundred and first year, on the first day of the first month, the waters had dried up from the earth. So \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* removed the covering from the ark and saw that the surface of the ground was dry. 
\v 14 \zdate|Q81422:P569+P601Y>Nisan!1>Iyyar!27\*By the twenty-seventh day of the second month, the earth was fully dry. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 Then God said to \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd*, 
\v 16 “Come out of the ark, you and your wife, along with \zwd your sons|Q200902,Q229702,Q200637\zwd* and \zwd their wives|\zwd*. 
\v 17 Bring out all the living creatures that are with you—birds, livestock, and everything that crawls upon the ground—so that they can spread out over the earth and be fruitful and multiply upon it.” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 So \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* came out, along with \zwd his sons|Q200902,Q229702,Q200637\zwd* and \zwd his wife|Q107442921\zwd* and \zwd \zwd his sons|id="Q200902,Q229702,Q200637" t="2"\zwd*’ wives|\zwd*. 
\v 19 Every living creature, every creeping thing, and every bird—everything that moves upon the earth—came out of the ark, kind by kind. 
\s1 Noah Builds an Altar
\b
\m 
\v 20 Then \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* built an altar to the LORD. And taking from every kind of clean animal and clean bird, he offered burnt offerings on the altar. 
\v 21 When the LORD smelled the pleasing aroma, He said in His heart, “Never again will I curse the ground because of man, even though every inclination of his heart is evil from his youth. And never again will I destroy all living creatures as I have done. 
\b
\q1 
\v 22 As long as the earth endures, 
\q2 seedtime and harvest, 
\q1 cold and heat, 
\q2 summer and winter, 
\q1 day and night 
\q2 shall never cease.” 
\c 9
\s1 The Covenant of the Rainbow
\m 
\v 1 And God blessed \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* and \zwd his sons|Q200902,Q229702,Q200637\zwd* and said to them, “Be fruitful and multiply and fill the earth. 
\v 2 The fear and dread of you will fall on every living creature on the earth, every bird of the air, every creature that crawls on the ground, and all the fish of the sea. They are delivered into your hand. 
\v 3 Everything that lives and moves will be food for you; just as I gave you the green plants, I now give you all things. 
\v 4 But you must not eat meat with its lifeblood still in it. 
\v 5 And surely I will require the life of any man or beast by whose hand your lifeblood is shed. I will demand an accounting from anyone who takes the life of his fellow man: 
\b
\q1 
\v 6 Whoever sheds the blood of man, 
\q2 by man his blood will be shed; 
\q1 for in His own image 
\q2 God has made mankind. 
\q1 
\v 7 But as for you, 
\q2 be fruitful and multiply; 
\q1 spread out across the earth 
\q2 and multiply upon it.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 Then God said to \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* and \zwd his sons|Q200902,Q229702,Q200637\zwd* with him, 
\v 9 “Behold, I now establish My covenant with you and your descendants after you, 
\v 10 and with every living creature that was with you—the birds, the livestock, and every beast of the earth—every living thing that came out of the ark. 
\v 11 And I establish My covenant with you: Never again will all life be cut off by the waters of a flood; never again will there be a flood to destroy the earth.” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 And God said, “This is the sign of the covenant I am making between Me and you and every living creature with you, a covenant for all generations to come: 
\v 13 I have set My rainbow in the clouds, and it will be a sign of the covenant between Me and the earth. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Whenever I form clouds over the earth and the rainbow appears in the clouds, 
\v 15 I will remember My covenant between Me and you and every living creature of every kind. Never again will the waters become a flood to destroy all life. 
\v 16 And whenever the rainbow appears in the clouds, I will see it and remember the everlasting covenant between God and every living creature of every kind that is on the earth.” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 So God said to \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd*, “This is the sign of the covenant that I have established between Me and every creature on the earth.”\zdate|Q81422:P569+P601Y>Nisan!1>Iyyar!27\* 
\s1 Noah’s Shame and Canaan’s Curse
\b
\m 
\v 18 The sons of \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* who came out of the ark were \zwd Shem|Q200902\zwd*, \zwd Ham|Q229702\zwd*, and \zwd Japheth|Q200637\zwd*. And \zwd Ham|Q229702\zwd* was the father of \zwd Canaan|Q204797\zwd*. 
\v 19 These three were the sons of \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd*, and from them the whole earth was populated. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 Now \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd*, a man of the soil, proceeded \f + \fr 9:20 \ft Or was the first\f* to plant a vineyard. 
\v 21 But when he drank some of its wine, he became drunk and uncovered himself inside his tent. 
\v 22 And \zwd Ham|Q229702\zwd*, the father of \zwd Canaan|Q204797\zwd*, saw \zwd his father|Q81422\zwd*’s nakedness and told \zwd his two brothers|Q200902,Q200637\zwd* outside. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 Then \zwd Shem|Q200902\zwd* and \zwd Japheth|Q200637\zwd* took a garment and placed it across their shoulders, and walking backward, they covered their father’s nakedness. Their faces were turned away so that they did not see their father’s nakedness. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 When \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* awoke from his drunkenness and learned what \zwd his youngest son|Q229702\zwd* had done to him, 
\v 25 he said, 
\b
\q1 “Cursed be \zwd Canaan|Q204797\zwd*! 
\q2 A servant of servants 
\q2 shall he be to his brothers.” 
\s1 Shem’s Blessing and Noah’s Death
\b
\m 
\v 26 He also declared: 
\b
\q1 “Blessed be the LORD, the God of \zwd Shem|Q200902\zwd*! 
\q2 May \zwd Canaan|Q204797\zwd* be the servant of \zwd Shem|Q200902\zwd*. 
\q1 
\v 27 May God expand the territory of \zwd Japheth|Q200637\zwd*;\f + \fr 9:27 \ft Japheth sounds like the Hebrew for expand.\f* 
\q2 may he dwell in the tents of \zwd Shem|Q200902\zwd*, 
\q2 and may \zwd Canaan|Q204797\zwd* be his servant.” 
\b
\m 
\v 28 After the flood, \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* lived 350 years. 
\v 29 So \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd* lived a total of 950 years, and then he died. 
\c 10
\s1 The Table of Nations
\r (1 Chronicles 1:4–27)
\m 
\v 1 This is the account of \zwd Noah|Q81422\zwd*’s sons \zwd Shem|Q200902\zwd*, \zwd Ham|Q229702\zwd*, and \zwd Japheth|Q200637\zwd*, who also had sons after the flood. 
\s2 The Japhethites
\b
\m 
\v 2 The sons of \zwd Japheth|Q200637\zwd*: 
\b
\pmo \zwd Gomer|Q1148172\zwd*, \zwd Magog|Q1964706\zwd*, \zwd Madai|Q1790796\zwd*, \zwd Javan|Q1684266\zwd*, \zwd Tubal|Q1481766\zwd*, \zwd Meshech|Q30973\zwd*, and \zwd Tiras|Q1481751\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 The sons of \zwd Gomer|Q1148172\zwd*: 
\b
\pmo \zwd Ashkenaz|Q722770\zwd*, \zwd Riphath|Q2918052\zwd*, and \zwd Togarmah|Q1068499\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 And the sons of \zwd Javan|Q1684266\zwd*: 
\b
\pmo \zwd Elishah|Q1330627\zwd*, \zwd Tarshish|Q44826\zwd*, the Kittites, and the Rodanites.\f + \fr 10:4 \ft SP and some MT manuscripts (see also LXX and 1 Chronicles 1:7); most MT manuscripts Dodanites\f* 
\v 5 From these, the maritime peoples separated into their territories, according to their languages, by clans within their nations. 
\s2 The Hamites
\b
\m 
\v 6 The sons of \zwd Ham|Q229702\zwd*: 
\b
\pmo \zwd Cush|Q1138595\zwd*, \zwd Mizraim|Q1369215\zwd*, \zwd Put|Q946939\zwd*, and \zwd Canaan|Q204797\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 The sons of \zwd Cush|Q1138595\zwd*: 
\b
\pmo \zwd Seba|Q23639444\zwd*, \zwd Havilah|Q32945196\zwd*, \zwd Sabtah|Q13143737\zwd*,\f + \fr 10:7 \ft Sabtah is a variant of Sabta; see 1 Chronicles 1:9.\f* \zwd Raamah|Q3085764\zwd*, and \zwd Sabteca|Q124169540\zwd*. 
\b
\m And the sons of \zwd Raamah|Q3085764\zwd*: 
\b
\pmo \zwd Sheba|Q125399193\zwd* and \zwd Dedan|Q124305605\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 \zwd Cush|Q1138595\zwd* was the father of \zwd Nimrod|Q201861\zwd*, who began to be a mighty one \f + \fr 10:8 \ft Or who established himself as a mighty warrior or who became the first fearless leader\f* on the earth. 
\v 9 He was a mighty hunter before \f + \fr 10:9 \ft Or in defiance of; twice in this verse\f* the LORD; so it is said, “Like \zwd Nimrod|Q201861\zwd*, a mighty hunter before the LORD.” 
\v 10 His kingdom began in Babylon, Erech, Accad, and Calneh, in the land of Shinar.\f + \fr 10:10 \ft That is, Babylonia\f* 
\v 11 From that land he went forth into Assyria, where he built Nineveh, Rehoboth-Ir, Calah, 
\v 12 and Resen, which is between Nineveh and the great city of Calah. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 \zwd Mizraim|Q1369215\zwd* was the father of the Ludites, the Anamites, the Lehabites, the Naphtuhites, 
\v 14 the Pathrusites, the Casluhites (from whom the Philistines came), and the Caphtorites.\f + \fr 10:14 \ft Some translators adjust the Hebrew word order to the Casluhites, and the Caphtorites (from whom the Philistines came); see also Jeremiah 47:4 and Amos 9:7.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 15 And \zwd Canaan|Q204797\zwd* was the father of \zwd Sidon|Q3541553\zwd* his firstborn,\f + \fr 10:15 \ft Or of the Sidonians, the foremost\f* and of the Hittites, 
\v 16 the Jebusites, the Amorites, the Girgashites, 
\v 17 the Hivites, the Arkites, the Sinites, 
\v 18 the Arvadites, the Zemarites, and the Hamathites. 
\b
\m Later the Canaanite clans were scattered, 
\v 19 and the borders of \zwd Canaan|id="Q204797" t="3"\zwd* extended from \zwd Sidon|id="Q3541553" t="3"\zwd* toward Gerar as far as Gaza, and then toward Sodom, Gomorrah, Admah, and Zeboiim, as far as Lasha. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 These are the sons of \zwd Ham|Q229702\zwd* according to their clans, languages, lands, and nations. 
\s2 The Semites
\b
\m 
\v 21 And sons were also born to \zwd Shem|Q200902\zwd*, the older brother of \zwd Japheth|id="Q200637" t="2"\zwd*;\f + \fr 10:21 \ft Or Shem, whose older brother was Japheth\f* \zwd Shem|Q200902\zwd* was the forefather of all the sons of \zwd Eber|id="Q502282" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 The sons of \zwd Shem|Q200902\zwd*: 
\b
\pmo \zwd Elam|Q1147704\zwd*, \zwd Asshur|Q2426606\zwd*, \zwd Arphaxad|Q457520\zwd*, \zwd Lud|Q1815870\zwd*, and \zwd Aram|Q625667\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 The sons of \zwd Aram|Q625667\zwd*: 
\b
\pmo \zwd Uz|Q2558734\zwd*, \zwd Hul|Q2898616\zwd*, \zwd Gether|Q2910407\zwd*, and \zwd Mash|Q13418757\zwd*.\f + \fr 10:23 \ft Hebrew; LXX and 1 Chronicles 1:17 Meshech\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 24 \zwd Arphaxad|Q457520\zwd* was the father of \zwd Shelah|Q1827950\zwd*,\f + \fr 10:24 \ft Hebrew; LXX (see also Luke 3:35–36) And Arphaxad was the father of Cainan, and Cainan was the father of Shelah,\f* and \zwd Shelah|Q1827950\zwd* was the father of \zwd Eber|Q502282\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 \zdate|Q1648259:P569\*Two sons were born to \zwd Eber|Q502282\zwd*: One was named \zwd Peleg|Q1648259\zwd*,\f + \fr 10:25 \ft Peleg means division.\f* because in his days the earth was divided, and his brother was named \zwd Joktan|Q1553059\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 And \zwd Joktan|Q1553059\zwd* was the father of \zwd Almodad|Q2905798\zwd*, \zwd Sheleph|Q7493584\zwd*, \zwd Hazarmaveth|Q2905463\zwd*, \zwd Jerah|Q18276083\zwd*, 
\v 27 \zwd Hadoram|Q5638029\zwd*, \zwd Uzal|Q7904500\zwd*, \zwd Diklah|Q63433898\zwd*, 
\v 28 \zwd Obal|Q115760490\zwd*, \zwd Abimael|Q14925390\zwd*, \zwd Sheba|Q138636936\zwd*, 
\v 29 \zwd Ophir|Q125345983\zwd*, \zwd Havilah|Q138636935\zwd*, and \zwd Jobab|Q44123555\zwd*. All these were sons of \zwd Joktan|Q1553059\zwd*. 
\v 30 Their territory extended from Mesha to Sephar, in the eastern hill country. 
\b
\m 
\v 31 These are the sons of \zwd Shem|Q200902\zwd*, according to their clans, languages, lands, and nations. 
\b
\m 
\v 32 All these are the clans of \zwd \zwd Noah|id="Q81422" t="2"\zwd*’s sons|Q200902,Q229702,Q200637\zwd*, according to their generations and nations. From these the nations of the earth spread out after the flood. 
\c 11
\s1 The Tower of Babel
\r (Deuteronomy 32:8; Acts 2:1–13)
\m 
\v 1 Now the whole world had one language and a common form of speech. 
\v 2 And as people journeyed eastward,\f + \fr 11:2 \ft Or from the east or in the east\f* they found a plain in the land of Shinar \f + \fr 11:2 \ft That is, Babylonia\f* and settled there. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 And they said to one another, “Come, let us make bricks and bake them thoroughly.” So they used brick instead of stone, and tar instead of mortar. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 “Come,” they said, “let us build for ourselves a city with a tower that reaches to the heavens, that we may make a name for ourselves and not be scattered over the face of all the earth.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Then the LORD came down to see the city and the tower that the sons of men were building. 
\v 6 And the LORD said, “If they have begun to do this as one people speaking the same language, then nothing they devise will be beyond them. 
\v 7 Come, let Us go down and confuse their language, so that they will not understand one another’s speech.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 So the LORD scattered them from there over the face of all the earth, and they stopped building the city. 
\v 9 That is why it is called Babel,\f + \fr 11:9 \ft Or Babylon; the Hebrew word for Babel sounds like the Hebrew for confused.\f* for there the LORD confused the language of the whole world, and from that place the LORD scattered them over the face of all the earth. 
\s1 Genealogy from Shem to Abram
\r (1 Chronicles 1:17–27)
\b
\m 
\v 10 \zdate|Q200902:P569+P100Y\*This is the account of \zwd Shem|Q200902\zwd*. Two years after the flood, when \zwd Shem|Q200902\zwd* was 100 years old, he became the father of \zwd Arphaxad|Q457520\zwd*. 
\v 11 And after he had become the father of \zwd Arphaxad|Q457520\zwd*, \zwd Shem|Q200902\zwd* lived 500 years and had other sons and daughters. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 \zdate|Q457520:P569+P35Y\*When \zwd Arphaxad|Q457520\zwd* was 35 years old, he became the father of \zwd Shelah|Q1827950\zwd*. 
\v 13 And after he had become the father of \zwd Shelah|Q1827950\zwd*, \zwd Arphaxad|Q457520\zwd* lived 403 years and had other sons and daughters.\f + \fr 11:13 \ft Hebrew; LXX (see also Luke 3:35–36) 12 When Arphaxad was 135 years old, he became the father of Cainan. 13 And after he had become the father of Cainan, Arphaxad lived 430 years and had other sons and daughters, and then he died. When Cainan had lived 130 years, he became the father of Shelah. And after he had become the father of Shelah, Cainan lived 330 years and had other sons and daughters. Note that LXX also adds 100 years to the ages of Shelah, Eber, Peleg, Reu, Serug, and Nahor in this genealogy.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 14 \zdate|Q1827950:P569+P30Y\*When \zwd Shelah|Q1827950\zwd* was 30 years old, he became the father of \zwd Eber|Q502282\zwd*. 
\v 15 And after he had become the father of \zwd Eber|Q502282\zwd*, \zwd Shelah|Q1827950\zwd* lived 403 years and had other sons and daughters. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 \zdate|Q502282:P569+P34Y\*When \zwd Eber|Q502282\zwd* was 34 years old, he became the father of \zwd Peleg|Q1648259\zwd*. 
\v 17 And after he had become the father of \zwd Peleg|Q1648259\zwd*, \zwd Eber|Q502282\zwd* lived 430 years and had other sons and daughters. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 \zdate|Q1648259:P569+P30Y\*When \zwd Peleg|Q1648259\zwd* was 30 years old, he became the father of \zwd Reu|Q2040793\zwd*. 
\v 19 And after he had become the father of \zwd Reu|Q2040793\zwd*, \zwd Peleg|Q1648259\zwd* lived 209 years and had other sons and daughters. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 \zdate|Q2040793:P569+P32Y\*When \zwd Reu|Q2040793\zwd* was 32 years old, he became the father of \zwd Serug|Q1161313\zwd*. 
\v 21 And after he had become the father of \zwd Serug|Q1161313\zwd*, \zwd Reu|Q2040793\zwd* lived 207 years and had other sons and daughters. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 \zdate|Q1161313:P569+P30Y\*When \zwd Serug|Q1161313\zwd* was 30 years old, he became the father of \zwd Nahor|Q888890\zwd*. 
\v 23 And after he had become the father of \zwd Nahor|Q888890\zwd*, \zwd Serug|Q1161313\zwd* lived 200 years and had other sons and daughters. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 \zdate|Q888890:P569+P29Y\*When \zwd Nahor|Q888890\zwd* was 29 years old, he became the father of \zwd Terah|Q586541\zwd*. 
\v 25 And after he had become the father of \zwd Terah|Q586541\zwd*, \zwd Nahor|Q888890\zwd* lived 119 years and had other sons and daughters. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 \zdate|Q586541:P569+P70Y\*When \zwd Terah|Q586541\zwd* was 70 years old, he became the father of \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd*, \zwd Nahor|Q6323112\zwd*, and \zwd Haran|Q1199156\zwd*. 
\s1 Terah’s Descendants
\b
\m 
\v 27 This is the account of \zwd Terah|Q586541\zwd*. \zwd Terah|Q586541\zwd* became the father of \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd*, \zwd Nahor|Q6323112\zwd*, and \zwd Haran|Q1199156\zwd*. And \zwd Haran|Q1199156\zwd* became the father of \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd*. 
\v 28 During his father \zwd Terah|Q586541\zwd*’s lifetime, \zwd Haran|Q1199156\zwd* died in his native land, in Ur of the Chaldeans. 
\b
\m 
\v 29 And \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* and \zwd Nahor|Q6323112\zwd* took wives for themselves. \zwd \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd*’s wife|Q194808\zwd* was named \zwd Sarai|Q194808\zwd*, and \zwd \zwd Nahor|Q6323112\zwd*’s wife|Q1752707\zwd* was named \zwd Milcah|Q1752707\zwd*; she was the daughter of \zwd Haran|Q1199156\zwd*, who was the father of both \zwd Milcah|Q1752707\zwd* and \zwd Iscah|Q6079192\zwd*. 
\v 30 But \zwd Sarai|Q194808\zwd* was barren; she had no children. 
\b
\m 
\v 31 And \zwd Terah|Q586541\zwd* took \zwd his son Abram|Q9181\zwd*, \zwd his grandson Lot son of \zwd Haran|id="Q1199156" t="2"\zwd*|Q40574\zwd*, and \zwd his daughter-in-law Sarai the wife of \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd*|Q194808\zwd*, and they set out from Ur of the Chaldeans for the land of Canaan. But when they arrived in Haran, they settled there. 
\v 32 \zwd Terah|Q586541\zwd* lived 205 years, and he died in Haran. 
\c 12
\s1 The Call of Abram
\r (Genesis 26:1–5; Acts 7:1–8)
\m 
\v 1 Then the LORD said to \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd*, “Leave your country, your kindred, and your father’s household, and go to the land I will show you.\f + \fr 12:1 \ft Cited in Acts 7:3\f* 
\b
\q1 
\v 2 I will make you into a great nation, 
\q2 and I will bless you; 
\q1 I will make your name great, 
\q2 so that you will be a blessing. 
\q1 
\v 3 I will bless those who bless you 
\q2 and curse those who curse you; 
\q1 and all the families of the earth 
\q2 will be blessed through you.\f + \fr 12:3 \ft See Galatians 3:8\f*” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 \zdate|Q9181:P569+P75Y\*So \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* departed, as the LORD had directed him, and \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd* went with him. \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* was seventy-five years old when he left Haran. 
\v 5 And \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* took \zwd his wife Sarai|Q194808\zwd*, \zwd his nephew Lot|Q40574\zwd*, and all the possessions and people they had acquired in Haran, and set out for the land of Canaan. 
\b
\m When they came to the land of Canaan, 
\v 6 \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* traveled through the land as far as the site of the Oak \f + \fr 12:6 \ft Or Terebinth or Great Tree\f* of Moreh at Shechem. And at that time the Canaanites were in the land. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Then the LORD appeared to \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* and said, “I will give this land to your offspring.\f + \fr 12:7 \ft Cited in Galatians 3:16\f*” So \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* built an altar there to the LORD, who had appeared to him. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 From there \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* moved on to the hill country east of Bethel and pitched his tent, with Bethel to the west and Ai to the east. There he built an altar to the LORD, and he called on the name of the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 And \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* journeyed on toward the Negev. 
\s1 Abram and Sarai in Egypt
\b
\m 
\v 10 Now there was a famine in the land. So \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* went down to Egypt to live there for a while because the famine was severe. 
\v 11 As he was about to enter Egypt, he said to \zwd his wife Sarai|Q194808\zwd*, “Look, I know that you are a beautiful woman, 
\v 12 and when the Egyptians see you, they will say, ‘This is his wife.’ Then they will kill me but will let you live. 
\v 13 Please say you are my sister, so that I will be treated well for your sake, and on account of you my life will be spared.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 So when \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* entered Egypt, the Egyptians saw that \zwd the woman|Q194808\zwd* was very beautiful. 
\v 15 When \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*’s officials saw \zwd Sarai|Q194808\zwd*, they commended her to him, and she was taken into the palace of \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*. 
\v 16 He treated \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* well on her account, and \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* acquired sheep and cattle, male and female donkeys, menservants and maidservants, and camels. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 The LORD, however, afflicted \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* and his household with severe plagues because of \zwd \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd*’s wife Sarai|Q194808\zwd*. 
\v 18 So \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* summoned \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* and asked, “What have you done to me? Why didn’t you tell me she was \zwd your wife|Q194808\zwd*? 
\v 19 Why did you say, ‘She is my sister,’ so that I took her as my wife? Now then, here is \zwd your wife|Q194808\zwd*. Take her and go!” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 Then \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* gave his men orders concerning \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd*, and they sent him away with \zwd his wife|Q194808\zwd* and all his possessions. 
\c 13
\s1 Abram and Lot Part Ways
\m 
\v 1 So \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* went up out of Egypt into the Negev—he and \zwd his wife|Q194808\zwd* and all his possessions—and \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd* was with him. 
\v 2 And \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* had become extremely wealthy in livestock and silver and gold. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 From the Negev he journeyed from place to place toward Bethel, until he came to the place between Bethel and Ai where his tent had formerly been pitched, 
\v 4 to the site where he had built the altar. And there \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* called on the name of the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Now \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd*, who was traveling with \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd*, also had flocks and herds and tents. 
\v 6 But the land was unable to support both of them while they stayed together, for they had so many possessions that they were unable to coexist. 
\v 7 And there was discord between the herdsmen of \zwd Abram|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd* and the herdsmen of \zwd Lot|id="Q40574" t="2"\zwd*. At that time the Canaanites and the Perizzites were also living in the land. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 So \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* said to \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd*, “Please let there be no contention between you and me, or between your herdsmen and my herdsmen. After all, we are kinsmen. 
\v 9 Is not the whole land before you? Now separate yourself from me. If you go to the left, I will go to the right; if you go to the right, I will go to the left.” 
\s1 Lot Proceeds toward Sodom
\b
\m 
\v 10 And \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd* looked out and saw that the whole plain of the Jordan, all the way to Zoar, was well watered like the garden of the LORD, like the land of Egypt. (This was before the LORD destroyed Sodom and Gomorrah.) 
\v 11 So \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd* chose the whole plain of the Jordan for himself and set out toward the east. And \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* and \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd* parted company. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* lived in the land of Canaan, but \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd* settled in the cities of the plain and pitched his tent toward Sodom. 
\v 13 But the men of Sodom were wicked, sinning greatly against the LORD. 
\s1 God Renews the Promise to Abram
\b
\m 
\v 14 After \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd* had departed, the LORD said to \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd*, “Now lift up your eyes from the place where you are, and look to the north and south and east and west, 
\v 15 for all the land that you see, I will give to you and your offspring forever.\f + \fr 13:15 \ft Cited in Galatians 3:16\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 16 I will make your offspring like the dust of the earth, so that if one could count the dust of the earth, then your offspring could be counted. 
\v 17 Get up and walk around the land, through its length and breadth, for I will give it to you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 So \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* moved his tent and went to live near the Oaks \f + \fr 13:18 \ft Or Terebinths or Great Trees\f* of Mamre at Hebron, where he built an altar to the LORD. 
\c 14
\s1 The War of the Kings
\m
\v 1 \zdate|\*In those days \zwd Amraphel king of Shinar|Q2844493\zwd*,\f + \fr 14:1 \ft That is, Babylonia; also in verse 9\f* \zwd Arioch king of Ellasar|Q2712606\zwd*, \zwd Chedorlaomer king of Elam|Q2118973\zwd*, and \zwd Tidal king of Goiim|Q7800767\zwd*
\v 2 went to war against \zwd Bera king of Sodom|Q4890957\zwd*, \zwd Birsha king of Gomorrah|Q106079868\zwd*, \zwd Shinab king of Admah|Q9351421\zwd*, \zwd Shemeber king of Zeboiim|Q125415429\zwd*, and \zwd the king of Bela|\zwd* (that is, Zoar).
\b
\m
\v 3 \zwd The latter five|Q4890957,Q106079868,Q9351421,Q125415429\zwd* came as allies to the Valley of Siddim (that is, the Salt Sea \f + \fr 14:3 \ft That is, the Dead Sea\f*).
\v 4 For twelve years they had been subject to \zwd Chedorlaomer|Q2118973\zwd*, but in the thirteenth year they rebelled.
\b
\m
\v 5 \zdate|\*In the fourteenth year, \zwd Chedorlaomer|Q2118973\zwd* and \zwd the kings allied with him|Q2844493,Q2712606,Q7800767\zwd* went out and defeated the Rephaites in Ashteroth-karnaim, the Zuzites in Ham, the Emites in Shaveh-kiriathaim,
\v 6 and the Horites in the area of Mount Seir, as far as El-paran, which is near the desert.
\v 7 Then they turned back to invade En-mishpat (that is, Kadesh), and they conquered the whole territory of the Amalekites, as well as the Amorites who lived in Hazazon-tamar.
\b
\m
\v 8 Then \zwd the king of Sodom|Q4890957\zwd*, \zwd the king of Gomorrah|Q106079868\zwd*, \zwd the king of Admah|Q9351421\zwd*, \zwd the king of Zeboiim|Q125415429\zwd*, and \zwd the king of Bela|\zwd* (that is, Zoar) marched out and arrayed themselves for battle in the Valley of Siddim \f + \fr 14:8 \ft That is, the Valley of the Dead Sea\f*
\v 9 against \zwd Chedorlaomer king of Elam|Q2118973\zwd*, \zwd Tidal king of Goiim|Q7800767\zwd*, \zwd Amraphel king of Shinar|Q2844493\zwd*, and \zwd Arioch king of Ellasar|Q2712606\zwd*—four kings against five.
\s1 Abram Rescues Lot
\b
\m
\v 10 Now the Valley of Siddim was full of tar pits, and as \zwd the kings of Sodom and Gomorrah|Q4890957,Q106079868\zwd* fled, some men fell into the pits, but the survivors fled to the hill country.
\b
\m
\v 11 \zwd The four kings|Q2844493,Q2712606,Q2118973,Q7800767\zwd* seized all the goods of Sodom and Gomorrah and all their food, and they went on their way.
\v 12 They also carried off \zwd \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd*’s nephew |Q40574\zwd*Lot and his possessions, since \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd* was living in Sodom.
\b
\m
\v 13 Then an escapee came and reported this to \zwd Abram the Hebrew|Q9181\zwd*. Now \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* was living near the Oaks \f + \fr 14:13 \ft Or Terebinths or Great Trees\f* of \zwd Mamre the Amorite|Q125295074\zwd*, a brother of \zwd Eshcol|id="Q125022203" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd Aner|id="Q124541960" t="2"\zwd*, all of whom were bound by treaty \f + \fr 14:13 \ft Forms of the Hebrew berit are translated in most passages as covenant.\f* to \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd*.
\v 14 And when \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* heard that his relative had been captured, he mobilized the 318 trained men born in his household, and they set out in pursuit as far as Dan.
\b
\m
\v 15 During the night, \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* divided his forces and routed \zwd Chedorlaomer|Q2118973\zwd*’s army, pursuing them as far as Hobah, north of Damascus.
\v 16 He retrieved all the goods, as well as \zwd his relative Lot|Q40574\zwd* and his possessions, together with the women and the rest of the people.
\s1 Melchizedek Blesses Abram
\r (Psalms 110:1–7; Hebrews 7:1–10)
\b
\m
\v 17 After \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* returned from defeating \zwd Chedorlaomer|Q2118973\zwd* and \zwd the kings allied with him|Q2844493,Q2712606,Q7800767\zwd*, \zwd the king of Sodom|Q4890957\zwd* went out to meet him in the Valley of Shaveh (that is, the King’s Valley).
\b
\m
\v 18 Then \zwd Melchizedek king of Salem|Q219395\zwd* brought out bread and wine—since he was priest of God Most High \f + \fr 14:18 \ft Hebrew El-Elyon; also in verses 19, 20, and 22; cited in Hebrews 7:1\f*—
\v 19 and he blessed \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* and said:
\b
\q1 “Blessed be \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* by God Most High,
\q2 Creator of heaven and earth,
\q1
\v 20 and blessed be God Most High,
\q2 who has delivered your enemies into your hand.”
\b
\m Then \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* gave \zwd Melchizedek|Q219395\zwd* a tenth of everything.
\b
\m
\v 21 \zwd The king of Sodom|Q4890957\zwd* said to \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd*, “Give me the people, but take the goods for yourself.”
\b
\m
\v 22 But \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* replied to \zwd the king of Sodom|Q4890957\zwd*, “I have raised my hand to the LORD God Most High, Creator of heaven and earth,
\v 23 that I will not accept even a thread, or a strap of a sandal, or anything that belongs to you, lest you should say, ‘I have made \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* rich.’
\v 24 I will accept nothing but what my men have eaten and the share for the men who went with me—\zwd Aner|Q124541960\zwd*, \zwd Eshcol|Q125022203\zwd*, and \zwd Mamre|Q125295074\zwd*. They may take their portion.”
\c 15
\s1 God’s Covenant with Abram
\r (Romans 4:1–12; Hebrews 11:8–19)
\m 
\v 1 After these events, the word of the LORD came to \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* in a vision: 
\b
\q1 “Do not be afraid, \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd*. 
\q2 I am your shield, 
\q2 your very great reward.” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 But \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* replied, “O Lord GOD, what can You give me, since I remain childless, and the heir of my house is \zwd Eliezer of Damascus|Q14933103\zwd*?” 
\v 3 \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* continued, “Behold, You have given me no offspring, so a servant in my household will be my heir.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Then the word of the LORD came to \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd*, saying, “This one will not be your heir, but one who comes from your own body will be your heir.” 
\v 5 And the LORD took him outside and said, “Now look to the heavens and count the stars, if you are able.” Then He told him, “So shall your offspring be.”\f + \fr 15:5 \ft Cited in Romans 4:18\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 6 \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* believed the LORD, and it was credited to him as righteousness.\f + \fr 15:6 \ft Cited in Romans 4:3, Romans 4:22, Galatians 3:6, and James 2:23\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 7 The LORD also told him, “I am the LORD, who brought you out of Ur of the Chaldeans to give you this land to possess.” 
\s1 God Confirms His Promise
\r (Numbers 34:1–15; Romans 4:13–25)
\b
\m 
\v 8 But \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* replied, “Lord GOD, how can I know that I will possess it?” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 And the LORD said to him, “Bring Me a heifer, a goat, and a ram, each three years old, along with a turtledove and a young pigeon.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 So \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* brought all these to Him, split each of them down the middle, and laid the halves opposite each other. The birds, however, he did not cut in half. 
\v 11 And the birds of prey descended on the carcasses, but \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* drove them away. 
\v 12 As the sun was setting, \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* fell into a deep sleep, and suddenly great terror and darkness overwhelmed him. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 Then the LORD said to \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd*, “Know for certain that your descendants will be strangers in a land that is not their own, and they will be enslaved and mistreated four hundred years. 
\v 14 But I will judge the nation they serve as slaves, and afterward they will depart with many possessions.\f + \fr 15:14 \ft Cited in Acts 7:6–7\f* 
\v 15 You, however, will go to your fathers in peace and be buried at a ripe old age. 
\v 16 In the fourth generation your descendants will return here, for the iniquity of the Amorites is not yet complete.” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 When the sun had set and darkness had fallen, behold, a smoking firepot and a flaming torch appeared and passed between the halves of the carcasses. 
\v 18 On that day the LORD made a covenant with \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd*, saying, “To your descendants I have given this land—from the river of Egypt to the great River Euphrates—
\v 19 the land of the Kenites, Kenizzites, Kadmonites, 
\v 20 Hittites, Perizzites, Rephaites, 
\v 21 Amorites, Canaanites, Girgashites, and Jebusites.” 
\c 16
\s1 Hagar and Ishmael
\m 
\v 1 Now \zwd Abram’s wife Sarai|Q194808\zwd* had borne him no children, but she had an Egyptian maidservant named \zwd Hagar|Q214617\zwd*. 
\v 2 So \zwd Sarai|Q194808\zwd* said to \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd*, “Look now, the LORD has prevented me from bearing children. Please go to \zwd my maidservant|Q214617\zwd*; perhaps I can build a family by her.” 
\b
\m And \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* listened to the voice of \zwd Sarai|Q194808\zwd*. 
\v 3 \zdate|Q183403:P569+P-9M\*So after he had lived in Canaan for ten years, \zwd his wife Sarai|Q194808\zwd* took \zwd her Egyptian maidservant Hagar|Q214617\zwd* and gave her to \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* to be his wife. 
\v 4 And he slept with \zwd Hagar|Q214617\zwd*, and she conceived. But when \zwd Hagar|Q214617\zwd* realized that she was pregnant, she began to despise \zwd her mistress|Q194808\zwd*.\f + \fr 16:4 \ft Or her mistress became despised in her sight\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Then \zwd Sarai|Q194808\zwd* said to \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd*, “May the wrong done to me be upon you! I delivered \zwd my servant|Q214617\zwd* into your arms, and ever since she saw that she was pregnant, she has treated me with contempt. May the LORD judge between you and me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 “Here,” said \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd*, “\zwd your servant|Q214617\zwd* is in your hands. Do whatever you want with her.” Then \zwd Sarai|Q194808\zwd* treated \zwd Hagar|Q214617\zwd* so harshly that she fled from her. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Now the angel \f + \fr 16:7 \ft Or Angel; also in verses 9, 10, and 11; corresponding pronouns may also be capitalized.\f* of the LORD found \zwd Hagar|Q214617\zwd* by a spring of water in the desert—the spring along the road to Shur. 
\v 8 “\zwd Hagar|Q214617\zwd*, servant of \zwd Sarai|id="Q194808" t="2"\zwd*,” he said, “where have you come from, and where are you going?” 
\b
\m “I am running away from \zwd my mistress Sarai|id="Q194808" t="2"\zwd*,” she replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 So the angel of the LORD told her, “Return to \zwd your mistress|Q194808\zwd* and submit to her authority.” 
\v 10 Then the angel added, “I will greatly multiply your offspring so that they will be too numerous to count.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 The angel of the LORD proceeded: 
\b
\q1 “Behold, you have conceived and will bear \zwd a son|id="Q183403" t="2"\zwd*. 
\q2 And you shall name him \zwd Ishmael|id="Q183403" t="2"\zwd*,\f + \fr 16:11 \ft Ishmael means God hears.\f* 
\q2 for the LORD has heard your cry of affliction. 
\q1 
\v 12 He will be a wild donkey of a man, 
\q2 and his hand will be against everyone, 
\q2 and everyone’s hand against him; 
\q1 he will live in hostility 
\q2 toward all his brothers.” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 So \zwd Hagar|Q214617\zwd* gave this name to the LORD who had spoken to her: “You are the God who sees me,\f + \fr 16:13 \ft Hebrew El-Roi\f*” for she said, “Here I have seen the One who sees me!” 
\v 14 Therefore the well was called Beer-lahai-roi.\f + \fr 16:14 \ft Beer-lahai-roi means well of the Living One who sees me.\f* It is located between Kadesh and Bered. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 And \zwd Hagar|Q214617\zwd* bore \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* a son, and \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* gave the name \zwd Ishmael|Q183403\zwd* to the son she had borne. 
\v 16 \zdate|Q183403:P569\*\zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* was eighty-six years old when \zwd Hagar|Q214617\zwd* bore \zwd Ishmael|Q183403\zwd* to him. 
\c 17
\s1 Abraham to Father Many Nations
\m 
\v 1 \zdate|Q9181:P569+P99Y\*When \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* was ninety-nine years old, the LORD appeared to him and said, “I am God Almighty.\f + \fr 17:1 \ft Hebrew El-Shaddai\f* Walk before Me and be blameless. 
\v 2 I will establish My covenant between Me and you, and I will multiply you exceedingly.” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Then \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd* fell facedown, and God said to him, 
\v 4 “As for Me, this is My covenant with you: You will be the father of many nations. 
\v 5 No longer will you be called \zwd Abram|Q9181\zwd*,\f + \fr 17:5 \ft Abram means exalted father.\f* but your name will be \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*,\f + \fr 17:5 \ft Abraham means father of many.\f* for I have made you a father of many nations.\f + \fr 17:5 \ft Cited in Romans 4:17\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 6 I will make you exceedingly fruitful; I will make nations of you, and kings will descend from you. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 I will establish My covenant as an everlasting covenant between Me and you and your descendants after you, to be your God and the God of your descendants after you. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 And to you and your descendants I will give the land where you are residing—all the land of Canaan—as an eternal possession; and I will be their God.” 
\s1 The Covenant of Circumcision
\b
\m 
\v 9 God also said to \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*, “You must keep My covenant—you and your descendants in the generations after you. 
\v 10 This is My covenant with you and your descendants after you, which you are to keep: Every male among you must be circumcised. 
\v 11 You are to circumcise the flesh of your foreskin, and this will be a sign of the covenant between Me and you. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 Generation after generation, every male must be circumcised when he is eight days old, including those born in your household and those purchased from a foreigner—even those who are not your offspring. 
\v 13 Whether they are born in your household or purchased, they must be circumcised. My covenant in your flesh will be an everlasting covenant. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 But if any male is not circumcised, he will be cut off from his people; he has broken My covenant.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 Then God said to \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*, “As for \zwd Sarai your wife|Q194808\zwd*, do not call her \zwd Sarai|Q194808\zwd*, for her name is to be \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd*.\f + \fr 17:15 \ft Both Sarai and Sarah mean princess; the change in spelling may reflect the difference in dialect between Ur and Canaan.\f* 
\v 16 And I will bless her and will surely give you a son by her. I will bless her, and she will be the mother of nations; kings of peoples will descend from her.” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* fell facedown. Then he laughed and said to himself, “Can a child be born to a man who is a hundred years old? Can \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd* give birth at the age of ninety?” 
\v 18 And \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* said to God, “O that \zwd Ishmael|Q183403\zwd* might live under Your blessing!” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 But God replied, “\zwd Your wife Sarah|Q194808\zwd* will indeed bear you a son, and you are to name him \zwd Isaac|id="Q671872" t="2"\zwd*.\f + \fr 17:19 \ft Isaac means he laughs.\f* I will establish My covenant with him as an everlasting covenant for his descendants after him. 
\v 20 As for \zwd Ishmael|Q183403\zwd*, I have heard you, and I will surely bless him; I will make him fruitful and multiply him greatly. He will become the father of \zwd twelve rulers|\zwd*, and I will make him into a great nation. 
\v 21 But I will establish My covenant with \zwd Isaac|id="Q671872" t="2"\zwd*, whom \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd* will bear to you at this time next year.” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 When He had finished speaking with \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*, God went up from him. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 On that very day \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* took \zwd his son Ishmael|Q183403\zwd* and all those born in his household or purchased with his money—every male among the members of \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*’s household—and he circumcised them, just as God had told him. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 So \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* was ninety-nine years old when he was circumcised, 
\v 25 and \zwd his son Ishmael|Q183403\zwd* was thirteen; 
\v 26 \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* and \zwd his son Ishmael|Q183403\zwd* were circumcised on the same day. 
\v 27 And all the men of \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*’s household—both servants born in his household and those purchased from foreigners—were circumcised with him.\zdate|Q9181:P569+P99Y\* 
\c 18
\s1 The Three Visitors
\m 
\v 1 Then the LORD appeared to \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* by the Oaks \f + \fr 18:1 \ft Or Terebinths or Great Trees\f* of Mamre in the heat of the day, while he was sitting at the entrance of his tent. 
\v 2 And \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* looked up and saw three men standing nearby. When he saw them, he ran from the entrance of his tent to meet them and bowed low to the ground. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 “My lord,” said \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*, “if I have found favor in your sight, please do not pass \zwd your servant|Q9181\zwd* by. 
\v 4 Let a little water be brought, that you may wash your feet and rest yourselves under the tree. 
\v 5 And I will bring a bit of bread so that you may refresh yourselves. This is why you have passed \zwd your servant|Q9181\zwd*’s way. After that, you may continue on your way.” 
\b
\m “Yes,” they replied, “you may do as you have said.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 So \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* hurried into the tent and said to \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd*, “Quick! Prepare three seahs of fine flour,\f + \fr 18:6 \ft 3 seahs is approximately 19.8 dry quarts or 21.9 liters (probably about 24.5 pounds or 11.1 kilograms of flour).\f* knead it, and bake some bread.” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Meanwhile, \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* ran to the herd, selected a tender and choice calf, and gave it to a servant, who hurried to prepare it. 
\v 8 Then \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* brought curds and milk and the calf that had been prepared, and he set them before the men and stood by them under the tree as they ate. 
\s1 Sarah Laughs at the Promise
\b
\m 
\v 9 “Where is \zwd your wife Sarah|Q194808\zwd*?” they asked. 
\b
\m “There, in the tent,” he replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Then the LORD said, “I will surely return to you at this time next year, and \zwd your wife Sarah|Q194808\zwd* will have a son!” 
\b
\m Now \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd* was behind him, listening at the entrance to the tent. 
\v 11 And \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* and \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd* were already old and well along in years; \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd* had passed the age of childbearing. 
\v 12 So she laughed to herself, saying, “After I am worn out and my master is old, will I now have this pleasure?” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 And the LORD asked \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*, “Why did \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd* laugh and say, ‘Can I really bear a child when I am old?’ 
\v 14 Is anything too difficult for the LORD? At the appointed time I will return to you—in about a year—and \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd* will have a son.”\f + \fr 18:14 \ft Cited in Romans 9:9\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 15 But \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd* was afraid, so she denied it and said, “I did not laugh.” 
\b
\m “No,” replied the LORD, “but you did laugh.” 
\s1 Abraham Intercedes for Sodom
\b
\m 
\v 16 When the men got up to leave, they looked out over Sodom, and \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* walked along with them to see them off. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 And the LORD said, “Shall I hide from \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd* what I am about to do? 
\v 18 \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd* will surely become a great and powerful nation, and through him all the nations of the earth will be blessed. 
\v 19 For I have chosen him, so that he will command his children and his household after him to keep the way of the LORD by doing what is right and just, in order that the LORD may bring upon \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd* what He has promised.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 Then the LORD said, “The outcry against Sodom and Gomorrah is great. Because their sin is so grievous, 
\v 21 I will go down to see if their actions fully justify the outcry that has reached Me. If not, I will find out.” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 And the two men turned away and went toward Sodom, but \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* remained standing before the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* stepped forward and said, “Will You really sweep away the righteous with the wicked? 
\v 24 What if there are fifty righteous ones in the city? Will You really sweep it away and not spare the place for the sake of the fifty righteous ones who are there? 
\v 25 Far be it from You to do such a thing—to kill the righteous with the wicked, so that the righteous and the wicked are treated alike. Far be it from You! Will not the Judge of all the earth do what is right?” 
\b
\m 
\v 26 So the LORD replied, “If I find fifty righteous ones within the city of Sodom, on their account I will spare the whole place.” 
\b
\m 
\v 27 Then \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* answered, “Now that I have ventured to speak to the Lord—though I am but dust and ashes—
\v 28 suppose the fifty righteous ones lack five. Will You destroy the whole city for the lack of five?” 
\b
\m He replied, “If I find forty-five there, I will not destroy it.” 
\b
\m 
\v 29 Once again \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* spoke to the LORD, “Suppose forty are found there?” 
\b
\m He answered, “On account of the forty, I will not do it.” 
\b
\m 
\v 30 Then \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* said, “May the Lord not be angry, but let me speak further. Suppose thirty are found there?” 
\b
\m He replied, “If I find thirty there, I will not do it.” 
\b
\m 
\v 31 And \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* said, “Now that I have ventured to speak to the Lord, suppose twenty are found there?” 
\b
\m He answered, “On account of the twenty, I will not destroy it.” 
\b
\m 
\v 32 Finally, \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* said, “May the Lord not be angry, but let me speak once more. Suppose ten are found there?” 
\b
\m And He answered, “On account of the ten, I will not destroy it.” 
\b
\m 
\v 33 When the LORD had finished speaking with \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*, He departed, and \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* returned home. 
\c 19
\s1 Lot Welcomes the Angels
\r (Judges 19:1–30)
\m 
\v 1 Now the two angels arrived at Sodom in the evening, and \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd* was sitting in the gateway of the city. When \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd* saw them, he got up to meet them, bowed facedown, 
\v 2 and said, “My lords, please turn aside into the house of \zwd your servant|Q40574\zwd*; wash your feet and spend the night. Then you can rise early and go on your way.” 
\b
\m “No,” they answered, “we will spend the night in the square.” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 But \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd* insisted so strongly that they followed him into his house. He prepared a feast for them and baked unleavened bread, and they ate. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Before they had gone to bed, all the men of the city of Sodom, both young and old, surrounded the house. 
\v 5 They called out to \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd*, saying, “Where are the men who came to you tonight? Send them out to us so we can have relations with them!” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd* went outside to meet them, shutting the door behind him. 
\v 7 “Please, my brothers,” he pleaded, “don’t do such a wicked thing! 
\v 8 Look, I have \zwd two daughters|Q109496487,Q109496488\zwd* who have never slept with a man. Let me bring them to you, and you can do to them as you please. But do not do anything to these men, for they have come under the protection of my roof.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 “Get out of the way!” they replied. And they declared, “This one came here as a foreigner, and he is already acting like a judge! Now we will treat you worse than them.” And they pressed in on \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd* and moved in to break down the door. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 But the men inside reached out, pulled \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd* into the house with them, and shut the door. 
\v 11 And they struck the men at the entrance, young and old, with blindness, so that they wearied themselves trying to find the door. 
\s1 Lot Flees to Zoar
\b
\m 
\v 12 Then the two men said to \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd*, “Do you have anyone else here—a son-in-law, your sons or daughters, or anyone else in the city who belongs to you? Get them out of here, 
\v 13 because we are about to destroy this place. For the outcry to the LORD against its people is so great that He has sent us to destroy it.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 So \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd* went out and spoke to \zwd the sons-in-law|\zwd* who were pledged in marriage to \zwd his daughters|Q109496487,Q109496488\zwd*. “Get up,” he said. “Get out of this place, for the LORD is about to destroy the city!” But \zwd his sons-in-law|\zwd* thought he was joking. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 At daybreak the angels hurried \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd* along, saying, “Get up! Take \zwd your wife|Q2575060\zwd* and \zwd your two daughters|Q109496487,Q109496488\zwd* who are here, or you will be swept away in the punishment of the city.” 
\v 16 But when \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd* hesitated, the men grabbed his hand and the hands of \zwd his wife|Q2575060\zwd* and \zwd his two daughters|Q109496487,Q109496488\zwd*. And they led them safely out of the city, because of the LORD’s compassion for them. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 As soon as the men had brought them out, one of them said, “Run for your lives! Do not look back, and do not stop anywhere on the plain! Flee to the mountains, or you will be swept away!” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 But \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd* replied, “No, my lords, please! 
\v 19 \zwd Your servant|Q40574\zwd* has indeed found favor in your sight, and you have shown me great kindness by sparing my life. But I cannot run to the mountains; the disaster will overtake me, and I will die. 
\v 20 Look, there is a town nearby where I can flee, and it is a small place. Please let me flee there—is it not a small place? Then my life will be saved.” 
\b
\m 
\v 21 “Very well,” he answered, “I will grant this request as well, and will not demolish the town you indicate. 
\v 22 Hurry! Run there quickly, for I cannot do anything until you reach it.” That is why the town was called Zoar.\f + \fr 19:22 \ft Zoar means small.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 23 And by the time the sun had risen over the land, \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd* had reached Zoar. 
\s1 The Destruction of Sodom and Gomorrah
\r (Luke 17:20–37)
\b
\m 
\v 24 Then the LORD rained down sulfur and fire on Sodom and Gomorrah—from the LORD out of the heavens. 
\v 25 Thus He destroyed these cities and the entire plain, including all the inhabitants of the cities and everything that grew on the ground. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 But \zwd \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd*’s wife|Q2575060\zwd* looked back, and she became a pillar of salt. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 Early the next morning, \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* got up and returned to the place where he had stood before the LORD. 
\v 28 He looked down toward Sodom and Gomorrah and all the land of the plain, and he saw the smoke rising from the land like smoke from a furnace. 
\b
\m 
\v 29 So when God destroyed the cities of the plain, He remembered \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*, and He brought \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd* out of the catastrophe that destroyed the cities where he had lived. 
\s1 Lot and His Daughters
\b
\m 
\v 30 \zwd Lot|Q40574\zwd* and \zwd his two daughters|Q109496487,Q109496488\zwd* left Zoar and settled in the mountains—for he was afraid to stay in Zoar—where they lived in a cave. 
\b
\m 
\v 31 One day \zwd the older daughter|Q109496487\zwd* said to \zwd the younger|Q109496488\zwd*, “\zwd Our father|Q40574\zwd* is old, and there is no man in the land to sleep with us, as is the custom over all the earth. 
\v 32 Come, let us get \zwd our father|Q40574\zwd* drunk with wine so we can sleep with him and preserve his line.” 
\b
\m 
\v 33 So that night they got \zwd their father|Q40574\zwd* drunk with wine, and \zwd the firstborn|Q109496487\zwd* went in and slept with her father; he was not aware when she lay down or when she got up. 
\b
\m 
\v 34 The next day \zwd the older daughter|Q109496487\zwd* said to \zwd the younger|Q109496488\zwd*, “Look, I slept with \zwd my father|Q40574\zwd* last night. Let us get him drunk with wine again tonight so you can go in and sleep with him and we can preserve \zwd our father|Q40574\zwd*’s line.” 
\b
\m 
\v 35 So again that night they got \zwd their father|Q40574\zwd* drunk with wine, and \zwd the younger daughter|Q109496488\zwd* went in and slept with him; he was not aware when she lay down or when she got up. 
\b
\m 
\v 36 Thus \zwd both of Lot’s daughters|Q109496487,Q109496488\zwd* became pregnant by \zwd their father|Q40574\zwd*. 
\v 37 \zwd The older daughter|Q109496487\zwd* gave birth to a son and named him \zwd Moab|Q1585141\zwd*.\f + \fr 19:37 \ft Moab sounds like the Hebrew for from my father.\f* He is the father of the Moabites of today. 
\v 38 \zwd The younger daughter|Q109496488\zwd* also gave birth to a son, and she named him \zwd Ben-ammi|Q9168580\zwd*.\f + \fr 19:38 \ft Ben-ammi means son of my people.\f* He is the father of the Ammonites of today. 
\c 20
\s1 Abraham, Sarah, and Abimelech
\m 
\v 1 Now \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* journeyed from there to the region of the Negev and settled between Kadesh and Shur. While he was staying in Gerar, 
\v 2 \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* said of \zwd his wife Sarah|Q194808\zwd*, “She is my sister.” So \zwd Abimelech king of Gerar|Q1343803\zwd* had \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd* brought to him. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 One night, however, God came to \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd* in a dream and told him, “You are as good as dead because of the woman you have taken, for she is a married woman.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Now \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd* had not gone near her, so he replied, “Lord, would You destroy a nation even though it is innocent? 
\v 5 Didn’t \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* tell me, ‘She is my sister’? And she herself said, ‘He is my brother.’ I have done this in the integrity of my heart and the innocence of my hands.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 Then God said to \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd* in the dream, “Yes, I know that you did this with a clear conscience, and so I have kept you from sinning against Me. That is why I did not let you touch her. 
\v 7 Now return \zwd \zwd the man|Q9181\zwd*’s wife|Q194808\zwd*, for he is a prophet; he will pray for you and you will live. But if you do not restore her, be aware that you will surely die—you and all who belong to you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 Early the next morning \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd* got up and summoned all his servants; and when he described to them all that had happened, the men were terrified. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 Then \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd* called \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* and asked, “What have you done to us? How have I sinned against you, that you have brought such tremendous guilt upon me and my kingdom? You have done things to me that should not be done.” 
\v 10 \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd* also asked \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*, “What prompted you to do such a thing?” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* replied, “I thought to myself, ‘Surely there is no fear of God in this place. They will kill me on account of \zwd my wife|Q194808\zwd*.’ 
\v 12 Besides, she really is my sister, the daughter of \zwd my father|Q586541\zwd*—though not the daughter of \zwd my mother|\zwd*—and she became my wife. 
\v 13 So when God had me journey from \zwd my father|Q586541\zwd*’s house, I said to \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd*, ‘This is how you can show your loyalty to me: Wherever we go, say of me, “He is my brother.”’” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 So \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd* brought sheep and cattle, menservants and maidservants, and he gave them to \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* and restored \zwd his wife Sarah|Q194808\zwd* to him. 
\v 15 And \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd* said, “Look, my land is before you. Settle wherever you please.” 
\v 16 And he said to \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd*, “See, I am giving \zwd your brother|Q9181\zwd* a thousand pieces of silver.\f + \fr 20:16 \ft Or a thousand shekels of silver; that is, approximately 25.1 pounds or 11.4 kilograms of silver\f* It is your vindication before all who are with you; you are completely cleared.” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 Then \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* prayed to God, and God healed \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd* and \zwd his wife|\zwd* and his maidservants, so that they could again bear children—
\v 18 for on account of \zwd Abraham’s wife Sarah|Q194808\zwd*, the LORD had completely closed all the wombs in \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd*’s household. 
\c 21
\s1 The Birth of Isaac
\m 
\v 1 \zdate|Q671872:P569\*Now the LORD attended to \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd* as He had said, and the LORD did for \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd* what He had promised. 
\v 2 So \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd* conceived and bore a son to \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* in his old age, at the very time God had promised. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 And \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* gave the name \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* \f + \fr 21:3 \ft Isaac means he laughs.\f* to the son \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd* bore to him. 
\v 4 When \zwd his son Isaac|Q671872\zwd* was eight days old, \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* circumcised him, as God had commanded him. 
\v 5 \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* was a hundred years old when \zwd his son Isaac|Q671872\zwd* was born to him. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 Then \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd* said, “God has made me laugh, and everyone who hears of this will laugh with me.” 
\v 7 She added, “Who would have told \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* that \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd* would nurse children? Yet I have borne him a son in his old age.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 So the child grew and was weaned, and \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* held a great feast on the day \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* was weaned. 
\s1 Sarah Turns against Hagar
\r (Galatians 4:21–30)
\b
\m 
\v 9 But \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd* saw that the son whom \zwd Hagar the Egyptian|Q214617\zwd* had borne to \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd* was mocking \zwd her son|Q671872\zwd*,\f + \fr 21:9 \ft LXX and Vulgate; Hebrew lacks her son\f* 
\v 10 and she said to \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*, “Expel \zwd the slave woman|Q214617\zwd* and \zwd her son|Q183403\zwd*, for \zwd \zwd the slave woman|Q214617\zwd*’s son|Q183403\zwd* will never share in the inheritance with \zwd my son Isaac|Q671872\zwd*!”\f + \fr 21:10 \ft Cited in Galatians 4:30\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 11 Now this matter distressed \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* greatly because it concerned \zwd his son Ishmael|Q183403\zwd*. 
\v 12 But God said to \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*, “Do not be distressed about \zwd the boy|Q183403\zwd* and \zwd your maidservant|Q214617\zwd*. Listen to everything that \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd* tells you, for through \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* your offspring will be reckoned.\f + \fr 21:12 \ft Cited in Romans 9:7 and Hebrews 11:18\f* 
\v 13 But I will also make a nation of \zwd \zwd the slave woman|Q214617\zwd*’s son|Q183403\zwd*, because he is your offspring.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Early in the morning, \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* got up, took bread and a skin of water, put them on \zwd Hagar|Q214617\zwd*’s shoulders, and sent her away with \zwd the boy|Q183403\zwd*. She left and wandered in the Wilderness of Beersheba. 
\v 15 When the water in the skin was gone, she left \zwd the boy|Q183403\zwd* under one of the bushes. 
\v 16 Then she went off and sat down nearby, about a bowshot away, for she said, “I cannot bear to watch \zwd the boy|Q183403\zwd* die!” And as she sat nearby, she lifted up her voice and wept.\f + \fr 21:16 \ft Hebrew; LXX the child lifted up his voice and wept\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 17 Then God heard the voice of \zwd the boy|Q183403\zwd*, and the angel of God called to \zwd Hagar|Q214617\zwd* from heaven, “What is wrong, \zwd Hagar|Q214617\zwd*? Do not be afraid, for God has heard the voice of \zwd the boy|Q183403\zwd* where he lies. 
\v 18 Get up, lift up \zwd the boy|Q183403\zwd*, and take him by the hand, for I will make him into a great nation.” 
\v 19 Then God opened her eyes, and she saw a well of water. So she went and filled the skin with water and gave \zwd the boy|Q183403\zwd* a drink. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 And God was with \zwd the boy|Q183403\zwd*, and he grew up and settled in the wilderness and became a great archer. 
\v 21 And while he was dwelling in the Wilderness of Paran, \zwd his mother|Q214617\zwd* got a wife for him from the land of Egypt. 
\s1 The Covenant at Beersheba
\b
\m 
\v 22 At that time \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd* and \zwd Phicol the commander of his army|Q10282102\zwd* said to \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*, “God is with you in all that you do. 
\v 23 Now, therefore, swear to me here before God that you will not deal falsely with me or my children or descendants. Show to me and to the country in which you reside the same kindness that I have shown to you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 24 And \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* replied, “I swear it.” 
\b
\m 
\v 25 But when \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* complained to \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd* about a well that \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd*’s servants had seized, 
\v 26 \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd* replied, “I do not know who has done this. You did not tell me, so I have not heard about it until today.” 
\b
\m 
\v 27 So \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* brought sheep and cattle and gave them to \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd*, and \zwd the two men|Q9181,Q1343803\zwd* made a covenant. 
\v 28 \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* separated seven ewe lambs from the flock, 
\v 29 and \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd* asked him, “Why have you set apart these seven ewe lambs?” 
\b
\m 
\v 30 He replied, “You are to accept the seven ewe lambs from my hand as my witness that I dug this well.” 
\v 31 So that place was called Beersheba,\f + \fr 21:31 \ft Beersheba means well of seven or well of the oath.\f* because it was there that \zwd the two of them|Q9181,Q1343803\zwd* swore an oath. 
\v 32 After they had made the covenant at Beersheba, \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd* and \zwd Phicol the commander of his army|Q10282102\zwd* got up and returned to the land of the Philistines. 
\b
\m 
\v 33 And \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* planted a tamarisk tree in Beersheba, and there he called upon the name of the LORD, the Eternal God.\f + \fr 21:33 \ft Hebrew El-Olam\f* 
\v 34 And \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* resided in the land of the Philistines for a long time. 
\c 22
\s1 The Offering of Isaac
\r (John 3:1–21)
\m 
\v 1 Some time later God tested \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* and said to him, “\zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*!” 
\b
\m “Here I am,” he answered. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 “Take \zwd your son|Q671872\zwd*,” God said, “\zwd your only son Isaac|Q671872\zwd*, whom you love, and go to the land of Moriah. Offer him there as a burnt offering on one of the mountains, which I will show you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 So \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* got up early the next morning, saddled his donkey, and took along two of his servants and \zwd his son Isaac|Q671872\zwd*. He split the wood for a burnt offering and set out for the place God had designated. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 On the third day \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* looked up and saw the place in the distance. 
\v 5 “Stay here with the donkey,” \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* told his servants. “\zwd The boy|Q671872\zwd* and I will go over there to worship, and then we will return to you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* took the wood for the burnt offering and placed it on \zwd his son Isaac|Q671872\zwd*. He himself carried the fire and the sacrificial knife, and \zwd the two of them|Q9181,Q671872\zwd* walked on together. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Then \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* said to \zwd his father Abraham|Q9181\zwd*, “\zwd My father|Q9181\zwd*!” 
\b
\m “Here I am, \zwd my son|Q671872\zwd*,” he replied. 
\b
\m “The fire and the wood are here,” said \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd*, “but where is the lamb for the burnt offering?” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* answered, “God Himself will provide the lamb for the burnt offering, \zwd my son|Q671872\zwd*.” And \zwd the two|Q9181,Q671872\zwd* walked on together. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 When they arrived at the place God had designated, \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* built the altar there and arranged the wood. He bound \zwd his son Isaac|Q671872\zwd* and placed him on the altar, atop the wood. 
\v 10 Then \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* reached out his hand and took the knife to slaughter \zwd his son|Q671872\zwd*. 
\s1 The LORD Provides the Sacrifice
\b
\m 
\v 11 Just then the angel \f + \fr 22:11 \ft Or Angel; also in verses 12 and 15\f* of the LORD called out to him from heaven, “\zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*, \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*!” 
\b
\m “Here I am,” he replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 “Do not lay a hand on \zwd the boy|Q671872\zwd* or do anything to him,” said the angel, “for now I know that you fear God, since you have not withheld \zwd your only son|Q671872\zwd* from me.\f + \fr 22:12 \ft Or from Me\f*” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 Then \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* looked up and saw behind him a ram \f + \fr 22:13 \ft Most MT manuscripts; other Hebrew manuscripts, SP, LXX, and Syriac saw one ram\f* in a thicket, caught by its horns. So he went and took the ram and offered it as a burnt offering in place of \zwd his son|Q671872\zwd*. 
\v 14 And \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* called that place The LORD Will Provide.\f + \fr 22:14 \ft Hebrew YHWH Yireh\f* So to this day it is said, “On the mountain of the LORD it will be provided.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 And the angel of the LORD called to \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* from heaven a second time, 
\v 16 saying, “By Myself I have sworn, declares the LORD, that because you have done this and have not withheld \zwd your only son|Q671872\zwd*, 
\v 17 I will surely bless you, and I will multiply your descendants \f + \fr 22:17 \ft Cited in Hebrews 6:14\f* like the stars in the sky and the sand on the seashore. Your descendants will possess the gates of their enemies. 
\v 18 And through your offspring all nations of the earth will be blessed,\f + \fr 22:18 \ft Cited in Acts 3:25\f* because you have obeyed My voice.” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* went back to his servants, and they got up and set out together for Beersheba. And \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* settled in Beersheba. 
\s1 The Sons of Nahor
\b
\m 
\v 20 Some time later, \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* was told, “\zwd Milcah|Q1752707\zwd* has also borne sons to \zwd your brother Nahor|Q6323112\zwd*: 
\v 21 \zwd Uz the firstborn|Q47010789\zwd*, \zwd his brother Buz|Q114056663\zwd*, \zwd Kemuel|Q11944206\zwd* (the father of \zwd Aram|\zwd*), 
\v 22 \zwd Chesed|Q114056787\zwd*, \zwd Hazo|Q114056831\zwd*, \zwd Pildash|Q125072190\zwd*, \zwd Jidlaph|Q125188731\zwd*, and \zwd Bethuel|Q1579827\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 And \zwd Bethuel|Q1579827\zwd* became the father of \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd*. \zwd Milcah|Q1752707\zwd* bore \zwd these eight sons|Q47010789,Q114056663,Q11944206,Q114056787,Q114056831,Q125072190,Q125188731,Q1579827\zwd* to \zwd \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*’s brother Nahor|Q6323112\zwd*. 
\v 24 Moreover, \zwd \zwd Nahor|Q6323112\zwd*’s concubine|Q108773808\zwd*, whose name was \zwd Reumah|Q108773808\zwd*, bore \zwd Tebah|Q14923275\zwd*, \zwd Gaham|Q125150030\zwd*, \zwd Tahash|Q125484379\zwd*, and \zwd Maacah|Q125295940\zwd*. 
\c 23
\s1 The Death and Burial of Sarah
\m 
\v 1 \zdate|Q194808:P569+P127Y\*Now \zwd Sarah|Q194808\zwd* lived to be 127 years old. 
\v 2 She died in Kiriath-arba (that is, Hebron) in the land of Canaan, and \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* went out to mourn and to weep for her. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Then \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* got up from beside \zwd his dead wife|Q194808\zwd* and said to the Hittites,\f + \fr 23:3 \ft Or sons of Heth; also in verses 5, 7, 10, 16, 18, and 20\f* 
\v 4 “I am a foreigner and an outsider among you. Give me a burial site among you so that I can bury my dead.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 The Hittites replied to \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*, 
\v 6 “Listen to us, sir. You are God’s chosen one among us. Bury your dead in the finest of our tombs. None of us will withhold his tomb for burying your dead.” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Then \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* rose and bowed down before the people of the land, the Hittites. 
\v 8 “If you are willing for me to bury my dead,” he said to them, “listen to me, and approach \zwd Ephron son of \zwd Zohar|id="Q138714970" t="2"\zwd*|Q6775384\zwd* on my behalf 
\v 9 to sell me the cave of Machpelah that belongs to him; it is at the end of his field. Let him sell it to me in your presence for full price, so that I may have a burial site.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Now \zwd Ephron|Q6775384\zwd* was sitting among the sons of Heth. So in the presence of all the Hittites who had come to the gate of his city, \zwd Ephron the Hittite|Q6775384\zwd* answered \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*, 
\v 11 “No, \zwd my lord|Q9181\zwd*. Listen to me. I give you the field, and I give you the cave that is in it. I give it to you in the presence of my people. Bury your dead.” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 Again \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* bowed down before the people of the land 
\v 13 and said to \zwd Ephron|Q6775384\zwd* in their presence, “If you will please listen to me, I will pay you the price of the field. Accept it from me, so that I may bury my dead there.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 \zwd Ephron|Q6775384\zwd* answered \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*, 
\v 15 “Listen to me, \zwd my lord|Q9181\zwd*. The land is worth four hundred shekels of silver,\f + \fr 23:15 \ft 400 shekels is approximately 10.1 pounds or 4.6 kilograms of silver; also in verse 16.\f* but what is that between you and me? Bury your dead.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* agreed to \zwd Ephron|Q6775384\zwd*’s terms and weighed out for him the price he had named in the hearing of the Hittites: four hundred shekels of silver, according to the standard of the merchants. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 So \zwd Ephron|Q6775384\zwd*’s field at Machpelah near Mamre, the cave that was in it, and all the trees within the boundaries of the field were deeded over 
\v 18 to \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*’s possession in the presence of all the Hittites who had come to the gate of his city. 
\v 19 After this, \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* buried \zwd his wife Sarah|Q194808\zwd* in the cave of the field at Machpelah near Mamre (that is, Hebron) in the land of Canaan. 
\v 20 So the field and its cave were deeded by the Hittites to \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* as a burial site. 
\c 24
\s1 A Wife for Isaac
\m 
\v 1 By now \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* was old and well along in years, and the LORD had blessed him in every way. 
\v 2 So \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* instructed \zwd the chief servant of his household|\zwd*, who managed all he owned, “Place your hand under my thigh, 
\v 3 and I will have you swear by the LORD, the God of heaven and the God of earth, that you will not take a wife for \zwd my son|Q671872\zwd* from the daughters of the Canaanites among whom I am dwelling, 
\v 4 but will go to my country and my kindred to take a wife for \zwd my son Isaac|Q671872\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 \zwd The servant|\zwd* asked him, “What if the woman is unwilling to follow me to this land? Shall I then take \zwd your son|Q671872\zwd* back to the land from which you came?” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* replied, “Make sure that you do not take \zwd my son|Q671872\zwd* back there. 
\v 7 The LORD, the God of heaven, who brought me from \zwd my father|Q586541\zwd*’s house and my native land, who spoke to me and promised me on oath, saying, ‘To your offspring I will give this land’—He will send His angel before you so that you can take a wife for \zwd my son|Q671872\zwd* from there. 
\v 8 And if the woman is unwilling to follow you, then you are released from this oath of mine. Only do not take \zwd my son|Q671872\zwd* back there.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 So \zwd the servant|\zwd* placed his hand under the thigh of \zwd his master Abraham|Q9181\zwd* and swore an oath to him concerning this matter. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Then \zwd the servant|\zwd* took ten of \zwd his master|Q9181\zwd*’s camels and departed with all manner of good things from \zwd his master|Q9181\zwd* in hand. And he set out for \zwd Nahor|id="Q6323112" t="2"\zwd*’s hometown in Aram-naharaim.\f + \fr 24:10 \ft That is, Mesopotamia; Aram-naharaim means Aram of the two rivers, likely the region between the Euphrates and Balih Rivers in northwestern Mesopotamia.\f* 
\v 11 As evening approached, he made the camels kneel down near the well outside the town at the time when the women went out to draw water. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 “O LORD, God of \zwd my master Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*,” he prayed, “please grant me success today, and show kindness to \zwd my master Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 13 Here I am, standing beside the spring, and the daughters of the townspeople are coming out to draw water. 
\v 14 Now may it happen that the girl to whom I say, ‘Please let down your jar that I may drink,’ and who responds, ‘Drink, and I will water your camels as well’—let her be the one You have appointed for \zwd Your servant Isaac|Q671872\zwd*. By this I will know that You have shown kindness to my master.” 
\s1 Rebekah Is Chosen
\b
\m 
\v 15 Before \zwd the servant|\zwd* had finished praying, \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd* came out with her jar on her shoulder. She was the daughter of \zwd Bethuel son of \zwd Milcah|id="Q1752707" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q1579827" t="2"\zwd*, the wife of \zwd \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*’s brother Nahor|id="Q6323112" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 16 Now \zwd the girl|Q40520\zwd* was very beautiful, a virgin who had not had relations with any man. She went down to the spring, filled her jar, and came up again. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 So \zwd the servant|\zwd* ran to meet her and said, “Please let me have a little water from your jar.” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 “Drink, my lord,” she replied, and she quickly lowered her jar to her hands and gave him a drink. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 After she had given him a drink, she said, “I will also draw water for your camels, until they have had enough to drink.” 
\v 20 And she quickly emptied her jar into the trough and ran back to the well to draw water, until she had drawn water for all his camels. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 Meanwhile, \zwd the man|\zwd* watched her silently to see whether or not the LORD had made his journey a success. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 And after the camels had finished drinking, he took out a gold ring weighing a beka,\f + \fr 24:22 \ft A beka is half a shekel, or approximately 0.2 ounces or 5.7 grams.\f* and two gold bracelets for her wrists weighing ten shekels.\f + \fr 24:22 \ft 10 shekels is approximately 4 ounces or 114 grams.\f* 
\v 23 “Whose daughter are you?” he asked. “Please tell me, is there room in \zwd your father|id="Q1579827" t="2"\zwd*’s house for us to spend the night?” 
\b
\m 
\v 24 She replied, “I am the daughter of \zwd Bethuel|id="Q1579827" t="2"\zwd*, the son that \zwd Milcah|id="Q1752707" t="2"\zwd* bore to \zwd Nahor|id="Q6323112" t="2"\zwd*.” 
\v 25 Then she added, “We have plenty of straw and feed, as well as a place for you to spend the night.” 
\b
\m 
\v 26 Then the man bowed down and worshiped the LORD, 
\v 27 saying, “Blessed be the LORD, the God of \zwd my master Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*, who has not withheld His kindness and faithfulness from \zwd my master|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*. As for me, the LORD has led me on the journey to the house of \zwd my master|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*’s relatives.” 
\b
\m 
\v 28 \zwd The girl|Q40520\zwd* ran and told \zwd her mother|\zwd*’s household about these things. 
\v 29 Now \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd* had a brother named \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd*, and he rushed out to \zwd the man|\zwd* at the spring. 
\v 30 As soon as he saw the ring, and the bracelets on \zwd his sister|Q40520\zwd*’s wrists, and heard \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd*’s words, “\zwd The man|\zwd* said this to me,” he went and found \zwd the man|\zwd* standing by the camels near the spring. 
\b
\m 
\v 31 “Come, you who are blessed by the LORD,” said \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd*. “Why are you standing out here? I have prepared the house and a place for the camels.” 
\v 32 So the man came to the house, and the camels were unloaded. Straw and feed were brought to the camels, and water to wash his feet and the feet of his companions. 
\b
\m 
\v 33 Then a meal was set before the man, but he said, “I will not eat until I have told you what I came to say.” 
\b
\m So \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* said, “Please speak.” 
\b
\m 
\v 34 “I am \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*’s servant,” he replied. 
\v 35 “The LORD has greatly blessed \zwd my master|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*, and he has become rich. He has given him sheep and cattle, silver and gold, menservants and maidservants, camels and donkeys. 
\v 36 \zwd \zwd My master|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*’s wife Sarah|Q194808\zwd* has borne him \zwd a son|id="Q671872" t="2"\zwd* in her old age, and \zwd my master|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd* has given him everything he owns. 
\b
\m 
\v 37 \zwd My master|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd* made me swear an oath and said, ‘You shall not take a wife for \zwd my son|id="Q671872" t="2"\zwd* from the daughters of the Canaanites in whose land I dwell, 
\v 38 but you shall go to \zwd my father|\zwd*’s house and to my kindred to take a wife for \zwd my son|id="Q671872" t="2"\zwd*.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 39 Then I asked \zwd my master|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*, ‘What if the woman will not come back with me?’ 
\b
\m 
\v 40 And he told me, ‘The LORD, before whom I have walked, will send His angel with you and make your journey a success, so that you may take a wife for \zwd my son|id="Q671872" t="2"\zwd* from my kindred and from \zwd my father|\zwd*’s house. 
\v 41 And when you go to my kindred, if they refuse to give her to you, then you will be released from my oath.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 42 So when I came to the spring today, I prayed: O LORD, God of \zwd my master Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*, if only You would make my journey a success! 
\v 43 Here I am, standing beside this spring. Now if a maiden comes out to draw water and I say to her, ‘Please let me drink a little water from your jar,’ 
\v 44 and she replies, ‘Drink, and I will draw water for your camels as well,’ may she be the woman the LORD has appointed for \zwd \zwd my master|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*’s son|id="Q671872" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 45 And before I had finished praying in my heart, there was \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd* coming out with her jar on her shoulder, and she went down to the spring and drew water. So I said to her, ‘Please give me a drink.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 46 She quickly lowered her jar from her shoulder and said, ‘Drink, and I will water your camels as well.’ So I drank, and she also watered the camels. 
\b
\m 
\v 47 Then I asked her, ‘Whose daughter are you?’ 
\b
\m She replied, ‘The daughter of \zwd Bethuel son of \zwd Nahor|id="Q6323112" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q1579827" t="2"\zwd*, whom \zwd Milcah|id="Q1752707" t="2"\zwd* bore to him.’ So I put the ring on her nose and the bracelets on her wrists. 
\v 48 Then I bowed down and worshiped the LORD; and I blessed the LORD, the God of \zwd my master Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*, who led me on the right road to take \zwd the granddaughter of \zwd \zwd my master|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*’s brother|id="Q6323112" t="2"\zwd*|Q40520\zwd* for \zwd his son|id="Q671872" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 49 Now if you will show kindness and faithfulness to \zwd my master|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*, tell me; but if not, let me know, so that I may go elsewhere.” 
\b
\m 
\v 50 \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* and \zwd Bethuel|Q1579827\zwd* answered, “This is from the LORD; we have no choice in the matter. 
\v 51 \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd* is here before you. Take her and go, and let her become the wife of \zwd \zwd your master|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*’s son|Q671872\zwd*, just as the LORD has decreed.” 
\b
\m 
\v 52 When \zwd \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*’s servant|\zwd* heard their words, he bowed down to the ground before the LORD. 
\v 53 Then he brought out jewels of silver and gold, and articles of clothing, and he gave them to \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd*. He also gave precious gifts to \zwd her brother|\zwd* and \zwd her mother|\zwd*. 
\v 54 Then he and the men with him ate and drank and spent the night there. 
\b
\m When they got up the next morning, he said, “Send me on my way to \zwd my master|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 55 But \zwd her brother|\zwd* and \zwd mother|\zwd* said, “Let \zwd the girl|Q40520\zwd* remain with us ten days or so. After that, she may go.” 
\b
\m 
\v 56 But he replied, “Do not delay me, since the LORD has made my journey a success. Send me on my way so that I may go to \zwd my master|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 57 So they said, “We will call \zwd the girl|Q40520\zwd* and ask her opinion.” 
\b
\m 
\v 58 They called \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd* and asked her, “Will you go with \zwd this man|\zwd*?” 
\b
\m “I will go,” she replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 59 So they sent \zwd their sister Rebekah|Q40520\zwd* on her way, along with \zwd her nurse|Q2903219\zwd* and \zwd \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*’s servant|\zwd* and his men. 
\v 60 And they blessed \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd* and said to her, 
\b
\q1 “\zwd Our sister|Q40520\zwd*, may you become the mother 
\q2 of thousands upon thousands. 
\q1 May your offspring possess 
\q2 the gates of their enemies.” 
\b
\m 
\v 61 Then \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd* and her servant girls got ready, mounted the camels, and followed \zwd the man|\zwd*. So \zwd the servant|\zwd* took \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd* and left. 
\s1 Isaac Marries Rebekah
\b
\m 
\v 62 Now \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* had just returned from Beer-lahai-roi, for he was living in the Negev. 
\v 63 Early in the evening, \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* went out to the field to meditate, and looking up, he saw the camels approaching. 
\b
\m 
\v 64 And when \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd* looked up and saw \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd*, she got down from her camel 
\v 65 and asked \zwd the servant|\zwd*, “Who is that man in the field coming to meet us?” 
\b
\m “It is \zwd my master|Q671872\zwd*,” \zwd the servant|\zwd* answered. So she took her veil and covered herself. 
\v 66 Then \zwd the servant|\zwd* told \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* all that he had done. 
\b
\m 
\v 67 And \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* brought her into the tent of \zwd his mother Sarah|id="Q194808" t="2"\zwd* and took \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd* as his wife. And \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* loved her and was comforted after \zwd his mother|id="Q194808" t="2"\zwd*’s death. 
\c 25
\s1 Abraham and Keturah
\r (1 Chronicles 1:32–33)
\m 
\v 1 Now \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* had taken another wife, named \zwd Keturah|Q908531\zwd*, 
\v 2 and she bore him \zwd Zimran|Q786540\zwd*, \zwd Jokshan|Q737548\zwd*, \zwd Medan|Q1212757\zwd*, \zwd Midian|Q755316\zwd*, \zwd Ishbak|Q594903\zwd*, and \zwd Shuah|Q369850\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 \zwd Jokshan|Q737548\zwd* was the father of \zwd Sheba|Q9332549\zwd* and \zwd Dedan|Q124819618\zwd*. And the sons of \zwd Dedan|Q124819618\zwd* were the Asshurites, the Letushites, and the Leummites. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 The sons of \zwd Midian|Q755316\zwd* were \zwd Ephah|Q5382125\zwd*, \zwd Epher|Q5382171\zwd*, \zwd Hanoch|Q108207665\zwd*, \zwd Abida|Q3314892\zwd*, and \zwd Eldaah|Q124325068\zwd*. 
\b
\m All these were descendants of \zwd Keturah|Q908531\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* left everything he owned to \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd*. 
\v 6 But while he was still alive, \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* gave gifts to \zwd the sons of \zwd his concubines|\zwd*|\zwd* and sent them away from \zwd his son Isaac|Q671872\zwd* to the land of the east. 
\s1 The Death of Abraham
\b
\m 
\v 7 \zdate|Q9181:P570\*\zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* lived a total of 175 years. 
\v 8 And at a ripe old age he breathed his last and died, old and contented, and was gathered to his people. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 His sons \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* and \zwd Ishmael|Q183403\zwd* buried him in the cave of Machpelah near Mamre, in the field of \zwd Ephron son of \zwd Zohar|id="Q138714970" t="2"\zwd* the Hittite|Q6775384\zwd*. 
\v 10 This was the field that \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* had bought from the Hittites. \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* was buried there with \zwd his wife Sarah|id="Q194808" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 After \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*’s death, God blessed \zwd his son Isaac|Q671872\zwd*, who lived near Beer-lahai-roi. 
\s1 The Descendants of Ishmael
\r (1 Chronicles 1:28–31)
\b
\m 
\v 12 This is the account of \zwd \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*’s son Ishmael|Q183403\zwd*, whom \zwd Hagar the Egyptian|Q214617\zwd*, \zwd Sarah|id="Q194808" t="2"\zwd*’s maidservant, bore to \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd*. 
\v 13 These are the names of the sons of \zwd Ishmael|Q183403\zwd* in the order of their birth: \zwd Nebaioth the firstborn of \zwd Ishmael|Q183403\zwd*|Q3773487\zwd*, then \zwd Kedar|Q21087985\zwd*, \zwd Adbeel|Q24238724\zwd*, \zwd Mibsam|Q14925213\zwd*, 
\v 14 \zwd Mishma|Q20101216\zwd*, \zwd Dumah|Q5313606\zwd*, \zwd Massa|Q21087988\zwd*, 
\v 15 \zwd Hadad|Q22004334\zwd*, \zwd Tema|Q20101919\zwd*, \zwd Jetur|Q11927540\zwd*, \zwd Naphish|Q10335361\zwd*, and \zwd Kedemah|Q11944201\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 These were the sons of \zwd Ishmael|Q183403\zwd*, and these were their names by their villages and encampments—twelve princes of their tribes. 
\v 17 \zwd Ishmael|Q183403\zwd* lived a total of 137 years. Then he breathed his last and died, and was gathered to his people. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 \zwd Ishmael|Q183403\zwd*’s descendants settled from Havilah to Shur, which is near the border of Egypt as you go toward Asshur.\f + \fr 25:18 \ft Or Assyria\f* And they lived in hostility toward all their brothers. 
\s1 Jacob and Esau
\r (Malachi 1:1–5; Romans 9:6–29)
\b
\m 
\v 19 This is the account of \zwd \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*’s son Isaac|Q671872\zwd*. \zwd Abraham|Q9181\zwd* became the father of \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd*, 
\v 20 \zdate|Q671872:P569+P40Y\*and \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* was forty years old when he married \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd*, the daughter of \zwd Bethuel the Aramean|id="Q1579827" t="2"\zwd* from Paddan-aram \f + \fr 25:20 \ft That is, northwest Mesopotamia\f* and the sister of \zwd Laban the Aramean|Q840401\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 Later, \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* prayed to the LORD on behalf of \zwd his wife|Q40520\zwd*, because she was barren. And the LORD heard his prayer, and \zwd his wife Rebekah|Q40520\zwd* conceived. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 But \zwd the children|Q289957,Q220822\zwd* inside her struggled with each other, and she said, “Why is this happening to me?” So \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd* went to inquire of the LORD, 
\v 23 and He declared to her: 
\b
\q1 “Two nations are in your womb, 
\q2 and two peoples from within you will be separated; 
\q1 one people will be stronger than the other, 
\q2 and \zwd the older|Q220822\zwd* will serve \zwd the younger|Q289957\zwd*.”\f + \fr 25:23 \ft Cited in Romans 9:12\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 24 \zdate|Q289957:P569\*When her time came to give birth, there were indeed twins in her womb. 
\v 25 The first one came out red, covered with hair like a fur coat; so they named him \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*.\f + \fr 25:25 \ft Esau sounds like a Hebrew term that means hairy.\f* 
\v 26 After this, his brother came out grasping \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*’s heel; so he was named \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*.\f + \fr 25:26 \ft Jacob means he grasps the heel or he deceives.\f* And \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* was sixty years old when the twins were born. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 When \zwd the boys|Q220822,Q289957\zwd* grew up, \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* became a skillful hunter, a man of the field, while \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* was a quiet man who stayed at home. 
\v 28 Because \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* had a taste for wild game, he loved \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*; but \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd* loved \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*. 
\s1 Esau Sells His Birthright
\b
\m 
\v 29 One day, while \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* was cooking some stew, \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* came in from the field and was famished. 
\v 30 He said to \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, “Let me eat some of that red stew, for I am famished.” (That is why he was also called Edom.\f + \fr 25:30 \ft Edom means red.\f*) 
\b
\m 
\v 31 “First sell me your birthright,” \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 32 “Look,” said \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*, “I am about to die, so what good is a birthright to me?” 
\b
\m 
\v 33 “Swear to me first,” \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* said. 
\b
\m So \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* swore to \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* and sold him the birthright. 
\v 34 Then \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* gave some bread and lentil stew to \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*, who ate and drank and then got up and went away. Thus \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* despised his birthright. 
\c 26
\s1 God’s Promise to Isaac
\r (Genesis 12:1–9)
\m 
\v 1 Now there was another famine in the land, subsequent to the one that had occurred in \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*’s time. And \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* went to \zwd Abimelech king of the Philistines|Q1343803\zwd* at Gerar. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 The LORD appeared to \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* and said, “Do not go down to Egypt. Settle in the land where I tell you. 
\v 3 Stay in this land as a foreigner, and I will be with you and bless you. For I will give all these lands to you and your offspring, and I will confirm the oath that I swore to your father \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 4 I will make your descendants as numerous as the stars in the sky, and I will give them all these lands, and through your offspring all nations of the earth will be blessed, 
\v 5 because \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd* listened to My voice and kept My charge, My commandments, My statutes, and My laws.” 
\s1 Isaac Deceives Abimelech
\b
\m 
\v 6 So \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* settled in Gerar. 
\v 7 But when the men of that place asked about \zwd his wife|Q40520\zwd*, he said, “She is my sister.” For he was afraid to say, “She is my wife,” since he thought to himself, “The men of this place will kill me on account of \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd*, because she is so beautiful.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 When \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* had been there a long time, \zwd Abimelech king of the Philistines|Q1343803\zwd* looked down from the window and was surprised to see \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* caressing \zwd his wife Rebekah|Q40520\zwd*. 
\v 9 \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd* sent for \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* and said, “So she is really your wife! How could you say, ‘She is my sister’?” 
\b
\m \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* replied, “Because I thought I might die on account of her.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 “What is this you have done to us?” asked \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd*. “One of the people could easily have slept with \zwd your wife|Q40520\zwd*, and you would have brought guilt upon us.” 
\v 11 So \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd* warned all the people, saying, “Whoever harms \zwd this man|Q671872\zwd* or \zwd his wife|Q40520\zwd* will surely be put to death.” 
\s1 Isaac’s Prosperity
\b
\m 
\v 12 Now \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* sowed seed in the land, and that very year he reaped a hundredfold. And the LORD blessed him, 
\v 13 and he became richer and richer, until he was exceedingly wealthy. 
\v 14 He owned so many flocks and herds and servants that the Philistines envied him. 
\v 15 So the Philistines took dirt and stopped up all the wells that \zwd his father|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*’s servants had dug in the days of his father \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 Then \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd* said to \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd*, “Depart from us, for you are much too powerful for us.” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 So \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* left that place and encamped in the Valley of Gerar and settled there. 
\v 18 \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* reopened the wells that had been dug in the days of \zwd his father Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*, which the Philistines had stopped up after \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd* died. And he gave these wells the same names \zwd his father|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd* had given them. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 Then \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd*’s servants dug in the valley and found a well of fresh water \f + \fr 26:19 \ft Or flowing water or living water\f* there. 
\v 20 But the herdsmen of Gerar quarreled with \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd*’s herdsmen and said, “The water is ours!” So he named the well Esek,\f + \fr 26:20 \ft Esek means contention.\f* because they contended with him. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 Then they dug another well and quarreled over that one also; so he named it Sitnah.\f + \fr 26:21 \ft Sitnah means enmity or hostility.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 22 He moved on from there and dug another well, and they did not quarrel over it. He named it Rehoboth \f + \fr 26:22 \ft Rehoboth means broad places or open spaces.\f* and said, “At last the LORD has made room for us, and we will be fruitful in the land.” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 From there \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* went up to Beersheba, 
\v 24 and that night the LORD appeared to him and said, “I am the God of \zwd your father Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*. Do not be afraid, for I am with you. I will bless you and multiply your descendants for the sake of \zwd My servant Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 25 So \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* built an altar there and called on the name of the LORD, and he pitched his tent there. His servants also dug a well there. 
\s1 Isaac’s Covenant with Abimelech
\b
\m 
\v 26 Later, \zwd Abimelech|Q1343803\zwd* came to \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* from Gerar, with \zwd Ahuzzath his adviser|Q125345108\zwd* and \zwd Phicol the commander of his army|Q10282102\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 “Why have you come to me?” \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* asked them. “You hated me and sent me away.” 
\b
\m 
\v 28 “We can plainly see that the LORD has been with you,” they replied. “We recommend that there should now be an oath between us and you. Let us make a covenant with you 
\v 29 that you will not harm us, just as we have not harmed you but have done only good to you, sending you on your way in peace. And now you are blessed by the LORD.” 
\b
\m 
\v 30 So \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* prepared a feast for them, and they ate and drank. 
\v 31 And they got up early the next morning and swore an oath to each other. Then \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* sent them on their way, and they left him in peace. 
\b
\m 
\v 32 On that same day, \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd*’s servants came and told him about the well they had dug. “We have found water!” they told him. 
\v 33 So he called it Shibah,\f + \fr 26:33 \ft Shibah can mean oath or seven.\f* and to this day the name of the city is Beersheba.\f + \fr 26:33 \ft Beersheba means well of seven or well of the oath.\f* 
\s1 Esau’s Wives
\b
\m 
\v 34 \zdate|Q220822:P569+P40Y\*When \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* was forty years old, he took as his wives \zwd Judith daughter of \zwd Beeri the Hittite|id="Q124751781" t="2"\zwd*|Q3187975\zwd* and \zwd Basemath daughter of \zwd Elon the Hittite|id="Q65252558" t="2"\zwd*|Q3320047\zwd*. 
\v 35 And they brought grief to \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* and \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd*. 
\c 27
\s1 Isaac Blesses Jacob
\r (Hebrews 11:20)
\m 
\v 1 When \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* was old and his eyes were so weak that he could no longer see, he called \zwd his older son Esau|Q220822\zwd* and said to him, “My son.” 
\b
\m “Here I am,” \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 “Look,” said \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd*, “I am now old, and I do not know the day of my death. 
\v 3 Take your weapons—your quiver and bow—and go out into the field to hunt some game for me. 
\v 4 Then prepare a tasty dish that I love and bring it to me to eat, so that I may bless you before I die.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Now \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd* was listening to what \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* told \zwd his son Esau|Q220822\zwd*. So when \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* went into the field to hunt game and bring it back, 
\v 6 \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd* said to \zwd her son Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, “Behold, I overheard \zwd your father|Q671872\zwd* saying to \zwd your brother Esau|Q220822\zwd*, 
\v 7 ‘Bring me some game and prepare me a tasty dish to eat, so that I may bless you in the presence of the LORD before I die.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 8 Now, \zwd my son|Q289957\zwd*, listen to my voice and do exactly as I tell you. 
\v 9 Go out to the flock and bring me two choice young goats, so that I can make them into a tasty dish for \zwd your father|Q671872\zwd*—the kind he loves. 
\v 10 Then take it to \zwd your father|Q671872\zwd* to eat, so that he may bless you before he dies.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* answered \zwd his mother Rebekah|Q40520\zwd*, “Look, \zwd my brother Esau|Q220822\zwd* is a hairy man, but I am smooth-skinned. 
\v 12 What if \zwd my father|Q671872\zwd* touches me? Then I would be revealed to him as a deceiver, and I would bring upon myself a curse rather than a blessing.” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 \zwd His mother|Q40520\zwd* replied, “Your curse be on me, \zwd my son|Q289957\zwd*. Just obey my voice and go get them for me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 So \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* went and got two goats and brought them to \zwd his mother|Q40520\zwd*, who made the tasty food \zwd his father|Q671872\zwd* loved. 
\v 15 And \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd* took the finest clothes in the house that belonged to \zwd her older son Esau|Q220822\zwd*, and she put them on \zwd her younger son Jacob|Q289957\zwd*. 
\v 16 She also put the skins of the young goats on his hands and on the smooth part of his neck. 
\v 17 Then she handed \zwd her son Jacob|Q289957\zwd* the tasty food and bread she had made. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 So \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* went to \zwd his father|Q671872\zwd* and said, “My father.” 
\b
\m “Here I am!” he answered. “Which one are you, my son?” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* said to \zwd his father|Q671872\zwd*, “I am \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*, your firstborn. I have done as you told me. Please sit up and eat some of my game, so that you may bless me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 But \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* asked his son, “How did you ever find it so quickly, my son?” 
\b
\m “Because the LORD your God brought it to me,” he replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 Then \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* said to \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, “Please come closer so I can touch you, my son. Are you really \zwd my son Esau|Q220822\zwd*, or not?” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 So \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* came close to \zwd his father Isaac|Q671872\zwd*, who touched him and said, “The voice is the voice of \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, but the hands are the hands of \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*.” 
\v 23 \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* did not recognize him, because his hands were hairy like those of \zwd his brother Esau|Q220822\zwd*; so he blessed him. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 Again he asked, “Are you really \zwd my son Esau|Q220822\zwd*?” 
\b
\m And he replied, “I am.” 
\b
\m 
\v 25 “Serve me,” said \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd*, “and let me eat some of my son’s game, so that I may bless you.” 
\b
\m \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* brought it to him, and he ate; then he brought him wine, and he drank. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 Then \zwd his father Isaac|Q671872\zwd* said to him, “Please come near and kiss me, my son.” 
\b
\m 
\v 27 So he came near and kissed him. When \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* smelled his clothing, he blessed him and said: 
\b
\q1 “Ah, the smell of my son 
\q2 is like the smell of a field 
\q2 that the LORD has blessed. 
\q1 
\v 28 May God give to you the dew of heaven 
\q2 and the richness of the earth—
\q2 an abundance of grain and new wine. 
\q1 
\v 29 May peoples serve you 
\q2 and nations bow down to you. 
\q1 May you be the master of your brothers, 
\q2 and may the sons of \zwd your mother|id="Q40520" t="2"\zwd* bow down to you. 
\q1 May those who curse you be cursed, 
\q2 and those who bless you be blessed.” 
\s1 Esau’s Lost Hope
\b
\m 
\v 30 As soon as \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* had finished blessing him and \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* had left \zwd his father|Q671872\zwd*’s presence, \zwd his brother Esau|Q220822\zwd* returned from the hunt. 
\v 31 He too made some tasty food, brought it to \zwd his father|Q671872\zwd*, and said to him, “\zwd My father|Q671872\zwd*, sit up and eat of \zwd your son|Q220822\zwd*’s game, so that you may bless me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 32 But \zwd his father Isaac|Q671872\zwd* replied, “Who are you?” 
\b
\m “I am \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*, your firstborn son,” he answered. 
\b
\m 
\v 33 \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* began to tremble violently and said, “Who was it, then, who hunted the game and brought it to me? Before you came in, I ate it all and blessed him—and indeed, he will be blessed!” 
\b
\m 
\v 34 When \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* heard \zwd his father|Q671872\zwd*’s words, he let out a loud and bitter cry and said to \zwd his father|Q671872\zwd*, “Bless me too, O \zwd my father|Q671872\zwd*!” 
\b
\m 
\v 35 But \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* replied, “\zwd Your brother|Q289957\zwd* came deceitfully and took your blessing.” 
\b
\m 
\v 36 So \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* declared, “Is he not rightly named \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*?\f + \fr 27:36 \ft Jacob means he grasps the heel or he deceives.\f* For he has cheated me twice. He took my birthright, and now he has taken my blessing.” Then he asked, “Haven’t you saved a blessing for me?” 
\b
\m 
\v 37 But \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* answered \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*: “Look, I have made him your master and given him all his relatives as servants; I have sustained him with grain and new wine. What is left that I can do for you, \zwd my son|Q220822\zwd*?” 
\b
\m 
\v 38 \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* said to his father, “Do you have only one blessing, \zwd my father|Q671872\zwd*? Bless me too, O \zwd my father|Q671872\zwd*!” Then \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* wept aloud. 
\b
\m 
\v 39 \zwd His father Isaac|Q671872\zwd* answered him: 
\b
\q1 “Behold, your dwelling place shall be 
\q2 away from the richness of the land, 
\q2 away from the dew of heaven above. 
\q1 
\v 40 You shall live by the sword 
\q2 and serve \zwd your brother|Q289957\zwd*. 
\q1 But when you rebel, 
\q2 you will tear his yoke from your neck.” 
\b
\m 
\v 41 \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* held a grudge against \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* because of the blessing \zwd his father|Q671872\zwd* had given him. And \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* said in his heart, “The days of mourning for \zwd my father|Q671872\zwd* are at hand; then I will kill \zwd my brother Jacob|Q289957\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 42 When the words of \zwd her older son Esau|Q220822\zwd* were relayed to \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd*, she sent for \zwd her younger son Jacob|Q289957\zwd* and told him, “Look, \zwd your brother Esau|Q220822\zwd* is consoling himself by plotting to kill you. 
\v 43 So now, \zwd my son|Q289957\zwd*, obey my voice and flee at once to \zwd my brother Laban|Q840401\zwd* in Haran. 
\v 44 Stay with him for a while, until \zwd your brother|Q220822\zwd*’s fury subsides—
\v 45 until \zwd your brother|Q220822\zwd*’s rage against you wanes and he forgets what you have done to him. Then I will send for you and bring you back from there. Why should I lose both of you in one day?” 
\b
\m 
\v 46 Then \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd* said to \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd*, “I am weary of my life because of these Hittite women.\f + \fr 27:46 \ft Or daughters of Heth\f* If \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* takes a Hittite wife from among them, what good is my life?” 
\c 28
\s1 Jacob’s Departure
\m 
\v 1 So \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* called for \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* and blessed him. “Do not take a wife from the Canaanite women,” he commanded. 
\v 2 “Go at once to Paddan-aram,\f + \fr 28:2 \ft That is, northwest Mesopotamia; also in verses 5, 6, and 7\f* to the house of \zwd \zwd your mother|Q40520\zwd*’s father Bethuel|id="Q1579827" t="2"\zwd*, and take a wife from among the daughters of \zwd Laban|id="Q840401" t="2"\zwd*, \zwd your mother|Q40520\zwd*’s brother. 
\v 3 May God Almighty \f + \fr 28:3 \ft Hebrew El-Shaddai\f* bless you and make you fruitful and multiply you, so that you may become a company of peoples. 
\v 4 And may He give the blessing of \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd* to you and your descendants, so that you may possess the land where you dwell as a foreigner, the land God gave to \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 So \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* sent \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* to Paddan-aram, to \zwd \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* son of Bethuel the Aramean|Q1579827\zwd*, the brother of \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd*, who was the mother of \zwd Jacob|id="Q289957" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd Esau|id="Q220822" t="2"\zwd*. 
\s1 Esau Marries Mahalath
\b
\m 
\v 6 Now \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* learned that \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* had blessed \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* and sent him to Paddan-aram to take a wife there, commanding him, “Do not marry a Canaanite woman,” 
\v 7 and that \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* had obeyed \zwd his father|Q671872\zwd* and \zwd mother|Q40520\zwd* and gone to Paddan-aram. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 And seeing that \zwd his father Isaac|Q671872\zwd* disapproved of the Canaanite women, 
\v 9 \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* went to \zwd Ishmael|Q183403\zwd* and married \zwd Mahalath|Q2669401\zwd*, the sister of \zwd Nebaioth|Q3773487\zwd* and daughter of \zwd \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*’s son Ishmael|Q183403\zwd*, in addition to the wives he already had. 
\s1 Jacob’s Ladder
\b
\m 
\v 10 Meanwhile \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* left Beersheba and set out for Haran. 
\v 11 On reaching a certain place, he spent the night there because the sun had set. And taking one of the stones from that place, he put it under his head and lay down to sleep. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 And \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* had a dream about a ladder that rested on the earth with its top reaching up to heaven, and God’s angels were going up and down the ladder. 
\v 13 And there at the top \f + \fr 28:13 \ft Or there beside him\f* the LORD was standing and saying, “I am the LORD, the God of \zwd your father Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd* and the God of \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd*. I will give you and your descendants the land on which you now lie. 
\v 14 Your descendants will be like the dust of the earth, and you will spread out to the west and east and north and south. All the families of the earth will be blessed through you and your offspring. 
\v 15 Look, I am with you, and I will watch over you wherever you go, and I will bring you back to this land. For I will not leave you until I have done what I have promised you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 When \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* woke up, he said, “Surely the LORD is in this place, and I was unaware of it.” 
\v 17 And he was afraid and said, “How awesome is this place! This is none other than the house of God; this is the gate of heaven!” 
\s1 The Stone of Bethel
\b
\m 
\v 18 Early the next morning, \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* took the stone that he had placed under his head, and he set it up as a pillar. He poured oil on top of it, 
\v 19 and he called that place Bethel,\f + \fr 28:19 \ft Bethel means house of God.\f* though previously the city had been named Luz. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 Then \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* made a vow, saying, “If God will be with me and watch over me on this journey, and if He will provide me with food to eat and clothes to wear, 
\v 21 so that I may return safely to \zwd my father|id="Q671872" t="2"\zwd*’s house, then the LORD will be my God. 
\v 22 And this stone I have set up as a pillar will be God’s house, and of all that You give me I will surely give You a tenth.” 
\c 29
\s1 Jacob Meets Rachel
\m 
\v 1 \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* resumed his journey and came to the land of the people of the east. 
\v 2 He looked and saw a well in the field, and near it lay three flocks of sheep, because the sheep were watered from this well. And a large stone covered the mouth of the well. 
\v 3 When all the flocks had been gathered there, the shepherds would roll away the stone from the mouth of the well and water the sheep. Then they would return the stone to its place over the mouth of the well. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 “My brothers,” \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* asked the shepherds, “where are you from?” 
\b
\m “We are from Haran,” they answered. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 “Do you know \zwd Laban the grandson of \zwd Nahor|id="Q6323112" t="2"\zwd*|Q840401\zwd*?” \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* asked. 
\b
\m “We know him,” they replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 “Is he well?” \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* inquired. 
\b
\m “Yes,” they answered, “and here comes \zwd his daughter Rachel|Q207389\zwd* with his sheep.” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 “Look,” said \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, “it is still broad daylight; it is not yet time to gather the livestock. Water the sheep and take them back to pasture.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 But they replied, “We cannot, until all the flocks have been gathered and the stone has been rolled away from the mouth of the well. Then we will water the sheep.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 While he was still speaking with them, \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* arrived with \zwd her father|id="Q840401" t="2"\zwd*’s sheep, for she was a shepherdess. 
\v 10 As soon as \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* saw \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd*, the daughter of his \zwd mother’s brother Laban|id="Q840401" t="2"\zwd*, with \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd*’s sheep, he went up and rolled the stone away from the mouth of the well and watered \zwd his uncle|Q840401\zwd*’s sheep. 
\v 11 Then \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* kissed \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* and wept aloud. 
\v 12 He told \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* that he was \zwd Rebekah|id="Q40520" t="2"\zwd*’s son, a relative of \zwd her father|Q840401\zwd*, and she ran and told \zwd her father|Q840401\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 When \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* heard the news about \zwd \zwd his sister|id="Q40520" t="2"\zwd*’s son Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, he ran out to meet him. He embraced him and kissed him and brought him to his home, where \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* told him all that had happened. 
\s1 Jacob Marries Leah and Rachel
\b
\m 
\v 14 Then \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* declared, “You are indeed my own flesh and blood.” 
\b
\m After \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* had stayed with him a month, 
\v 15 \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* said to him, “Just because you are my relative, should you work for nothing? Tell me what your wages should be.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 Now \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* had two daughters; the older was named \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd*, and the younger was named \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd*. 
\v 17 \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd* had weak eyes,\f + \fr 29:17 \ft Or had delicate eyes\f* but \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* was shapely and beautiful. 
\v 18 Since \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* loved \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd*, he answered, “I will serve you seven years for \zwd your younger daughter Rachel|Q207389\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* replied, “Better that I give her to you than to another. Stay here with me.” 
\v 20 So \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* served seven years for \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd*, yet it seemed but a few days because of his love for her. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 Finally \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* said to \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd*, “Grant me my wife, for my time is complete, and I want to sleep with her.” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 So \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* invited all the men of that place and prepared a feast. 
\v 23 But when evening came, \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* took \zwd his daughter Leah|Q128847\zwd* and gave her to \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, and he slept with her. 
\v 24 And \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* gave \zwd his servant girl Zilpah|Q221826\zwd* to \zwd his daughter Leah|Q128847\zwd* as her maidservant. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 When morning came, there was \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd*! “What have you done to me?” \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* said to \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd*. “Wasn’t it for \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* that I served you? Why have you deceived me?” 
\b
\m 
\v 26 \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* replied, “It is not our custom here to give the younger daughter in marriage before the older. 
\v 27 Finish this week’s celebration, and we will give you \zwd the younger one|Q207389\zwd* in return for another seven years of work.” 
\b
\m 
\v 28 And \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* did just that. He finished the week’s celebration, and \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* gave him \zwd his daughter Rachel|Q207389\zwd* as his wife. 
\v 29 \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* also gave \zwd his servant girl Bilhah|Q794991\zwd* to \zwd his daughter Rachel|Q207389\zwd* as her maidservant. 
\b
\m 
\v 30 \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* slept with \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* as well, and indeed, he loved \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* more than \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd*. So he worked for \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* another seven years. 
\s1 Reuben, Simeon, Levi, and Judah
\b
\m 
\v 31 When the LORD saw that \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd* was unloved, He opened her womb; but \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* was barren. 
\v 32 \zdate|Q625661:P569\*And \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd* conceived and gave birth to a son, and she named him \zwd Reuben|Q625661\zwd*,\f + \fr 29:32 \ft Reuben means Look, a son and also sounds like the Hebrew for He has seen my misery.\f* for she said, “The LORD has seen my affliction. Surely my husband will love me now.” 
\b
\m 
\v 33 \zdate|Q1773005:P569\*Again she conceived and gave birth to a son, and she said, “Because the LORD has heard that I am unloved, He has given me this son as well.” So she named him \zwd Simeon|Q1773005\zwd*.\f + \fr 29:33 \ft Simeon probably means one who hears.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 34 \zdate|Q215512:P569\*Once again \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd* conceived and gave birth to a son, and she said, “Now at last my husband will become attached to me, because I have borne him three sons.” So he was named \zwd Levi|Q215512\zwd*.\f + \fr 29:34 \ft Levi sounds like the Hebrew for being attached to or feeling affection for.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 35 \zdate|Q282220:P569\*And once more she conceived and gave birth to a son and said, “This time I will praise the LORD.” So she named him \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd*.\f + \fr 29:35 \ft Judah sounds like the Hebrew for praise.\f* Then \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd* stopped having children. 
\c 30
\s1 Dan and Naphtali
\m 
\v 1 When \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* saw that she was not bearing any children for \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, she envied her sister. “Give me children, or I will die!” she said to \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* became angry with \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* and said, “Am I in the place of God, who has withheld children from you?” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Then she said, “Here is \zwd my maidservant Bilhah|Q794991\zwd*. Sleep with her, that she may bear children for me,\f + \fr 30:3 \ft Literally bear children on my knees\f* so that through her I too can build a family.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 So \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* gave \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* \zwd her servant Bilhah|Q794991\zwd* as a wife, and he slept with her, 
\v 5 \zdate|Q550869:P569\*and \zwd Bilhah|Q794991\zwd* conceived and bore him a son. 
\v 6 Then \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* said, “God has vindicated me; He has heard my plea and given me a son.” So she named him \zwd Dan|Q550869\zwd*.\f + \fr 30:6 \ft Dan means He has judged or He has vindicated.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 7 \zdate|Q1771963:P569\*And \zwd \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd*’s servant Bilhah|Q794991\zwd* conceived again and bore \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* a second son. 
\v 8 Then \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* said, “In my great struggles, I have wrestled with my sister and won.” So she named him \zwd Naphtali|Q1771963\zwd*.\f + \fr 30:8 \ft Naphtali sounds like the Hebrew for wrestling.\f* 
\s1 Gad and Asher
\b
\m 
\v 9 When \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd* saw that she had stopped having children, she gave \zwd her servant Zilpah|Q221826\zwd* to \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* as a wife. 
\v 10 \zdate|Q851749:P569\*And \zwd \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd*’s servant Zilpah|Q221826\zwd* bore \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* a son. 
\v 11 Then \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd* said, “How fortunate!”\f + \fr 30:11 \ft Alternate MT reading (see also LXX); the other alternate reads “A troop is coming!”\f* So she named him \zwd Gad|Q851749\zwd*.\f + \fr 30:11 \ft Gad sounds like the Hebrew for good fortune, or alternately for band of raiders.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 12 \zdate|Q1775026:P569\*When \zwd \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd*’s servant Zilpah|Q221826\zwd* bore \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* a second son, 
\v 13 \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd* said, “How happy I am! For the women call me happy.” So she named him \zwd Asher|Q1775026\zwd*.\f + \fr 30:13 \ft Asher means happy.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Now during the wheat harvest, \zwd Reuben|Q625661\zwd* went out and found some mandrakes in the field. When he brought them to \zwd his mother|Q128847\zwd*, \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* begged \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd*, “Please give me some of \zwd your son|Q625661\zwd*’s mandrakes.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 But \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd* replied, “Is it not enough that you have taken away \zwd my husband|Q289957\zwd*? Now you want to take \zwd my son|Q625661\zwd*’s mandrakes as well?” 
\b
\m “Very well,” said \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd*, “he may sleep with you tonight in exchange for \zwd your son|Q625661\zwd*’s mandrakes.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 When \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* came in from the field that evening, \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd* went out to meet him and said, “You must come with me, for I have hired you with \zwd my son|Q625661\zwd*’s mandrakes.” So he slept with her that night. 
\s1 Issachar, Zebulun, and Dinah
\b
\m 
\v 17 \zdate|Q651256:P569\*And God listened to \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd*, and she conceived and bore a fifth son to \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*. 
\v 18 Then \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd* said, “God has rewarded me for giving my maidservant to \zwd my husband|Q289957\zwd*.” So she named him \zwd Issachar|Q651256\zwd*.\f + \fr 30:18 \ft Issachar sounds like the Hebrew for wages or reward.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 19 \zdate|Q614575:P569\*Again \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd* conceived and bore a sixth son to \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*. 
\v 20 “God has given me a good gift,” she said. “This time \zwd my husband|Q289957\zwd* will honor me, because I have borne him six sons.” And she named him \zwd Zebulun|Q614575\zwd*.\f + \fr 30:20 \ft Zebulun sounds like the Hebrew for honor.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 21 \zdate|Q122035:P569\*After that, \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd* gave birth to a daughter and named her \zwd Dinah|Q122035\zwd*. 
\s1 Joseph
\b
\m 
\v 22 Then God remembered \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd*. He listened to her and opened her womb, 
\v 23 \zdate|Q145746:P569\*and she conceived and gave birth to a son. “God has taken away my shame,” she said. 
\v 24 She named him \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*,\f + \fr 30:24 \ft Joseph means may He add.\f* and said, “May the LORD add to me another son.” 
\s1 Jacob Prospers
\b
\m 
\v 25 Now after \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* had given birth to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* said to \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd*, “Send me on my way so I can return to my homeland. 
\v 26 Give me \zwd my wives and children|\zwd* for whom I have served you, that I may go on my way. You know how hard I have worked for you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 27 But \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* replied, “If I have found favor in your eyes, please stay. I have learned by divination that the LORD has blessed me because of you.” 
\v 28 And he added, “Name your wages, and I will pay them.” 
\b
\m 
\v 29 Then \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* answered, “You know how I have served you and how your livestock have thrived under my care. 
\v 30 Indeed, you had very little before my arrival, but now your wealth has increased many times over. The LORD has blessed you wherever I set foot. But now, when may I also provide for my own household?” 
\b
\m 
\v 31 “What can I give you?” \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* asked. 
\b
\m “You do not need to give me anything,” \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* replied. “If you do this one thing for me, I will keep on shepherding and keeping your flocks. 
\v 32 Let me go through all your flocks today and remove from them every speckled or spotted sheep, every dark-colored lamb, and every spotted or speckled goat. These will be my wages. 
\v 33 So my honesty will testify for me when you come to check on my wages in the future. If I have any goats that are not speckled or spotted, or any lambs that are not dark-colored, they will be considered stolen.” 
\b
\m 
\v 34 “Agreed,” said \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd*. “Let it be as you have said.” 
\b
\m 
\v 35 That very day \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* removed all the streaked or spotted male goats and every speckled or spotted female goat—every one that had any white on it—and every dark-colored lamb, and he placed them under the care of \zwd his sons|\zwd*. 
\v 36 Then he put a three-day journey between himself and \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, while \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* was shepherding the rest of \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd*’s flocks. 
\b
\m 
\v 37 \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, however, took fresh branches of poplar, almond, and plane trees, and peeled the bark, exposing the white inner wood of the branches. 
\v 38 Then he set the peeled branches in the watering troughs in front of the flocks coming in to drink. So when the flocks were in heat and came to drink, 
\v 39 they mated in front of the branches. And they bore young that were streaked or speckled or spotted. 
\v 40 \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* set apart the young, but made the rest face the streaked dark-colored sheep in \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd*’s flocks. Then he set his own stock apart and did not put them with \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd*’s animals. 
\b
\m 
\v 41 Whenever the stronger females of the flock were in heat, \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* would place the branches in the troughs, in full view of the animals, so that they would breed in front of the branches. 
\v 42 But if the animals were weak, he did not set out the branches. So the weaker animals went to \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* and the stronger ones to \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 43 Thus \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* became exceedingly prosperous. He owned large flocks, maidservants and menservants, and camels and donkeys. 
\c 31
\s1 Jacob Flees from Laban
\m 
\v 1 Now \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* heard that \zwd \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd*’s sons|\zwd* were saying, “\zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* has taken away all that belonged to \zwd our father|Q840401\zwd* and built all this wealth at \zwd our father|Q840401\zwd*’s expense.” 
\v 2 And \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* saw from the countenance of \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* that his attitude toward him had changed. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Then the LORD said to \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, “Go back to the land of your fathers and to your kindred, and I will be with you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 So \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* sent word and called \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* and \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd* to the field where his flocks were, 
\v 5 and he told them, “I can see from \zwd your father|Q840401\zwd*’s countenance that his attitude toward me has changed; but the God of my father has been with me. 
\v 6 You know that I have served \zwd your father|Q840401\zwd* with all my strength. 
\v 7 And although he has cheated me and changed my wages ten times, God has not allowed him to harm me. 
\v 8 If he said, ‘The speckled will be your wages,’ then the whole flock bore speckled offspring. If he said, ‘The streaked will be your wages,’ then the whole flock bore streaked offspring. 
\v 9 Thus God has taken away \zwd your father|Q840401\zwd*’s livestock and given them to me. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 When the flocks were breeding, I saw in a dream that the streaked, spotted, and speckled males were mating with the females. 
\v 11 In that dream the angel \f + \fr 31:11 \ft Or Angel\f* of God said to me, ‘\zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*!’ 
\b
\m And I replied, ‘Here I am.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 12 ‘Look up,’ he said, ‘and see that all the males that are mating with the flock are streaked, spotted, or speckled; for I have seen all that \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* has done to you. 
\v 13 I am the God of Bethel, where you anointed the pillar and made a solemn vow to Me. Now get up, leave this land at once, and return to your native land.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 And \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* and \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd* replied, “Do we have any portion or inheritance left in \zwd our father|Q840401\zwd*’s house? 
\v 15 Are we not regarded by him as outsiders? Not only has he sold us, but he has certainly squandered what was paid for us. 
\v 16 Surely all the wealth that God has taken away from \zwd our father|Q840401\zwd* belongs to us and to our children. So do whatever God has told you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 Then \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* got up and put \zwd his children|Q1773005,Q282220,Q651256,Q614575,Q122035,Q145746,Q1775026,Q1771963,Q851749,Q550869,Q625661,Q215512\zwd* and \zwd his wives|Q128847,Q207389,Q794991,Q221826\zwd* on camels, 
\v 18 and he drove all his livestock before him, along with all the possessions he had acquired in Paddan-aram,\f + \fr 31:18 \ft That is, northwest Mesopotamia\f* to go to \zwd his father Isaac|Q671872\zwd* in the land in Canaan. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 Now while \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* was out shearing his sheep, \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* stole \zwd her father|Q840401\zwd*’s household idols. 
\v 20 Moreover, \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* deceived \f + \fr 31:20 \ft Or stole the heart of; also in verses 26 and 27\f* \zwd Laban the Aramean|Q840401\zwd* by not telling him that he was running away. 
\v 21 So he fled with all his possessions, crossed the Euphrates,\f + \fr 31:21 \ft Hebrew the River\f* and headed for the hill country of Gilead. 
\s1 Laban Pursues Jacob
\b
\m 
\v 22 On the third day \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* was informed that \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* had fled. 
\v 23 So he took his relatives with him, pursued \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* for seven days, and overtook him in the hill country of Gilead. 
\v 24 But that night God came to \zwd Laban the Aramean|Q840401\zwd* in a dream and warned him, “Be careful not to say anything to \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, either good or bad.” 
\b
\m 
\v 25 Now \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* had pitched his tent in the hill country of Gilead when \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* overtook him, and \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* and his relatives camped there as well. 
\v 26 Then \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* said to \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, “What have you done? You have deceived me and carried off \zwd my daughters|Q207389,Q128847\zwd* like captives of war! 
\v 27 Why did you run away secretly and deceive me, without even telling me? I would have sent you away with joy and singing, with tambourines and harps. 
\v 28 But you did not even let me kiss \zwd my grandchildren|Q1773005,Q282220,Q651256,Q614575,Q122035,Q145746,Q1775026,Q1771963,Q851749,Q550869,Q625661,Q215512\zwd* and \zwd my daughters|Q207389,Q128847\zwd* goodbye. Now you have done a foolish thing. 
\b
\m 
\v 29 I have power to do you great harm, but last night the God of \zwd your father|\zwd* said to me, ‘Be careful not to say anything to \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, either good or bad.’ 
\v 30 Now you have gone off because you long for \zwd your father|\zwd*’s house. But why have you stolen my gods?” 
\b
\m 
\v 31 “I was afraid,” \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* answered, “for I thought you would take \zwd your daughters|Q207389,Q128847\zwd* from me by force. 
\v 32 If you find your gods with anyone here, he shall not live! In the presence of our relatives, see for yourself if anything is yours, and take it back.” For \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* did not know that \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* had stolen the idols. 
\b
\m 
\v 33 So \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* went into \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*’s tent, then \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd*’s tent, and then the tents of \zwd the two maidservants|Q794991,Q221826\zwd*, but he found nothing. Then he left \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd*’s tent and entered \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd*’s tent. 
\v 34 Now \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* had taken Laban’s household idols, put them in the saddlebag of her camel, and was sitting on them. And \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* searched everything in the tent but found nothing. 
\b
\m 
\v 35 \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* said to her father, “Sir, do not be angry that I cannot stand up before you; for I am having my period.” So \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* searched but could not find the household idols. 
\b
\m 
\v 36 Then \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* became incensed and challenged \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd*. “What is my crime?” he said. “For what sin of mine have you so hotly pursued me? 
\v 37 You have searched all my goods! Have you found anything that belongs to you? Put it here before my brothers and yours, that they may judge between the two of us. 
\b
\m 
\v 38 I have been with you for twenty years now. Your sheep and goats have not miscarried, nor have I eaten the rams of your flock. 
\v 39 I did not bring you anything torn by wild beasts; I bore the loss myself. And you demanded payment from me for what was stolen by day or night. 
\v 40 As it was, the heat consumed me by day and the frost by night, and sleep fled from my eyes. 
\b
\m 
\v 41 Thus for twenty years I have served in your household—fourteen years for \zwd your two daughters|Q207389,Q128847\zwd* and six years for your flocks—and you have changed my wages ten times! 
\v 42 If the God of my father, the God of \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd* and the Fear of \zwd Isaac|id="Q671872" t="2"\zwd*, had not been with me, surely by now you would have sent me away empty-handed. But God has seen my affliction and the toil of my hands, and last night He rendered judgment.” 
\s1 Jacob’s Covenant with Laban
\b
\m 
\v 43 But \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* answered \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, “\zwd These daughters|Q207389,Q128847\zwd* are \zwd my daughters|Q207389,Q128847\zwd*, these sons are my sons, and these flocks are my flocks! Everything you see is mine! Yet what can I do today about \zwd these daughters of mine|Q207389,Q128847\zwd* or the children they have borne? 
\v 44 Come now, let us make a covenant, you and I, and let it serve as a witness between you and me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 45 So \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* picked out a stone and set it up as a pillar, 
\v 46 and he said to his relatives, “Gather some stones.” So they took stones and made a mound, and there by the mound they ate. 
\v 47 \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* called it Jegar-sahadutha, and \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* called it Galeed.\f + \fr 31:47 \ft The Aramaic Jegar-sahadutha and the Hebrew Galeed both mean heap of witnesses.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 48 Then \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* declared, “This mound is a witness between you and me this day.” 
\b
\m Therefore the place was called Galeed. 
\v 49 It was also called Mizpah,\f + \fr 31:49 \ft Mizpah means watchtower.\f* because \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* said, “May the LORD keep watch between you and me when we are absent from each other. 
\v 50 If you mistreat \zwd my daughters|Q207389,Q128847\zwd* or take other wives, although no one is with us, remember that God is a witness between you and me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 51 \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* also said to \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, “Here is the mound, and here is the pillar I have set up between you and me. 
\v 52 This mound is a witness, and this pillar is a witness, that I will not go past this mound to harm you, and you will not go past this mound and pillar to harm me. 
\v 53 May the God of \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd* and the God of \zwd Nahor|id="Q6323112" t="2"\zwd*, the God of \zwd their father|\zwd*, judge between us.” 
\b
\m So \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* swore by the Fear of \zwd his father Isaac|id="Q671872" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 54 Then \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* offered a sacrifice on the mountain and invited his relatives to eat a meal. And after they had eaten, they spent the night on the mountain. 
\v 55 Early the next morning, \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* got up and kissed \zwd his grandchildren|Q1773005,Q282220,Q651256,Q614575,Q122035,Q145746,Q1775026,Q1771963,Q851749,Q550869,Q625661,Q215512\zwd* and \zwd daughters|Q207389,Q128847\zwd* and blessed them. Then he left to return home. 
\c 32
\s1 Jacob Prepares to Meet Esau
\m 
\v 1 \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* also went on his way, and the angels of God met him. 
\v 2 When \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* saw them, he said, “This is the camp of God.” So he named that place Mahanaim.\f + \fr 32:2 \ft Mahanaim means two camps.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 3 \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* sent messengers ahead of him to \zwd his brother Esau|Q220822\zwd* in the land of Seir, the country of Edom. 
\v 4 He instructed them, “You are to say to \zwd my master Esau|Q220822\zwd*, ‘\zwd Your servant Jacob|Q289957\zwd* says: I have been staying with \zwd Laban|Q840401\zwd* and have remained there until now. 
\v 5 I have oxen, donkeys, flocks, menservants, and maidservants. I have sent this message to inform \zwd my master|Q220822\zwd*, so that I may find favor in your sight.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 When the messengers returned to \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, they said, “We went to \zwd your brother Esau|Q220822\zwd*, and now he is coming to meet you—he and four hundred men with him.” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 In great fear and distress, \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* divided his people into two camps, as well as the flocks and herds and camels. 
\v 8 He thought, “If \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* comes and attacks one camp, then the other camp can escape.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 Then \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* declared, “O God of \zwd my father Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*, God of \zwd my father Isaac|id="Q671872" t="2"\zwd*, the LORD who told me, ‘Go back to your country and to your kindred, and I will make you prosper,’ 
\v 10 I am unworthy of all the kindness and faithfulness You have shown \zwd Your servant|Q289957\zwd*. Indeed, with only my staff I came across the Jordan, but now I have become two camps. 
\v 11 Please deliver me from the hand of \zwd my brother Esau|Q220822\zwd*, for I am afraid that he may come and attack me and the mothers and children with me. 
\v 12 But You have said, ‘I will surely make you prosper, and I will make your offspring like the sand of the sea, too numerous to count.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* spent the night there, and from what he had brought with him, he selected a gift for \zwd his brother Esau|Q220822\zwd*: 
\v 14 200 female goats, 20 male goats, 200 ewes, 20 rams, 
\v 15 30 milk camels with their young, 40 cows, 10 bulls, 20 female donkeys, and 10 male donkeys. 
\v 16 He entrusted them to his servants in separate herds and told them, “Go on ahead of me, and keep some distance between the herds.” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 He instructed the one in the lead, “When \zwd my brother Esau|Q220822\zwd* meets you and asks, ‘To whom do you belong, where are you going, and whose animals are these before you?’ 
\v 18 then you are to say, ‘They belong to \zwd your servant Jacob|Q289957\zwd*. They are a gift, sent to \zwd my lord Esau|Q220822\zwd*. And behold, \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* is behind us.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 He also instructed the second, the third, and all those following behind the herds: “When you meet \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*, you are to say the same thing to him. 
\v 20 You are also to say, ‘Look, \zwd your servant Jacob|Q289957\zwd* is right behind us.’” For he thought, “I will appease \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* \f + \fr 32:20 \ft Or I will appease his face\f* with the gift that is going before me. After that I can face him, and perhaps he will accept me.\f + \fr 32:20 \ft Literally perhaps he will lift up my face\f*” 
\b
\m 
\v 21 So \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*’s gifts went on before him, while he spent the night in the camp. 
\s1 Jacob Wrestles with God
\b
\m 
\v 22 During the night \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* got up and took \zwd his two wives|Q207389,Q128847\zwd*, \zwd his two maidservants|Q794991,Q221826\zwd*, and \zwd his eleven sons|Q1773005,Q282220,Q651256,Q614575,Q145746,Q1775026,Q1771963,Q851749,Q550869,Q625661,Q215512\zwd*, and crossed the ford of the Jabbok. 
\v 23 He took them and sent them across the stream, along with all his possessions. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 So \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* was left all alone, and there a man \f + \fr 32:24 \ft Or Man; here and in verses 25–28; corresponding pronouns may also be capitalized\f* wrestled with him until daybreak. 
\v 25 When the man saw that he could not overpower \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, he struck the socket of \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*’s hip and dislocated it as they wrestled. 
\v 26 Then the man said, “Let me go, for it is daybreak.” 
\b
\m But \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* replied, “I will not let you go unless you bless me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 27 “What is your name?” the man asked. 
\b
\m “\zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*,” he replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 28 Then the man said, “Your name will no longer be \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*,\f + \fr 32:28 \ft Jacob means he grasps the heel or he deceives.\f* but \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd*,\f + \fr 32:28 \ft Israel means he struggles with God.\f* because you have struggled with God and with men, and you have prevailed.” 
\b
\m 
\v 29 And \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* requested, “Please tell me your name.” 
\b
\m But he replied, “Why do you ask my name?” Then he blessed \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* there. 
\b
\m 
\v 30 So \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* named the place Peniel,\f + \fr 32:30 \ft Peniel means the face of God.\f* saying, “Indeed, I have seen God face to face, and yet my life was spared.” 
\b
\m 
\v 31 The sun rose above him as he passed by Penuel,\f + \fr 32:31 \ft Penuel is a variant of Peniel; see verse 30.\f* and he was limping because of his hip. 
\v 32 Therefore to this day the Israelites do not eat the tendon attached to the socket of the hip, because the socket of \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*’s hip was struck near that tendon. 
\c 33
\s1 Jacob Meets Esau
\m 
\v 1 Now \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* looked up and saw \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* coming toward him with four hundred men. So he divided the children among \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd*, \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd*, and \zwd the two maidservants|Q794991,Q221826\zwd*. 
\v 2 He put \zwd the maidservants|Q794991,Q221826\zwd* and \zwd their children|\zwd* in front, \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd* and \zwd her children|\zwd* next, and \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* and \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* at the rear. 
\v 3 But \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* himself went on ahead and bowed to the ground seven times as he approached \zwd his brother|Q220822\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*, however, ran to him and embraced him, threw his arms around his neck, and kissed him. And they both wept. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 When \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* looked up and saw \zwd the women and children|Q128847,Q207389,Q794991,Q221826,Q1773005,Q282220,Q651256,Q614575,Q122035,Q145746,Q1775026,Q1771963,Q851749,Q550869,Q625661,Q215512\zwd*, he asked, “Who are these with you?” 
\b
\m \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* answered, “These are the children God has graciously given \zwd your servant|Q289957\zwd*.” 
\v 6 Then \zwd the maidservants|Q794991,Q221826\zwd* and \zwd their children|\zwd* approached and bowed down. 
\v 7 \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd* and her children also approached and bowed down, and then \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* and \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* approached and bowed down. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 “What do you mean by sending this whole company to meet me?” asked \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*. 
\b
\m “To find favor in your sight, my lord,” \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* answered. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 “I already have plenty, \zwd my brother|Q289957\zwd*,” \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* replied. “Keep what belongs to you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 But \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* insisted, “No, please! If I have found favor in your sight, then receive this gift from my hand. For indeed, I have seen your face, and it is like seeing the face of God, since you have received me favorably. 
\v 11 Please accept my gift \f + \fr 33:11 \ft Or blessing or treaty of peace\f* that was brought to you, because God has been gracious to me and I have all I need.” So \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* pressed him until he accepted. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 Then \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* said, “Let us be on our way, and I will go ahead of you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 But \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* replied, “My lord knows that the children are frail, and I must care for sheep and cattle that are nursing their young. If they are driven hard for even a day, all the animals will die. 
\v 14 Please let my lord go ahead of his servant. I will continue on slowly, at a comfortable pace for the livestock and children, until I come to my lord at Seir.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 “Let me leave some of my people with you,” \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* said. 
\b
\m But \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* replied, “Why do that? Let me find favor in the sight of my lord.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 So that day \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* started on his way back to Seir, 
\v 17 but \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* went on to Succoth,\f + \fr 33:17 \ft Succoth means booths or shelters or tabernacles; twice in this verse.\f* where he built a house for himself and shelters for his livestock; that is why the place was called Succoth. 
\s1 Jacob Settles in Shechem
\b
\m 
\v 18 After \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* had come from Paddan-aram,\f + \fr 33:18 \ft That is, northwest Mesopotamia\f* he arrived safely at the city of Shechem in the land of Canaan, and he camped just outside the city. 
\v 19 And the plot of ground where he pitched his tent, he purchased from the sons of \zwd Hamor|Q1573937\zwd*, \zwd Shechem|Q22054796\zwd*’s father, for a hundred pieces of silver.\f + \fr 33:19 \ft Hebrew a hundred kesitahs; the value or weight of the kesitah is no longer known\f* 
\v 20 There he set up an altar and called it El-Elohe-Israel.\f + \fr 33:20 \ft El-Elohe-Israel means God is the God of Israel or mighty is the God of Israel.\f* 
\c 34
\s1 The Defiling of Dinah
\m 
\v 1 Now \zwd Dinah|Q122035\zwd*, the daughter \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd* had borne to \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, went out to visit the daughters of the land. 
\v 2 When \zwd Shechem son of \zwd Hamor|Q1573937\zwd* the Hivite|Q22054796\zwd*, the prince of the region, saw her, he took her and lay with her by force. 
\v 3 And his soul was drawn to \zwd Dinah|Q122035\zwd*, the daughter of \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*. He loved \zwd the young girl|Q122035\zwd* and spoke to her tenderly. 
\v 4 So \zwd Shechem|Q22054796\zwd* told \zwd his father Hamor|Q1573937\zwd*, “Get me \zwd this girl|Q122035\zwd* as a wife.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* heard that \zwd Shechem|Q22054796\zwd* had defiled \zwd his daughter Dinah|Q122035\zwd*, but since \zwd his sons|Q1773005,Q282220,Q651256,Q614575,Q145746,Q1775026,Q1771963,Q851749,Q550869,Q625661,Q215512\zwd* were with his livestock in the field, he remained silent about it until they returned. 
\v 6 Meanwhile, \zwd \zwd Shechem|Q22054796\zwd*’s father Hamor|Q1573937\zwd* came to speak with \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*. 
\v 7 When \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*’s sons heard what had happened, they returned from the field. They were filled with grief and fury, because \zwd Shechem|Q22054796\zwd* had committed an outrage in \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* \f + \fr 34:7 \ft Or against Israel\f* by lying with \zwd \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*’s daughter|Q122035\zwd*—a thing that should not be done. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 But \zwd Hamor|Q1573937\zwd* said to them, “\zwd My son Shechem|Q22054796\zwd* longs for \zwd your daughter|Q122035\zwd*. Please give her to him as his wife. 
\v 9 Intermarry with us; give us your daughters and take our daughters for yourselves. 
\v 10 You may settle among us, and the land will be open to you. Live here, move about freely, and acquire your own property.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 Then \zwd Shechem|Q22054796\zwd* said to \zwd \zwd Dinah|Q122035\zwd*’s father|Q289957\zwd* and brothers, “Grant me this favor, and I will give you whatever you ask. 
\v 12 Demand a high dowry and an expensive gift, and I will give you whatever you ask. Only give me \zwd the girl|Q122035\zwd* as my wife!” 
\s1 The Revenge of Dinah’s Brothers
\b
\m 
\v 13 But because \zwd Shechem|Q22054796\zwd* had defiled \zwd their sister Dinah|Q122035\zwd*, \zwd \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*’s sons|Q1773005,Q282220,Q651256,Q614575,Q145746,Q1775026,Q1771963,Q851749,Q550869,Q625661,Q215512\zwd* answered him and \zwd his father Hamor|Q1573937\zwd* deceitfully. 
\v 14 “We cannot do such a thing,” they said. “To give \zwd our sister|Q122035\zwd* to an uncircumcised man would be a disgrace to us. 
\v 15 We will consent to this on one condition, that you become circumcised like us—every one of your males. 
\v 16 Then we will give you our daughters and take your daughters for ourselves. We will dwell among you and become one people. 
\v 17 But if you will not agree to be circumcised, then we will take \zwd our sister|Q122035\zwd* and go.” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 Their offer seemed good to \zwd Hamor|Q1573937\zwd* and \zwd his son Shechem|Q22054796\zwd*. 
\v 19 \zwd The young man|Q22054796\zwd*, who was the most respected of all his father’s household, did not hesitate to fulfill this request, because he was delighted with \zwd \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*’s daughter|Q122035\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 So \zwd Hamor|Q1573937\zwd* and \zwd his son Shechem|Q22054796\zwd* went to the gate of their city and addressed the men of their city: 
\v 21 “These men are at peace with us. Let them live and trade in our land; indeed, it is large enough for them. Let us take their daughters in marriage and give our daughters to them. 
\v 22 But only on this condition will the men agree to dwell with us and be one people: if all our men are circumcised as they are. 
\v 23 Will not their livestock, their possessions, and all their animals become ours? Only let us consent to them, and they will dwell among us.” 
\b
\m 
\v 24 All the men who went out of the city gate listened to \zwd Hamor|Q1573937\zwd* and \zwd his son Shechem|Q22054796\zwd*, and every male of the city was circumcised. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 Three days later, while they were still in pain, two of \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*’s sons (\zwd Dinah|id="Q122035" t="2"\zwd*’s brothers \zwd Simeon|Q1773005\zwd* and \zwd Levi|Q215512\zwd*) took their swords, went into the unsuspecting city, and slaughtered every male. 
\v 26 They killed \zwd Hamor|Q1573937\zwd* and \zwd his son Shechem|Q22054796\zwd* with their swords, took \zwd Dinah|Q122035\zwd* out of \zwd Shechem|Q22054796\zwd*’s house, and went away. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 \zwd \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*’s other sons|Q282220,Q651256,Q614575,Q145746,Q1775026,Q1771963,Q851749,Q550869,Q625661\zwd* came upon the slaughter and looted the city, because \zwd their sister|Q122035\zwd* had been defiled. 
\v 28 They took their flocks and herds and donkeys, and everything else in the city or in the field. 
\v 29 They carried off all their possessions and women and children, and they plundered everything in their houses. 
\b
\m 
\v 30 Then \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* said to \zwd Simeon|Q1773005\zwd* and \zwd Levi|Q215512\zwd*, “You have brought trouble upon me by making me a stench to the Canaanites and Perizzites, the people of this land. We are few in number; if they unite against me and attack me, I and my household will be destroyed.” 
\b
\m 
\v 31 But they replied, “Should he have treated \zwd our sister|Q122035\zwd* like a prostitute?” 
\c 35
\s1 Jacob Returns to Bethel
\m 
\v 1 Then God said to \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, “Arise, go up to Bethel, and settle there. Build an altar there to the God who appeared to you when you fled from \zwd your brother Esau|Q220822\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 So \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* told his household and all who were with him, “Get rid of the foreign gods that are among you. Purify yourselves and change your garments. 
\v 3 Then let us arise and go to Bethel. I will build an altar there to God, who answered me in my day of distress. He has been with me wherever I have gone.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 So they gave \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* all their foreign gods and all their earrings, and \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* buried them under the oak \f + \fr 35:4 \ft Or terebinth or great tree\f* near Shechem. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 As they set out, a terror from God fell over the surrounding cities, so that they did not pursue \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*’s sons. 
\v 6 So \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* and everyone with him arrived in Luz (that is, Bethel) in the land of Canaan. 
\v 7 There \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* built an altar, and he called that place El-bethel,\f + \fr 35:7 \ft El-bethel means God of Bethel.\f* because it was there that God had revealed Himself to \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* as he fled from \zwd his brother|Q220822\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 Now \zwd Deborah|Q2903219\zwd*, \zwd Rebekah|Q40520\zwd*’s nurse, died and was buried under the oak \f + \fr 35:8 \ft Or great tree\f* below Bethel. So \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* named it Allon-bacuth.\f + \fr 35:8 \ft Allon-bacuth means oak of weeping.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 9 After \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* had returned from Paddan-aram,\f + \fr 35:9 \ft That is, northwest Mesopotamia; also in verse 26\f* God appeared to him again and blessed him. 
\v 10 And God said to him, “Though your name is \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*,\f + \fr 35:10 \ft Jacob means he grasps the heel or he deceives.\f* you will no longer be called \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*. Instead, your name will be \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd*.\f + \fr 35:10 \ft Israel means he struggles with God.\f*” So God named him \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 And God told him, “I am God Almighty.\f + \fr 35:11 \ft Hebrew El-Shaddai\f* Be fruitful and multiply. A nation—even a company of nations—shall come from you, and kings shall descend from you. 
\v 12 The land that I gave to \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd Isaac|id="Q671872" t="2"\zwd* I will give to you, and I will give this land to your descendants after you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 Then God went up from the place where He had spoken with him. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 So \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* set up a pillar in the place where God had spoken with him—a stone marker—and he poured out a drink offering on it and anointed it with oil. 
\v 15 \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* called the place where God had spoken with him Bethel.\f + \fr 35:15 \ft Bethel means house of God.\f* 
\s1 Benjamin Born, Rachel Dies
\b
\m 
\v 16 \zdate|Q460763:P569\*Later, they set out from Bethel, and while they were still some distance from Ephrath, \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* began to give birth, and her labor was difficult. 
\v 17 During her severe labor, the midwife said to her, “Do not be afraid, for you are having another son.” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 And with her last breath—for she was dying—she named him \zwd Ben-oni|Q460763\zwd*.\f + \fr 35:18 \ft Ben-oni could mean son of my sorrow or son of my strength.\f* But his father called him \zwd Benjamin|Q460763\zwd*.\f + \fr 35:18 \ft Benjamin means son of my right hand.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 19 \zdate|Q207389:P570\*So \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* died and was buried on the way to Ephrath (that is, Bethlehem). 
\v 20 \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* set up a pillar on her grave; it marks \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd*’s tomb to this day. 
\s1 The Sons of Jacob
\r (1 Chronicles 2:1–2)
\b
\m 
\v 21 \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* again set out and pitched his tent beyond the Tower of Eder. 
\v 22 While \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* was living in that region, \zwd Reuben|Q625661\zwd* went in and slept with \zwd his father’s concubine Bilhah|Q794991\zwd*, and \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* heard about it. 
\b
\m \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* had twelve sons: 
\b
\li1 
\v 23 The sons of \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd* were \zwd Reuben|Q625661\zwd* the firstborn of \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, \zwd Simeon|Q1773005\zwd*, \zwd Levi|Q215512\zwd*, \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd*, \zwd Issachar|Q651256\zwd*, and \zwd Zebulun|Q614575\zwd*. 
\b
\li1 
\v 24 The sons of \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* were \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* and \zwd Benjamin|Q460763\zwd*. 
\b
\li1 
\v 25 The sons of \zwd \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd*’s maidservant Bilhah|Q794991\zwd* were \zwd Dan|Q550869\zwd* and \zwd Naphtali|Q1771963\zwd*. 
\b
\li1 
\v 26 And the sons of \zwd \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd*’s maidservant Zilpah|Q221826\zwd* were \zwd Gad|Q851749\zwd* and \zwd Asher|Q1775026\zwd*. 
\b
\m These are the sons of \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, who were born to him in Paddan-aram. 
\s1 The Death of Isaac
\b
\m 
\v 27 \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* returned to \zwd his father Isaac|Q671872\zwd* at Mamre, near Kiriath-arba (that is, Hebron), where \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* had stayed. 
\b
\m 
\v 28 And \zwd Isaac|Q671872\zwd* lived 180 years. 
\v 29 Then he breathed his last and died and was gathered to his people, old and full of years. And his sons \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* and \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* buried him. 
\c 36
\s1 The Descendants of Esau
\r (1 Chronicles 1:35–37)
\m 
\v 1 This is the account of \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* (that is, Edom). 
\v 2 \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* took his wives from the daughters of Canaan: \zwd Adah daughter of \zwd Elon the Hittite|id="Q65252558" t="2"\zwd*|Q104901768\zwd*, \zwd Oholibamah daughter of \zwd Anah|id="Q42532204" t="2"\zwd* and granddaughter of \zwd Zibeon the Hivite|\zwd*|Q2344483\zwd*, 
\v 3 and \zwd Basemath daughter of \zwd Ishmael|id="Q183403" t="2"\zwd* and sister of \zwd Nebaioth|Q3773487\zwd*|Q3320047\zwd*. 
\v 4 And \zwd Adah|Q104901768\zwd* bore \zwd Eliphaz|Q2899130\zwd* to \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*, \zwd Basemath|Q3320047\zwd* gave birth to \zwd Reuel|Q32979741\zwd*, 
\v 5 and \zwd Oholibamah|Q2344483\zwd* gave birth to \zwd Jeush|Q10306885\zwd*, \zwd Jalam|Q125263542\zwd*, and \zwd Korah|Q85774893\zwd*. These were the sons of \zwd Esau|id="Q220822" t="2"\zwd*, who were born to him in the land of Canaan. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 Later, \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* took \zwd his wives|\zwd* and sons and daughters and all the people of his household, along with his livestock, all his other animals, and all the property he had acquired in Canaan, and he moved to a land far away from his brother \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*. 
\v 7 For their possessions were too great for them to dwell together; the land where they stayed could not support them because of their livestock. 
\v 8 So \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* (that is, Edom) settled in the area of Mount Seir. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 This is the account of \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*, the father of the Edomites, in the area of Mount Seir. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 These are the names of \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*’s sons: \zwd Eliphaz son of \zwd \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*’s wife Adah|Q104901768\zwd*|Q2899130\zwd*, and \zwd Reuel son of \zwd \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*’s wife Basemath|Q3320047\zwd*|Q32979741\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 The sons of \zwd Eliphaz|Q2899130\zwd* were \zwd Teman|Q134715631\zwd*, \zwd Omar|Q7089609\zwd*, \zwd Zepho|Q8069299\zwd*, \zwd Gatam|Q106145044\zwd*, and \zwd Kenaz|Q114627203\zwd*. 
\v 12 Additionally, \zwd Timna|Q32979730\zwd*, a concubine of \zwd \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*’s son Eliphaz|Q2899130\zwd*, gave birth to \zwd Amalek|\zwd*. These are the grandsons of \zwd \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*’s wife Adah|Q104901768\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 These are the sons of \zwd Reuel|Q32979741\zwd*: \zwd Nahath|Q108487406\zwd*, \zwd Zerah|Q108487424\zwd*, \zwd Shammah|Q114627891\zwd*, and \zwd Mizzah|Q6884915\zwd*. They are the grandsons of \zwd \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*’s wife Basemath|Q3320047\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 These are the sons of \zwd \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*’s wife Oholibamah|Q2344483\zwd* (daughter of \zwd Anah|id="Q42532204" t="2"\zwd* and granddaughter of \zwd Zibeon|\zwd*) whom she bore to \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*: \zwd Jeush|Q10306885\zwd*, \zwd Jalam|Q125263542\zwd*, and \zwd Korah|Q85774893\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 These are the chiefs among the sons of \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*. The sons of \zwd Eliphaz|Q2899130\zwd* the firstborn of \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*: Chiefs \zwd Teman|Q134715631\zwd*, \zwd Omar|Q7089609\zwd*, \zwd Zepho|Q8069299\zwd*, \zwd Kenaz|Q114627203\zwd*, 
\v 16 \zwd Korah|\zwd*,\f + \fr 36:16 \ft Hebrew; SP (also in verse 11 and 1 Chronicles 1:36) does not include Korah.\f* \zwd Gatam|Q106145044\zwd*, and \zwd Amalek|\zwd*. They are the chiefs of \zwd Eliphaz|Q2899130\zwd* in the land of Edom, and they are the grandsons of \zwd Adah|Q104901768\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 These are the sons of \zwd \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*’s son Reuel|Q32979741\zwd*: Chiefs \zwd Nahath|Q108487406\zwd*, \zwd Zerah|Q108487424\zwd*, \zwd Shammah|Q114627891\zwd*, and \zwd Mizzah|Q6884915\zwd*. They are the chiefs descended from \zwd Reuel|Q32979741\zwd* in the land of Edom, and they are the grandsons of \zwd \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*’s wife Basemath|Q3320047\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 These are the sons of \zwd \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*’s wife Oholibamah|Q2344483\zwd*: Chiefs \zwd Jeush|Q10306885\zwd*, \zwd Jalam|Q125263542\zwd*, and \zwd Korah|Q85774893\zwd*. They are the chiefs descended from \zwd \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd*’s wife Oholibamah|Q2344483\zwd*, the daughter of \zwd Anah|id="Q42532204" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 All these are the sons of \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* (that is, Edom), and they were their chiefs. 
\s1 The Descendants of Seir
\r (1 Chronicles 1:38–42)
\b
\m 
\v 20 These are the sons of \zwd Seir the Horite|Q42532277\zwd*, who were living in the land: \zwd Lotan|Q6684617\zwd*, \zwd Shobal|Q125423902\zwd*, \zwd Zibeon|\zwd*, \zwd Anah|\zwd*, 
\v 21 \zwd Dishon|Q124820244\zwd*, \zwd Ezer|Q10274802\zwd*, and \zwd Dishan|Q124820697\zwd*. They are the chiefs of the Horites, the descendants of \zwd Seir|Q42532277\zwd* in the land of Edom. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 The sons of \zwd Lotan|Q6684617\zwd* were \zwd Hori|Q55120394\zwd* and \zwd Hemam|Q106084820\zwd*.\f + \fr 36:22 \ft Hemam is a variant of Homam; see 1 Chronicles 1:39.\f* \zwd Timna|\zwd* was \zwd Lotan|Q6684617\zwd*’s sister. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 These are the sons of \zwd Shobal|Q125423902\zwd*: \zwd Alvan|Q124552466\zwd*, \zwd Manahath|Q125315789\zwd*, \zwd Ebal|Q125045346\zwd*, \zwd Shepho|Q138580874\zwd*, and \zwd Onam|Q138580877\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 These are the sons of \zwd Zibeon|\zwd*: \zwd Aiah|\zwd* and \zwd Anah|Q42532204\zwd*. (This is the \zwd Anah|Q42532204\zwd* who found the hot springs in the wilderness as he was pasturing the donkeys of \zwd his father Zibeon|\zwd*.) 
\b
\m 
\v 25 These are the children of \zwd Anah|Q42532204\zwd*: \zwd Dishon|Q124821119\zwd* and \zwd Oholibamah daughter of \zwd Anah|id="Q42532204" t="2"\zwd*|Q2344483\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 These are the sons of \zwd Dishon|Q124820244\zwd*:\f + \fr 36:26 \ft Hebrew Dishan, a variant of Dishon\f* \zwd Hemdan|Q124556126\zwd*, \zwd Eshban|Q125038626\zwd*, \zwd Ithran|Q125263780\zwd*, and \zwd Cheran|Q138580889\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 These are the sons of \zwd Ezer|Q10274802\zwd*: \zwd Bilhan|\zwd*, \zwd Zaavan|Q125498533\zwd*, and \zwd Akan|\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 28 These are the sons of \zwd Dishan|Q124820697\zwd*: \zwd Uz|Q138580891\zwd* and \zwd Aran|Q124556347\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 29 These are the chiefs of the Horites: Chiefs \zwd Lotan|Q6684617\zwd*, \zwd Shobal|Q125423902\zwd*, \zwd Zibeon|\zwd*, \zwd Anah|\zwd*, 
\v 30 \zwd Dishon|Q124820244\zwd*, \zwd Ezer|Q10274802\zwd*, and \zwd Dishan|Q124820697\zwd*. They are the chiefs of the Horites, according to their divisions in the land of Seir. 
\s1 The Kings of Edom
\r (1 Chronicles 1:43–54)
\b
\m 
\v 31 These are the kings who reigned in the land of Edom before any king reigned over the Israelites:\f + \fr 36:31 \ft Or before an Israelite king reigned over them:\f* 
\b
\li1 
\v 32 \zwd Bela son of \zwd Beor|Q124759391\zwd*|Q4081479\zwd* reigned in Edom; the name of his city was Dinhabah. 
\b
\li1 
\v 33 \zdate|Q4081479:P570\*When \zwd Bela|Q4081479\zwd* died, \zwd Jobab son of \zwd Zerah|\zwd*|Q6206768\zwd* from Bozrah reigned in his place. 
\b
\li1 
\v 34 \zdate|Q6206768:P570\*When \zwd Jobab|Q6206768\zwd* died, \zwd Husham|Q5949264\zwd* from the land of the Temanites reigned in his place. 
\b
\li1 
\v 35 \zdate|Q5949264:P570\*When \zwd Husham|Q5949264\zwd* died, \zwd Hadad son of \zwd Bedad|Q124759472\zwd*|Q5637556\zwd*, who defeated Midian in the country of Moab, reigned in his place. And the name of his city was Avith. 
\b
\li1 
\v 36 \zdate|Q5637556:P570\*When \zwd Hadad|Q5637556\zwd* died, \zwd Samlah|Q7409616\zwd* from Masrekah reigned in his place. 
\b
\li1 
\v 37 \zdate|Q7409616:P570\*When \zwd Samlah|Q7409616\zwd* died, \zwd Shaul|Q138580913\zwd* from Rehoboth on the Euphrates \f + \fr 36:37 \ft Hebrew the River\f* reigned in his place. 
\b
\li1 
\v 38 \zdate|Q138580913:P570\*When \zwd Shaul|Q138580913\zwd* died, \zwd Baal-hanan son of \zwd Achbor|Q135454512\zwd*|Q5717054\zwd* reigned in his place. 
\b
\li1 
\v 39 \zdate|Q5717054:P570\*When \zwd Baal-hanan son of \zwd Achbor|Q135454512\zwd*|Q5717054\zwd* died, \zwd Hadad|Q134468308\zwd* \f + \fr 36:39 \ft Some MT manuscripts, SP, and Syriac (see also 1 Chronicles 1:50); other MT manuscripts Hadar\f* reigned in his place. His city was named Pau, and his wife’s name was \zwd Mehetabel|Q93697104\zwd* daughter of \zwd Matred|Q125271641\zwd*, the daughter of \zwd Me-zahab|Q125316367\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 40 These are the names of \zwd Esau|id="Q220822" t="2"\zwd*’s chiefs, according to their families and regions, by their names: Chiefs \zwd Timna|\zwd*, \zwd Alvah|\zwd*, \zwd Jetheth|\zwd*, 
\v 41 \zwd Oholibamah|\zwd*, \zwd Elah|id="Q5353134" t="2"\zwd*, \zwd Pinon|\zwd*, 
\v 42 \zwd Kenaz|\zwd*, \zwd Teman|\zwd*, \zwd Mibzar|\zwd*, 
\v 43 \zwd Magdiel|\zwd*, and \zwd Iram|\zwd*. These were the chiefs of Edom, according to their settlements in the land they possessed. \zwd Esau|Q220822\zwd* was the father of the Edomites. 
\c 37
\s1 Joseph’s Dreams
\m 
\v 1 Now \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* lived in the land where \zwd his father|id="Q671872" t="2"\zwd* had resided, the land of Canaan. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 \zdate|Q145746:P569+P17Y\*This is the account of \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*. When \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* was seventeen years old, he was tending the flock with \zwd his brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q460763,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd*, the sons of \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd*’s wives \zwd Bilhah|id="Q794991" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd Zilpah|id="Q221826" t="2"\zwd*, and he brought \zwd their father|Q289957\zwd* a bad report about them. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Now \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* loved \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* more than \zwd his other sons|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q460763,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd*, because \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* had been born to him in his old age; so he made him a robe of many colors.\f + \fr 37:3 \ft Possibly a robe with long sleeves; also in verses 23 and 32\f* 
\v 4 When \zwd \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q460763,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd* saw that \zwd their father|Q289957\zwd* loved him more than any of them, they hated him and could not speak a kind word to him. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Then \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* had a dream, and when he told it to \zwd his brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q460763,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd*, they hated him even more. 
\v 6 He said to them, “Listen to this dream I had: 
\v 7 We were binding sheaves of grain in the field, and suddenly my sheaf rose and stood upright, while your sheaves gathered around and bowed down to mine.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 “Do you intend to reign over us?” \zwd his brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q460763,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd* asked. “Will you actually rule us?” So they hated him even more because of his dream and his statements. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 Then \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* had another dream and told it to \zwd his brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q460763,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd*. “Look,” he said, “I had another dream, and this time the sun and moon and eleven stars were bowing down to me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 He told \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd* and \zwd brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q460763,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd*, but \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd* rebuked him and said, “What is this dream that you have had? Will \zwd your mother|id="Q207389" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q460763,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd* and I actually come and bow down to the ground before you?” 
\v 11 And \zwd his brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q460763,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd* were jealous of him, but \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd* kept in mind what he had said. 
\s1 Joseph Sold into Egypt
\r (Acts 7:9–14)
\b
\m 
\v 12 Some time later, \zwd \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q460763,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd* had gone to pasture \zwd their father|Q289957\zwd*’s flocks near Shechem. 
\v 13 \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* said to him, “Are not \zwd your brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q460763,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd* pasturing the flocks at Shechem? Get ready; I am sending you to them.” 
\b
\m “I am ready,” \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Then \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* told him, “Go now and see how \zwd your brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q460763,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd* and the flocks are faring, and bring word back to me.” 
\b
\m So he sent him off from the Valley of Hebron. And when \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* arrived in Shechem, 
\v 15 a man found him wandering in the field and asked, “What are you looking for?” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 “I am looking for \zwd my brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q460763,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd*,” \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* replied. “Can you please tell me where they are pasturing their flocks?” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 “They have moved on from here,” the man answered. “I heard them say, ‘Let us go to Dothan.’” So \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* set out after \zwd his brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q460763,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd* and found them at Dothan. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 Now \zwd \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q460763,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd* saw him in the distance, and before he arrived, they plotted to kill him. 
\v 19 “Here comes that dreamer!” they said to one another. 
\v 20 “Come now, let us kill him and throw him into one of the pits. We can say that a vicious animal has devoured him. Then we shall see what becomes of his dreams!” 
\b
\m 
\v 21 When \zwd Reuben|Q625661\zwd* heard this, he tried to rescue \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* from their hands. “Let us not take his life,” he said. 
\v 22 “Do not shed his blood. Throw him into this pit in the wilderness, but do not lay a hand on him.” \zwd Reuben|Q625661\zwd* said this so that he could rescue \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* from their hands and return him to \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 So when \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* came to \zwd his brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q460763,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd*, they stripped him of his robe—the robe of many colors he was wearing—
\v 24 and they took him and threw him into the pit. Now the pit was empty, with no water in it. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 And as they sat down to eat a meal, they looked up and saw a caravan of Ishmaelites coming from Gilead. Their camels were carrying spices, balm, and myrrh on their way down to Egypt. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 Then \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* said to \zwd his brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q460763,Q614575,Q651256,Q1773005\zwd*, “What profit will we gain if we kill our brother and cover up his blood? 
\v 27 Come, let us sell him to the Ishmaelites and not lay a hand on him; for he is our brother, our own flesh.” And they agreed. 
\v 28 So when the Midianite traders passed by, \zwd his brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q460763,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd* pulled \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* out of the pit and sold him for twenty shekels of silver \f + \fr 37:28 \ft 20 shekels is approximately 8 ounces or 228 grams of silver.\f* to the Ishmaelites, who took him to Egypt. 
\b
\m 
\v 29 When \zwd Reuben|Q625661\zwd* returned to the pit and saw that \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* was not there, he tore his clothes, 
\v 30 returned to \zwd his brothers|Q215512,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q460763,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd*, and said, “The boy is gone! What am I going to do?” 
\s1 Jacob Mourns Joseph
\b
\m 
\v 31 Then they took \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s robe, slaughtered a young goat, and dipped the robe in its blood. 
\v 32 They sent the robe of many colors to their father and said, “We found this. Examine it to see whether it is \zwd your son|Q145746\zwd*’s robe or not.” 
\b
\m 
\v 33 \zwd His father|Q289957\zwd* recognized it and said, “It is \zwd my son|Q145746\zwd*’s robe! A vicious animal has devoured him. \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* has surely been torn to pieces!” 
\v 34 Then \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* tore his clothes, put sackcloth around his waist, and mourned for \zwd his son|Q145746\zwd* many days. 
\v 35 All \zwd his sons and daughters|\zwd* tried to comfort him, but he refused to be comforted. “No,” he said. “I will go down to Sheol mourning for \zwd my son|Q145746\zwd*.” So \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd* wept for him. 
\b
\m 
\v 36 Meanwhile, the Midianites \f + \fr 37:36 \ft Hebrew the Medanites\f* sold \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* in Egypt to \zwd Potiphar|Q1148687\zwd*, an officer of Pharaoh and captain of the guard. 
\c 38
\s1 Judah and Tamar
\r (1 Chronicles 2:3–4)
\m 
\v 1 About that time, \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* left \zwd his brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q460763,Q614575,Q651256,Q1773005\zwd* and settled near a man named \zwd Hirah|Q65238144\zwd*, an Adullamite. 
\v 2 There \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* saw \zwd the daughter of a Canaanite man named \zwd Shua|Q30226376\zwd*|Q6940768\zwd*, and he took her as a wife and slept with her. 
\v 3 So she conceived and gave birth to a son, and \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* named him \zwd Er|Q1347943\zwd*. 
\v 4 Again she conceived and gave birth to a son, and she named him \zwd Onan|Q661074\zwd*. 
\v 5 Then she gave birth to another son and named him \zwd Shelah|Q1294423\zwd*; it was at Chezib that she gave birth to him. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 Now \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* acquired a wife for \zwd Er|Q1347943\zwd*, his firstborn, and her name was \zwd Tamar|Q1030008\zwd*. 
\v 7 But \zwd Er|Q1347943\zwd*, \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd*’s firstborn, was wicked in the sight of the LORD; so the LORD put him to death. 
\v 8 Then \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* said to \zwd Onan|Q661074\zwd*, “Sleep with \zwd \zwd your brother|Q1347943\zwd*’s wife|Q1030008\zwd*. Perform your duty as her brother-in-law and raise up offspring for \zwd your brother|Q1347943\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 But \zwd Onan|Q661074\zwd* knew that the offspring would not belong to him; so whenever he would sleep with \zwd \zwd his brother|Q1347943\zwd*’s wife|Q1030008\zwd*, he would spill his seed on the ground so that he would not produce offspring for \zwd his brother|Q1347943\zwd*. 
\v 10 What he did was wicked in the sight of the LORD, so He put \zwd Onan|Q661074\zwd* to death as well. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 Then \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* said to \zwd his daughter-in-law Tamar|Q1030008\zwd*, “Live as a widow in \zwd your father|\zwd*’s house until \zwd my son Shelah|Q1294423\zwd* grows up.” For he thought, “He may die too, like \zwd his brothers|Q1347943,Q661074\zwd*.” So \zwd Tamar|Q1030008\zwd* went to live in \zwd her father|\zwd*’s house. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 After a long time \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd*’s wife, \zwd the daughter of \zwd Shua|Q30226376\zwd*|Q6940768\zwd*, died. When \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* had finished mourning, he and his friend \zwd Hirah the Adullamite|Q65238144\zwd* went up to his sheepshearers at Timnah. 
\v 13 When \zwd Tamar|Q1030008\zwd* was told, “\zwd Your father-in-law|Q282220\zwd* is going up to Timnah to shear his sheep,” 
\v 14 she removed her widow’s garments, covered her face with a veil to disguise herself, and sat at the entrance to Enaim, which is on the way to Timnah. For she saw that although \zwd Shelah|Q1294423\zwd* had grown up, she had not been given to him as a wife. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 When \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* saw her, he thought she was a prostitute because she had covered her face. 
\v 16 Not realizing that she was his daughter-in-law, he went over to her and said, “Come now, let me sleep with you.” 
\b
\m “What will you give me for sleeping with you?” she inquired. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 “I will send you a young goat from my flock,” \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* answered. 
\b
\m But she replied, “Only if you leave me something as a pledge until you send it.” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 “What pledge should I give you?” he asked. 
\b
\m She answered, “Your seal and your cord, and the staff in your hand.” So he gave them to her and slept with her, and she became pregnant by him. 
\v 19 Then \zwd Tamar|Q1030008\zwd* got up and departed. And she removed her veil and put on her widow’s garments again. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 Now when \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* sent his friend \zwd Hirah the Adullamite|Q65238144\zwd* with the young goat to collect the items he had left with the woman, he could not find her. 
\v 21 He asked the men of that place, “Where is the shrine prostitute who was beside the road at Enaim?” 
\b
\m “No shrine prostitute has been here,” they answered. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 So \zwd Hirah|Q65238144\zwd* returned to \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* and said, “I could not find her, and furthermore, the men of that place said, ‘No shrine prostitute has been here.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 “Let her keep the items,” \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* replied. “Otherwise we will become a laughingstock.\f + \fr 38:23 \ft Or we will become despised\f* After all, I did send her this young goat, but you could not find her.” 
\b
\m 
\v 24 About three months later, \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* was told, “\zwd Your daughter-in-law Tamar|Q1030008\zwd* has prostituted herself, and now she is pregnant.” 
\b
\m “Bring her out!” \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* replied. “Let her be burned to death!” 
\b
\m 
\v 25 As she was being brought out, \zwd Tamar|Q1030008\zwd* sent a message to \zwd her father-in-law|Q282220\zwd*: “I am pregnant by the man to whom these items belong.” And she added, “Please examine them. Whose seal and cord and staff are these?” 
\b
\m 
\v 26 \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* recognized the items and said, “She is more righteous than I, since I did not give her to \zwd my son Shelah|Q1294423\zwd*.” And he did not have relations with her again. 
\s1 The Birth of Perez and Zerah
\b
\m 
\v 27 \zdate|Q1814684:P569\*When the time came for \zwd Tamar|Q1030008\zwd* to give birth, there were twins in her womb. 
\v 28 And as she was giving birth, one of them put out his hand; so the midwife took a scarlet thread and tied it around his wrist. “This one came out first,” she announced. 
\v 29 But when he pulled his hand back and his brother came out, she said, “You have broken out first!” So he was named \zwd Perez|Q1814684\zwd*.\f + \fr 38:29 \ft Perez means breaking out.\f* 
\v 30 Then \zwd his brother|Q3223065\zwd* came out with the scarlet thread around his wrist, and he was named \zwd Zerah|Q3223065\zwd*.\f + \fr 38:30 \ft Zerah can mean scarlet or brightness.\f* 
\c 39
\s1 Joseph and Potiphar’s Wife
\m 
\v 1 Meanwhile, \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* had been taken down to Egypt, where an Egyptian named \zwd Potiphar|Q1148687\zwd*, an officer of \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* and captain of the guard, bought him from the Ishmaelites who had taken him there. 
\v 2 And the LORD was with \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, and he became a successful man, serving in the household of \zwd his Egyptian master|Q1148687\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 When \zwd his master|Q1148687\zwd* saw that the LORD was with him and made him prosper in all he did, 
\v 4 \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* found favor in his sight and became his personal attendant. 
\b
\m \zwd Potiphar|Q1148687\zwd* put him in charge of his household and entrusted him with everything he owned. 
\v 5 From the time that he put \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* in charge of his household and all he owned, the LORD blessed the Egyptian’s household on account of him. The LORD’s blessing was on everything he owned, both in his house and in his field. 
\v 6 So \zwd Potiphar|Q1148687\zwd* left all that he owned in \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s care; he did not concern himself with anything except the food he ate. 
\b
\m Now \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* was well-built and handsome, 
\v 7 and after some time \zwd \zwd his master|Q1148687\zwd*’s wife|\zwd* cast her eyes upon \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* and said, “Sleep with me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 But he refused. “Look,” he said to \zwd \zwd his master|Q1148687\zwd*’s wife|\zwd*, “with me here, \zwd my master|Q1148687\zwd* does not concern himself with anything in his house, and he has entrusted everything he owns to my care. 
\v 9 No one in this house is greater than I am. He has withheld nothing from me except you, because you are \zwd his wife|\zwd*. So how could I do such a great evil and sin against God?” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Although \zwd \zwd Potiphar|Q1148687\zwd*’s wife|\zwd* spoke to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* day after day, he refused to go to bed with her or even be near her. 
\v 11 One day, however, \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* went into the house to attend to his work, and not a single household servant was inside. 
\v 12 She grabbed \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* by his cloak and said, “Sleep with me!” But leaving his cloak in her hand, he escaped and ran outside. 
\s1 Joseph Falsely Imprisoned
\b
\m 
\v 13 When she saw that he had left his cloak in her hand and had run out of the house, 
\v 14 she called her household servants. “Look,” she said, “this Hebrew has been brought to us to make sport of us. He came to me so he could sleep with me, but I screamed as loud as I could. 
\v 15 When he heard me scream for help, he left his cloak beside me and ran out of the house.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 So \zwd \zwd Potiphar|Q1148687\zwd*’s wife|\zwd* kept \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s cloak beside her until \zwd his master|Q1148687\zwd* came home. 
\v 17 Then she told him the same story: “The Hebrew slave you brought us came to me to make sport of me, 
\v 18 but when I screamed for help, he left his cloak beside me and ran out of the house.” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 When \zwd his master|Q1148687\zwd* heard the story \zwd his wife|\zwd* told him, saying, “This is what your slave did to me,” he burned with anger. 
\v 20 So \zwd \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s master|Q1148687\zwd* took him and had him thrown into the prison where the king’s prisoners were confined. 
\b
\m While \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* was there in the prison, 
\v 21 the LORD was with him and extended kindness to him, granting him favor in the eyes of the prison warden. 
\v 22 And the warden put all the prisoners under \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s care, so that he was responsible for all that was done in the prison. 
\v 23 The warden did not concern himself with anything under \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s care, because the LORD was with \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* and gave him success in whatever he did. 
\c 40
\s1 The Cupbearer and the Baker
\m 
\v 1 Some time later, the king’s cupbearer and baker offended their master, \zwd the king of Egypt|\zwd*. 
\v 2 \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* was angry with his two officers, the chief cupbearer and the chief baker, 
\v 3 and imprisoned them in the house of the captain of the guard, the same prison where \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* was confined. 
\v 4 The captain of the guard assigned them to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, and he became their personal attendant. 
\b
\m After they had been in custody for some time, 
\v 5 both of these men—\zwd the Egyptian king|\zwd*’s cupbearer and baker, who were being held in the prison—had a dream on the same night, and each dream had its own meaning. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 When \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* came to them in the morning, he saw that they were distraught. 
\v 7 So he asked the officials of \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* who were in custody with him in \zwd his master|\zwd*’s house, “Why are your faces so downcast today?” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 “We both had dreams,” they replied, “but there is no one to interpret them.” 
\b
\m Then \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* said to them, “Don’t interpretations belong to God? Tell me your dreams.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 So the chief cupbearer told \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* his dream: “In my dream there was a vine before me, 
\v 10 and on the vine were three branches. As it budded, its blossoms opened and its clusters ripened into grapes. 
\v 11 \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*’s cup was in my hand, and I took the grapes, squeezed them into his cup, and placed the cup in his hand.” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* replied, “This is the interpretation: The three branches are three days. 
\v 13 Within three days \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* will lift up your head and restore your position. You will put \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*’s cup in his hand, just as you did when you were his cupbearer. 
\v 14 But when it goes well for you, please remember me and show me kindness by mentioning me to \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*, that he might bring me out of this prison. 
\v 15 For I was kidnapped from the land of the Hebrews, and even here I have done nothing for which they should have put me in this dungeon.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 When the chief baker saw that the interpretation was favorable, he said to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, “I too had a dream: There were three baskets of white bread on my head. 
\v 17 In the top basket were all sorts of baked goods for \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*, but the birds were eating them out of the basket on my head.” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* replied, “This is the interpretation: The three baskets are three days. 
\v 19 Within three days \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* will lift off your head and hang you on a tree.\f + \fr 40:19 \ft Or and impale you on a pole; similarly in verse 22\f* Then the birds will eat the flesh of your body.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 On the third day, which was \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*’s birthday, he held a feast for all his officials, and in their presence he lifted up the heads of the chief cupbearer and the chief baker. 
\v 21 \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* restored the chief cupbearer to his position, so that he once again placed the cup in \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*’s hand. 
\v 22 But \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* hanged the chief baker, just as \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* had described to them in his interpretation.\f + \fr 40:22 \ft Literally had interpreted to them\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 23 The chief cupbearer, however, did not remember \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*; he forgot all about him. 
\c 41
\s1 The Dreams of Pharaoh
\m 
\v 1 After two full years had passed, \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* had a dream: He was standing beside the Nile, 
\v 2 when seven cows, sleek and well-fed, came up from the river and began to graze among the reeds. 
\v 3 After them, seven other cows, sickly and thin, came up from the Nile and stood beside the well-fed cows on the bank of the river. 
\v 4 And the cows that were sickly and thin devoured the seven sleek, well-fed cows. 
\b
\m Then \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* woke up, 
\v 5 but he fell back asleep and dreamed a second time: Seven heads of grain, plump and ripe, came up on one stalk. 
\v 6 After them, seven other heads of grain sprouted, thin and scorched by the east wind. 
\v 7 And the thin heads of grain swallowed up the seven plump, ripe ones. Then \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* awoke and realized it was a dream. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 In the morning his spirit was troubled, so he summoned all the magicians and wise men of Egypt. \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* told them his dreams, but no one could interpret them for him. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 Then the chief cupbearer said to \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*, “Today I recall my failures. 
\v 10 \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* was once angry with his servants, and he put me and the chief baker in the custody of the captain of the guard. 
\v 11 One night both the chief baker and I had dreams, and each dream had its own meaning. 
\v 12 Now a young Hebrew was there with us, a servant of the captain of the guard. We told him our dreams and he interpreted them for us individually. 
\v 13 And it happened to us just as he had interpreted: I was restored to my position, and the other man was hanged.” 
\s1 Joseph Interprets Pharaoh’s Dreams
\b
\m 
\v 14 So \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* sent for \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, who was quickly brought out of the dungeon. After he had shaved and changed his clothes, he went in before \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* said to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, “I had a dream, and no one can interpret it. But I have heard it said of you that when you hear a dream you can interpret it.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 “I myself cannot do it,” \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* replied, “but God will give \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* a sound answer.” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 Then \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* said to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*: “In my dream I was standing on the bank of the Nile, 
\v 18 when seven cows, well-fed and sleek, came up from the river and began to graze among the reeds. 
\v 19 After them, seven other cows—sickly, ugly, and thin—came up. I have never seen such ugly cows in all the land of Egypt! 
\v 20 Then the thin, ugly cows devoured the seven well-fed cows that were there first. 
\v 21 When they had devoured them, however, no one could tell that they had done so; their appearance was as ugly as it had been before. Then I awoke. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 In my dream I also saw seven heads of grain, plump and ripe, growing on a single stalk. 
\v 23 After them, seven other heads of grain sprouted—withered, thin, and scorched by the east wind. 
\v 24 And the thin heads of grain swallowed the seven plump ones. 
\b
\m I told this dream to the magicians, but no one could explain it to me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 25 At this, \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* said to \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*, “The dreams of \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* are one and the same. God has revealed to \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* what He is about to do. 
\v 26 The seven good cows are seven years, and the seven ripe heads of grain are seven years. The dreams have the same meaning. 
\v 27 Moreover, the seven thin, ugly cows that came up after them are seven years, and so are the seven worthless heads of grain scorched by the east wind—they are seven years of famine. 
\b
\m 
\v 28 It is just as I said to \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*: God has shown \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* what He is about to do. 
\v 29 Behold, seven years of great abundance are coming throughout the land of Egypt, 
\v 30 but seven years of famine will follow them. Then all the abundance in the land of Egypt will be forgotten, and the famine will devastate the land. 
\v 31 The abundance in the land will not be remembered, since the famine that follows it will be so severe. 
\b
\m 
\v 32 Moreover, because the dream was given to \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* in two versions, the matter has been decreed by God, and He will carry it out shortly. 
\b
\m 
\v 33 Now, therefore, \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* should look for a discerning and wise man and set him over the land of Egypt. 
\v 34 Let \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* take action and appoint commissioners over the land to take a fifth of the harvest \f + \fr 41:34 \ft See LXX; MT a fifth from the land\f* of Egypt during the seven years of abundance. 
\v 35 Under the authority of \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*, let them collect all the excess food from these good years, that they may come and lay up the grain to be preserved as food in the cities. 
\v 36 This food will be a reserve for the land during the seven years of famine to come upon the land of Egypt. Then the country will not perish in the famine.” 
\s1 Joseph Given Charge of Egypt
\b
\m 
\v 37 This proposal pleased \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* and all his officials. 
\v 38 So \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* asked them, “Can we find anyone like \zwd this man|Q145746\zwd*, in whom the Spirit of God \f + \fr 41:38 \ft Or the spirit of the gods\f* abides?” 
\b
\m 
\v 39 Then \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* said to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, “Since God has made all this known to you, there is no one as discerning and wise as you. 
\v 40 You shall be in charge of my house, and all my people are to obey your commands. Only with regard to the throne will I be greater than you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 41 \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* also told \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, “I hereby place you over all the land of Egypt.” 
\v 42 Then \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* removed the signet ring from his finger, put it on \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s finger, clothed him in garments of fine linen, and placed a gold chain around his neck. 
\v 43 He had \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* ride in his second chariot, with men calling out before him, “Bow the knee!”\f + \fr 41:43 \ft That is, “Abrek,” probably an Egyptian word that sounds similar to the Hebrew for kneel\f* So he placed him over all the land of Egypt. 
\b
\m 
\v 44 And \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* declared to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, “I am \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*, but without your permission, no one in all the land of Egypt shall lift his hand or foot.” 
\b
\m 
\v 45 \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* gave \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* the name \zwd Zaphenath-paneah|Q145746\zwd*,\f + \fr 41:45 \ft Zaphenath-paneah probably means Revealer of Mysteries or God speaks and lives.\f* and he gave him \zwd Asenath daughter of \zwd Potiphera|Q7234956\zwd*|Q723681\zwd*, priest of On,\f + \fr 41:45 \ft That is, Heliopolis, as in LXX; also in verse 50\f* to be his wife. And \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* took charge of all the land of Egypt. 
\s1 The Seven Years of Plenty
\b
\m 
\v 46 \zdate|Q145746:P569+P30Y\*Now \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* was thirty years old when he entered the service of \zwd Pharaoh king of Egypt|\zwd*. And \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* left \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*’s presence and traveled throughout the land of Egypt. 
\b
\m 
\v 47 During the seven years of abundance, the land brought forth bountifully. 
\v 48 During those seven years, \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* collected all the excess food in the land of Egypt and stored it in the cities. In every city he laid up the food from the fields around it. 
\v 49 So \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* stored up grain in such abundance, like the sand of the sea, that he stopped keeping track of it; for it was beyond measure. 
\b
\m 
\v 50 Before the years of famine arrived, two sons were born to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* by \zwd Asenath daughter of \zwd Potiphera|Q7234956\zwd*|Q723681\zwd*, priest of On. 
\v 51 \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* named the firstborn \zwd Manasseh|Q1056931\zwd*,\f + \fr 41:51 \ft Manasseh sounds like the Hebrew for making to forget.\f* saying, “God has made me forget all my hardship and all \zwd my father|Q289957\zwd*’s household.” 
\v 52 And the second son he named \zwd Ephraim|Q203251\zwd*,\f + \fr 41:52 \ft Ephraim sounds like the Hebrew for making fruitful or twice fruitful.\f* saying, “God has made me fruitful in the land of my affliction.” 
\s1 The Famine Begins
\b
\m 
\v 53 When the seven years of abundance in the land of Egypt came to an end, 
\v 54 the seven years of famine began, just as \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* had said. And although there was famine in every country, there was food throughout the land of Egypt. 
\v 55 When extreme hunger came to all the land of Egypt and the people cried out to \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* for food, he told all the Egyptians, “Go to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* and do whatever he tells you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 56 When the famine had spread over all the land, \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* opened up all the storehouses and sold grain to the Egyptians; for the famine was severe in the land of Egypt. 
\v 57 And every nation came to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* in Egypt to buy grain, because the famine was severe over all the earth. 
\c 42
\s1 Joseph’s Brothers Sent to Egypt
\m 
\v 1 When \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* learned that there was grain in Egypt, he said to \zwd his sons|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q460763,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd*, “Why are you staring at one another?” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 “Look,” he added, “I have heard that there is grain in Egypt. Go down there and buy some for us, so that we may live and not die.” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 So \zwd ten of \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd* went down to buy grain from Egypt. 
\v 4 But \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* did not send \zwd \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s brother Benjamin|Q460763\zwd* with his brothers, for he said, “I am afraid that harm might befall him.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 So the sons of \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* were among those who came to buy grain, since the famine had also spread to the land of Canaan. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 Now \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* was the ruler of the land; he was the one who sold grain to all its people. So when \zwd his brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd* arrived, they bowed down before him with their faces to the ground. 
\v 7 And when \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* saw \zwd his brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd*, he recognized them, but he treated them as strangers and spoke harshly to them. “Where have you come from?” he asked. 
\b
\m “From the land of Canaan,” they replied. “We are here to buy food.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 Although \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* recognized \zwd his brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd*, they did not recognize him. 
\v 9 \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* remembered his dreams about them and said, “You are spies! You have come to see if our land is vulnerable.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 “Not so, my lord,” they replied. “Your servants have come to buy food. 
\v 11 We are all sons of \zwd one man|Q289957\zwd*. Your servants are honest men, not spies.” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 “No,” he told them. “You have come to see if our land is vulnerable.” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 But they answered, “Your servants are twelve brothers, the sons of \zwd one man|Q289957\zwd* in the land of Canaan. \zwd The youngest|Q460763\zwd* is now with \zwd our father|Q289957\zwd*, and one is no more.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Then \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* declared, “Just as I said, you are spies! 
\v 15 And this is how you will be tested: As surely as \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* lives, you shall not leave this place unless \zwd your youngest brother|Q460763\zwd* comes here. 
\v 16 Send one of your number to get \zwd your brother|Q460763\zwd*; the rest of you will be confined so that the truth of your words may be tested. If they are untrue, then as surely as \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* lives, you are spies!” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 So \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* imprisoned them for three days, 
\v 18 and on the third day he said to them, “I fear God. So do this and you will live: 
\v 19 If you are honest, leave one of your brothers in custody while the rest of you go and take back grain to relieve the hunger of your households. 
\v 20 Then bring \zwd your youngest brother|Q460763\zwd* to me so that your words can be verified, that you may not die.” 
\b
\m And to this they consented. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 Then they said to one another, “Surely we are being punished because of \zwd our brother|Q145746\zwd*. We saw his anguish when he pleaded with us, but we would not listen. That is why this distress has come upon us.” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 And \zwd Reuben|Q625661\zwd* responded, “Didn’t I tell you not to sin against \zwd the boy|Q145746\zwd*? But you would not listen. Now we must account for his blood!” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 They did not realize that \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* understood them, since there was an interpreter between them. 
\v 24 And he turned away from them and wept. When he turned back and spoke to them, he took \zwd Simeon|Q1773005\zwd* from them and had him bound before their eyes. 
\s1 Joseph’s Brothers Return to Canaan
\b
\m 
\v 25 Then \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* gave orders to fill their bags with grain, to return each man’s silver \f + \fr 42:25 \ft Or money; here and throughout chapters 42–44\f* to his sack, and to give them provisions for their journey. This order was carried out, 
\v 26 and they loaded the grain on their donkeys and departed. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 At the place where they lodged for the night, one of them opened his sack to get feed for his donkey, and he saw his silver in the mouth of the sack. 
\v 28 “My silver has been returned!” he said to his brothers. “It is here in my sack.” 
\b
\m Their hearts sank, and trembling, they turned to one another and said, “What is this that God has done to us?” 
\b
\m 
\v 29 When they reached \zwd their father Jacob|Q289957\zwd* in the land of Canaan, they described to him all that had happened to them: 
\v 30 “The man who is lord of the land spoke harshly to us and accused us of spying on the country. 
\b
\m 
\v 31 But we told him, ‘We are honest men, not spies. 
\v 32 We are twelve brothers, sons of \zwd one father|Q289957\zwd*. One is no more, and \zwd the youngest|Q460763\zwd* is now with \zwd our father|Q289957\zwd* in the land of Canaan.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 33 Then the man who is lord of the land said to us, ‘This is how I will know whether you are honest: Leave one brother with me, take food to relieve the hunger of your households, and go. 
\v 34 But bring \zwd your youngest brother|Q460763\zwd* back to me so I will know that you are not spies but honest men. Then I will give \zwd your brother|Q1773005\zwd* back to you, and you can trade in the land.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 35 As they began emptying their sacks, there in each man’s sack was his bag of silver! And when they and \zwd their father|Q289957\zwd* saw the bags of silver, they were dismayed. 
\b
\m 
\v 36 \zwd Their father Jacob|Q289957\zwd* said to them, “You have deprived me of my sons. \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* is gone and \zwd Simeon|Q1773005\zwd* is no more. Now you want to take \zwd Benjamin|Q460763\zwd*. Everything is going against me!” 
\b
\m 
\v 37 Then \zwd Reuben|Q625661\zwd* said to \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd*, “You may kill \zwd my two sons|\zwd* if I fail to bring him back to you. Put him in my care, and I will return him.” 
\b
\m 
\v 38 But \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* replied, “\zwd My son|Q460763\zwd* will not go down there with you, for \zwd his brother|Q145746\zwd* is dead, and he alone is left. If any harm comes to him on your journey, you will bring my gray hair down to Sheol in sorrow.” 
\c 43
\s1 The Return to Egypt with Benjamin
\m 
\v 1 Now the famine was still severe in the land. 
\v 2 So when \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*’s sons had eaten all the grain they had brought from Egypt, \zwd their father|Q289957\zwd* said to them, “Go back and buy us a little more food.” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 But \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* replied, “The man solemnly warned us, ‘You will not see my face again unless \zwd your brother|Q460763\zwd* is with you.’ 
\v 4 If you will send \zwd our brother|Q460763\zwd* with us, we will go down and buy food for you. 
\v 5 But if you will not send him, we will not go; for the man told us, ‘You will not see my face again unless \zwd your brother|Q460763\zwd* is with you.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 “Why did you bring this trouble upon me?” \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* asked. “Why did you tell the man you had another brother?” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 They replied, “The man questioned us in detail about ourselves and our family: ‘Is \zwd your father|Q289957\zwd* still alive? Do you have another brother?’ And we answered him accordingly. How could we possibly know that he would say, ‘Bring \zwd your brother|Q460763\zwd* here’?” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 And \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* said to \zwd his father Israel|Q289957\zwd*, “Send \zwd the boy|Q460763\zwd* with me, and we will go at once, so that we may live and not die—neither we, nor you, nor our children. 
\v 9 I will guarantee his safety. You may hold me personally responsible. If I do not bring him back and set him before you, then may I bear the guilt before you all my life. 
\v 10 If we had not delayed, we could have come and gone twice by now.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 Then \zwd their father Israel|Q289957\zwd* said to them, “If it must be so, then do this: Put some of the best products of the land in your packs and carry them down as a gift for the man—a little balm and a little honey, spices and myrrh, pistachios and almonds. 
\v 12 Take double the silver with you so that you may return the silver that was put back into the mouths of your sacks. Perhaps it was a mistake. 
\v 13 Take \zwd your brother|Q460763\zwd* as well, and return to the man at once. 
\v 14 May God Almighty \f + \fr 43:14 \ft Hebrew El-Shaddai\f* grant you mercy before the man, that he may release \zwd your other brother|Q1773005\zwd* along with \zwd Benjamin|Q460763\zwd*. As for me, if I am bereaved, I am bereaved.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 So the men took these gifts, along with double the amount of silver, and \zwd Benjamin|Q460763\zwd* as well. Then they hurried down to Egypt and stood before \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*. 
\s1 Joseph’s Hospitality to His Brothers
\b
\m 
\v 16 When \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* saw \zwd Benjamin|Q460763\zwd* with \zwd his brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220\zwd*, he said to the steward of his house, “Take these men to my house. Slaughter an animal and prepare it, for they shall dine with me at noon.” 
\v 17 The man did as \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* had commanded and took \zwd the brothers|Q460763,Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220\zwd* to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s house. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 But \zwd the brothers|Q460763,Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220\zwd* were frightened that they had been taken to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s house. “We have been brought here because of the silver that was returned in our bags the first time,” they said. “They intend to overpower us and take us as slaves, along with our donkeys.” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 So they approached \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s steward and spoke to him at the entrance to the house. 
\v 20 “Please, sir,” they said, “we really did come down here the first time to buy food. 
\v 21 But when we came to the place we lodged for the night, we opened our sacks and, behold, each of us found his silver in the mouth of his sack! It was the full amount of our silver, and we have brought it back with us. 
\v 22 We have brought additional silver with us to buy food. We do not know who put our silver in our sacks.” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 “It is fine,” said the steward. “Do not be afraid. Your God, the God of your father, gave you the treasure that was in your sacks. I received your silver.” Then he brought \zwd Simeon|Q1773005\zwd* out to them. 
\v 24 And the steward took the men into \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s house, gave them water to wash their feet, and provided food for their donkeys. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 Since \zwd the brothers|Q460763,Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220\zwd* had been told that they were going to eat a meal there, they prepared their gift for \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s arrival at noon. 
\v 26 When \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* came home, they presented him with the gifts they had brought, and they bowed to the ground before him. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 He asked if they were well, and then he asked, “How is \zwd your elderly father|Q289957\zwd* you told me about? Is he still alive?” 
\b
\m 
\v 28 “\zwd Your servant our father|Q289957\zwd* is well,” they answered. “He is still alive.” And they bowed down to honor him. 
\b
\m 
\v 29 When \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* looked up and saw \zwd his brother Benjamin|Q460763\zwd*, \zwd his own mother|id="Q207389" t="2"\zwd*’s son, he asked, “Is this \zwd your youngest brother|Q460763\zwd*, the one you told me about?” Then he declared, “May God be gracious to you, my son.” 
\b
\m 
\v 30 \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* hurried out because he was moved to tears for \zwd his brother|Q460763\zwd*, and he went to a private room to weep. 
\v 31 Then he washed his face and came back out. Regaining his composure, he said, “Serve the meal.” 
\b
\m 
\v 32 They separately served \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, \zwd his brothers|Q460763,Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd*, and the Egyptians. They ate separately because the Egyptians would not eat with the Hebrews, since that was detestable to them. 
\v 33 They were seated before \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* in order by age, from the firstborn to the youngest, and the men looked at one another in astonishment. 
\v 34 When the portions were served to them from \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s table, \zwd Benjamin|Q460763\zwd*’s portion was five times larger than any of the others. So they feasted and drank freely with \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*. 
\c 44
\s1 Benjamin and the Silver Cup
\m 
\v 1 Then \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* instructed his steward: “Fill the men’s sacks with as much food as they can carry, and put each one’s silver in the mouth of his sack. 
\v 2 Put my cup, the silver one, in the mouth of \zwd the youngest one|Q460763\zwd*’s sack, along with the silver for his grain.” 
\b
\m So the steward did as \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* had instructed. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 At daybreak, the men were sent on their way with their donkeys. 
\v 4 They had not gone far from the city when \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* told his steward, “Pursue the men at once, and when you overtake them, ask, ‘Why have you repaid good with evil?\f + \fr 44:4 \ft LXX includes Why have you stolen my silver cup?\f* 
\v 5 Is this not the cup \f + \fr 44:5 \ft Hebrew Is it not this which\f* my master drinks from and uses for divination? What you have done is wicked!’” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 When the steward overtook them, he relayed these words to them. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 “Why does my lord say these things?” they asked. “Your servants could not possibly do such a thing. 
\v 8 We even brought back to you from the land of Canaan the silver we found in the mouths of our sacks. Why would we steal silver or gold from \zwd your master|Q145746\zwd*’s house? 
\v 9 If any of your servants is found to have it, he must die, and the rest will become slaves of my lord.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 “As you say,” replied the steward. “But only the one who is found with the cup will be my slave, and the rest of you shall be free of blame.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 So each one quickly lowered his sack to the ground and opened it. 
\v 12 The steward searched, beginning with the oldest and ending with the youngest—and the cup was found in \zwd Benjamin|Q460763\zwd*’s sack. 
\v 13 Then they all tore their clothes, loaded their donkeys, and returned to the city. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 When \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* and his brothers arrived at \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s house, he was still there, and they fell to the ground before him. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 “What is this deed you have done?” \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* declared. “Do you not know that a man like me can surely divine the truth?” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 “What can we say to my lord?” \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* replied. “How can we plead? How can we justify ourselves? God has exposed the iniquity of your servants. We are now my lord’s slaves—both we and the one who was found with the cup.” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 But \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* replied, “Far be it from me to do this. The man who was found with the cup will be my slave. The rest of you may return to your father in peace.” 
\s1 Judah Pleads for Benjamin
\b
\m 
\v 18 Then \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* approached \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* and said, “Sir, please let your servant speak personally to my lord. Do not be angry with your servant, for you are equal to \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* himself. 
\v 19 My lord asked his servants, ‘Do you have a father or a brother?’ 
\b
\m 
\v 20 And we answered, ‘We have \zwd an elderly father|Q289957\zwd* and \zwd a younger brother|Q460763\zwd*, the child of his old age. \zwd \zwd The boy|Q460763\zwd*’s brother|Q145746\zwd* is dead. He is the only one of \zwd his mother|id="Q207389" t="2"\zwd*’s sons left, and \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd* loves him.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 21 Then you told your servants, ‘Bring him down to me so that I can see him for myself.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 22 So we said to my lord, ‘\zwd The boy|Q460763\zwd* cannot leave \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd*. If he were to leave, \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd* would die.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 23 But you said to your servants, ‘Unless \zwd your younger brother|Q460763\zwd* comes down with you, you will not see my face again.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 24 Now when we returned to \zwd your servant my father|Q289957\zwd*, we relayed your words to him. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 Then \zwd our father|Q289957\zwd* said, ‘Go back and buy us some food.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 26 But we answered, ‘We cannot go down there unless \zwd our younger brother|Q460763\zwd* goes with us. So if \zwd our younger brother|Q460763\zwd* is not with us, we cannot see the man.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 27 And your servant \zwd my father|Q289957\zwd* said to us, ‘You know that \zwd my wife|id="Q207389" t="2"\zwd* bore me \zwd two sons|id="Q145746,Q460763" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 28 When one of them was gone, I said: “Surely he has been torn to pieces.” And I have not seen him since. 
\v 29 Now if you also take this one from me and harm comes to him, you will bring my gray hair down to Sheol in sorrow.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 30 So if \zwd the boy|Q460763\zwd* is not with us when I return to your servant, and if \zwd my father|Q289957\zwd*, whose life is wrapped up in \zwd the boy|Q460763\zwd*’s life, 
\v 31 sees that \zwd the boy|Q460763\zwd* is not with us, he will die. Then your servants will have brought the gray hair of \zwd your servant our father|Q289957\zwd* down to Sheol in sorrow. 
\v 32 Indeed, your servant guaranteed \zwd the boy|Q460763\zwd*’s safety to \zwd my father|Q289957\zwd*, saying, ‘If I do not return him to you, I will bear the guilt before you, \zwd my father|Q289957\zwd*, all my life.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 33 Now please let your servant stay here as my lord’s slave in place of the boy. Let him return with \zwd his brothers|Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd*. 
\v 34 For how can I go back to \zwd my father|Q289957\zwd* without the boy? I could not bear to see the misery that would overwhelm him.” 
\c 45
\s1 Joseph Reveals His Identity
\m 
\v 1 Then \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* could no longer control himself before all his attendants, and he cried out, “Send everyone away from me!” 
\b
\m So none of them were with \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* when he made himself known to \zwd his brothers|Q460763,Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd*. 
\v 2 But he wept so loudly that the Egyptians heard him, and \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*’s household soon heard of it. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* said to \zwd his brothers|Q460763,Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd*, “I am \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*! Is \zwd my father|Q289957\zwd* still alive?” 
\b
\m But they were unable to answer him, because they were terrified in his presence. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Then \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* said to \zwd his brothers|Q460763,Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd*, “Please come near me.” And they did so. 
\b
\m “I am \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, your brother,” he said, “the one you sold into Egypt! 
\v 5 And now, do not be distressed or angry with yourselves that you sold me into this place, because it was to save lives that God sent me before you. 
\v 6 For the famine has covered the land these two years, and there will be five more years without plowing or harvesting. 
\v 7 God sent me before you to preserve you as a remnant on the earth and to save your lives by a great deliverance.\f + \fr 45:7 \ft Or and to keep you alive as a great band of survivors\f* 
\v 8 Therefore it was not you who sent me here, but God, who has made me a father to \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*—lord of all his household and ruler over all the land of Egypt. 
\s1 Joseph Sends for His Father
\b
\m 
\v 9 Now return quickly to \zwd my father|Q289957\zwd* and tell him, ‘This is what your son \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* says: God has made me lord of all Egypt. Come down to me without delay. 
\v 10 You shall settle in the land of Goshen and be near me—you and your children and grandchildren, your flocks and herds, and everything you own. 
\v 11 And there I will provide for you, because there will be five more years of famine. Otherwise, you and your household and everything you own will come to destitution.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 12 Behold! You and \zwd my brother Benjamin|Q460763\zwd* can see that I, \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, am the one speaking with you. 
\v 13 Tell \zwd my father|Q289957\zwd* about all my splendor in Egypt and everything you have seen. And bring \zwd my father|Q289957\zwd* down here quickly.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Then \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* threw his arms around \zwd his brother Benjamin|Q460763\zwd* and wept, and \zwd Benjamin|Q460763\zwd* wept as they embraced. 
\v 15 \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* kissed each of \zwd his brothers|Q460763,Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd* as he wept over them. And afterward \zwd his brothers|Q460763,Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd* talked with him. 
\s1 Pharaoh Invites Jacob to Egypt
\b
\m 
\v 16 When the news reached \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*’s house that \zwd \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s brothers|Q460763,Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd* had come, \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* and his servants were pleased. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* said to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, “Tell your brothers, ‘Do as follows: Load your animals and return to the land of Canaan. 
\v 18 Then bring \zwd your father|Q289957\zwd* and your families and return to me. I will give you the best of the land of Egypt, and you shall eat from the fat of the land.’ 
\v 19 You are also directed to tell them: ‘Take wagons from the land of Egypt for your young children and your wives, and bring \zwd your father|Q289957\zwd* and come back. 
\v 20 But pay no regard to your belongings, for the best of all the land of Egypt is yours.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 21 So the sons of \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* did as they were told. \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* gave them wagons as \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* had instructed, and he also gave them provisions for their journey. 
\v 22 He gave new garments to each of them, but to \zwd Benjamin|Q460763\zwd* he gave three hundred shekels of silver \f + \fr 45:22 \ft 300 shekels is approximately 7.5 pounds or 3.4 kilograms of silver.\f* and five sets of clothes. 
\v 23 And he sent to \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd* the following: ten donkeys loaded with the best of Egypt, and ten female donkeys loaded with grain and bread and provisions for \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd*’s journey. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 Then \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* sent \zwd his brothers|Q460763,Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd* on their way, and as they were leaving, he said to them, “Do not quarrel on the way!” 
\s1 The Revival of Jacob
\b
\m 
\v 25 So \zwd the brothers|Q460763,Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd* went up out of Egypt and came to their father \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* in the land of Canaan. 
\v 26 “\zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* is still alive,” they said, “and he is ruler over all the land of Egypt!” 
\b
\m But \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* was stunned, for he did not believe them. 
\v 27 However, when they relayed all that \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* had told them, and when he saw the wagons that \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* had sent to carry him back, the spirit of \zwd their father Jacob|Q289957\zwd* was revived. 
\b
\m 
\v 28 “Enough!” declared \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd*. “My son \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* is still alive! I will go to see him before I die.” 
\c 46
\s1 Jacob’s Journey to Egypt
\m 
\v 1 So \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* set out with all that he had, and when he came to Beersheba, he offered sacrifices to the God of \zwd his father Isaac|id="Q671872" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 2 And that night God spoke to \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* in a vision: “\zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*!” He said. 
\b
\m “Here I am,” replied \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 “I am God,” He said, “the God of \zwd your father|\zwd*. Do not be afraid to go down to Egypt, for I will make you into a great nation there. 
\v 4 I will go down with you to Egypt, and I will surely bring you back. And \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s own hands will close your eyes.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Then \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* departed from Beersheba, and \zwd the sons of \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd*|Q460763,Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005\zwd* took \zwd their father Jacob|Q289957\zwd* in the wagons \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* had sent to carry him, along with their children and wives. 
\v 6 They also took the livestock and possessions they had acquired in the land of Canaan, and \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* and all his offspring went to Egypt. 
\s1 Those Who Went to Egypt
\r (Exodus 1:1–7)
\b
\m 
\v 7 \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* took with him to Egypt his sons and grandsons, and his daughters and granddaughters—all his offspring. 
\s2 The Children of Leah
\b
\li1 
\v 8 Now these are the names of the sons of \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* (\zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* and his descendants) who went to Egypt: \zwd Reuben|Q625661\zwd*, \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*’s firstborn. 
\b
\li1 
\v 9 The sons of \zwd Reuben|Q625661\zwd*: \zwd Hanoch|Q20757870\zwd*, \zwd Pallu|Q65128328\zwd*, \zwd Hezron|Q85853423\zwd*, and \zwd Carmi|Q85853675\zwd*. 
\b
\li1 
\v 10 The sons of \zwd Simeon|Q1773005\zwd*: \zwd Jemuel|Q24954387\zwd*,\f + \fr 46:10 \ft Jemuel is another name for Nemuel; see Numbers 26:12.\f* \zwd Jamin|Q11716821\zwd*, \zwd Ohad|Q65124128\zwd*, \zwd Jachin|Q18088109\zwd*, \zwd Zohar|Q65124445\zwd*,\f + \fr 46:10 \ft Zohar is a variant of Zerah; see Numbers 26:13 and 1 Chronicles 4:24.\f* and \zwd Shaul the son of a Canaanite woman|Q65125957\zwd*. 
\b
\li1 
\v 11 The sons of \zwd Levi|Q215512\zwd*: \zwd Gershon|Q2915121\zwd*, \zwd Kohath|Q2914557\zwd*, and \zwd Merari|Q2670550\zwd*. 
\b
\li1 
\v 12 The sons of \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd*: \zwd Er|Q1347943\zwd*, \zwd Onan|Q661074\zwd*, \zwd Shelah|Q1294423\zwd*, \zwd Perez|Q1814684\zwd*, and \zwd Zerah|Q3223065\zwd*; but \zwd Er|Q1347943\zwd* and \zwd Onan|Q661074\zwd* died in the land of Canaan. 
\b
\li1 The sons of \zwd Perez|Q1814684\zwd*: \zwd Hezron|Q4177234\zwd* and \zwd Hamul|Q65129982\zwd*. 
\b
\li1 
\v 13 The sons of \zwd Issachar|Q651256\zwd*: \zwd Tola|Q3992381\zwd*, \zwd Puvah|Q85854338\zwd*,\f + \fr 46:13 \ft Hebrew; SP and Syriac Puah; see 1 Chronicles 7:1.\f* \zwd Job|Q85853555\zwd*,\f + \fr 46:13 \ft Hebrew; SP and some LXX manuscripts Jashub; see Numbers 26:24 and 1 Chronicles 7:1.\f* and \zwd Shimron|Q85854694\zwd*. 
\b
\li1 
\v 14 The sons of \zwd Zebulun|Q614575\zwd*: \zwd Sered|Q44126900\zwd*, \zwd Elon|Q44126901\zwd*, and \zwd Jahleel|Q44126902\zwd*. 
\b
\li1 
\v 15 These are the sons of \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd* born to \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* in Paddan-aram,\f + \fr 46:15 \ft That is, northwest Mesopotamia\f* in addition to \zwd his daughter Dinah|Q122035\zwd*. The total number of sons and daughters was thirty-three. 
\s2 The Children of Zilpah
\b
\li1 
\v 16 The sons of \zwd Gad|Q851749\zwd*: \zwd Ziphion|Q85854942\zwd*,\f + \fr 46:16 \ft SP and LXX Zephon; see also Numbers 26:15.\f* \zwd Haggi|Q85853387\zwd*, \zwd Shuni|Q85854721\zwd*, \zwd Ezbon|Q85853189\zwd*, \zwd Eri|Q85853183\zwd*, \zwd Arodi|Q85852721\zwd*,\f + \fr 46:16 \ft Arodi is a variant of Arod; see Numbers 26:17.\f* and \zwd Areli|Q85852725\zwd*. 
\b
\li1 
\v 17 The children of \zwd Asher|Q1775026\zwd*: \zwd Imnah|Q67219744\zwd*, \zwd Ishvah|Q67219745\zwd*, \zwd Ishvi|Q67219746\zwd*, \zwd Beriah|Q85852816\zwd*, and their sister \zwd Serah|Q2899037\zwd*. 
\b
\li1 The sons of \zwd Beriah|Q85852816\zwd*: \zwd Heber|Q85853470\zwd* and \zwd Malchiel|Q85854115\zwd*. 
\b
\li1 
\v 18 These are the sons of \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* born to \zwd Zilpah|Q221826\zwd*—whom \zwd Laban|id="Q840401" t="2"\zwd* gave to his daughter \zwd Leah|Q128847\zwd*—sixteen in all. 
\s2 The Children of Rachel
\b
\li1 
\v 19 The sons of \zwd Jacob|id="Q289957" t="2"\zwd*’s wife \zwd Rachel|id="Q207389" t="2"\zwd*: \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* and \zwd Benjamin|Q460763\zwd*. 
\b
\li1 
\v 20 \zwd Manasseh|Q1056931\zwd* and \zwd Ephraim|Q203251\zwd* were born to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* in the land of Egypt by \zwd Asenath daughter of \zwd Potiphera|Q7234956\zwd*|Q723681\zwd*, priest of On.\f + \fr 46:20 \ft That is, Heliopolis, as in LXX\f* 
\b
\li1 
\v 21 The sons of \zwd Benjamin|Q460763\zwd*: \zwd Bela|Q22018139\zwd*, \zwd Becher|Q85852881\zwd*, \zwd Ashbel|Q85852737\zwd*, \zwd Gera|Q85853373\zwd*, \zwd Naaman|Q31841835\zwd*, \zwd Ehi|Q85853140\zwd*, \zwd Rosh|Q85854575\zwd*, \zwd Muppim|Q85854185\zwd*, \zwd Huppim|Q85853451\zwd*, and \zwd Ard|Q638332\zwd*. 
\b
\li1 
\v 22 These are the sons of \zwd Rachel|Q207389\zwd* born to \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*—fourteen in all. 
\s2 The Children of Bilhah
\b
\li1 
\v 23 The son of \zwd Dan|Q550869\zwd*: \zwd Hushim|Q6582523\zwd*. 
\b
\li1 
\v 24 The sons of \zwd Naphtali|Q1771963\zwd*: \zwd Jahzeel|Q67219740\zwd*, \zwd Guni|Q80198030\zwd*, \zwd Jezer|Q67219747\zwd*, and \zwd Shillem|Q85854718\zwd*. 
\b
\li1 
\v 25 These are the sons of \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* born to \zwd Bilhah|id="Q794991" t="2"\zwd*, whom \zwd Laban|id="Q840401" t="2"\zwd* gave to \zwd his daughter Rachel|id="Q207389" t="2"\zwd*—seven in all. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 All those belonging to \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* who came to Egypt—his direct descendants, besides \zwd the wives of \zwd \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*’s sons|id="Q145746,Q460763,Q215512,Q625661,Q550869,Q851749,Q1771963,Q1775026,Q614575,Q651256,Q282220,Q1773005" t="2"\zwd*|\zwd*—numbered sixty-six persons. 
\v 27 And with the \zwd two sons|Q1056931,Q203251\zwd* \f + \fr 46:27 \ft Hebrew; LXX the nine sons, probably including Joseph’s grandsons through Ephraim and Manasseh; see 1 Chronicles 7:14–29.\f* who had been born to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* in Egypt, the members of \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*’s family who went to Egypt were seventy \f + \fr 46:27 \ft Hebrew (see also Exodus 1:5); LXX (see also Acts 7:14) seventy-five\f* in all. 
\s1 Jacob Arrives in Egypt
\b
\m 
\v 28 \zdate|Q289957:P569+P130Y\*Now \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* had sent \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* ahead of him to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* to get directions to Goshen. When \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*’s family arrived in the land of Goshen, 
\v 29 \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* prepared his chariot and went there to meet \zwd his father Israel|Q289957\zwd*. \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* presented himself to him, embraced him, and wept profusely. 
\b
\m 
\v 30 Then \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* said to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, “Finally I can die, now that I have seen your face and know that you are still alive!” 
\b
\m 
\v 31 \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* said to \zwd his brothers|Q1771963,Q1773005,Q215512,Q282220,Q460763,Q550869,Q614575,Q651256,Q851749,Q1775026,Q625661\zwd* and to \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd*’s household, “I will go up and inform \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*: ‘\zwd My brothers|Q1771963,Q1773005,Q215512,Q282220,Q460763,Q550869,Q614575,Q651256,Q851749,Q1775026,Q625661\zwd* and \zwd my father|Q289957\zwd*’s household from the land of Canaan have come to me. 
\v 32 The men are shepherds; they raise livestock, and they have brought their flocks and herds and all that they own.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 33 When \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* summons you and asks, ‘What is your occupation?’ 
\v 34 you are to say, ‘Your servants have raised livestock ever since our youth—both we and our fathers.’ Then you will be allowed to settle in the land of Goshen, since all shepherds are detestable to the Egyptians.” 
\c 47
\s1 Jacob Settles in Goshen
\m 
\v 1 So \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* went and told \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*: “\zwd My father|Q289957\zwd* and \zwd my brothers|Q1771963,Q1773005,Q215512,Q282220,Q460763,Q550869,Q614575,Q651256,Q851749,Q1775026,Q625661\zwd*, with their flocks and herds and all they own, have come from the land of Canaan and are now in Goshen.” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 And he chose \zwd five of his brothers|\zwd* and presented them before \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 “What is your occupation?” \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* asked \zwd \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s brothers|\zwd*. 
\b
\m “Your servants are shepherds,” they replied, “both we and our fathers.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Then they said to \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*, “We have come to live in the land for a time, because there is no pasture for the flocks of your servants, since the famine in the land of Canaan has been severe. So now, please allow your servants to settle in the land of Goshen.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* said to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, “Now that \zwd your father|Q289957\zwd* and \zwd brothers|Q1771963,Q1773005,Q215512,Q282220,Q460763,Q550869,Q614575,Q651256,Q851749,Q1775026,Q625661\zwd* have come to you, 
\v 6 the land of Egypt is before you; settle \zwd your father|Q289957\zwd* and \zwd brothers|Q1771963,Q1773005,Q215512,Q282220,Q460763,Q550869,Q614575,Q651256,Q851749,Q1775026,Q625661\zwd* in the best part of the land. They may dwell in the land of Goshen. And if you know of any talented men among them, put them in charge of my own livestock.” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Then \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* brought in \zwd his father Jacob|Q289957\zwd* and presented him before \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*, and \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* blessed \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 “How many years have you lived?” \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* asked. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 “My travels \f + \fr 47:9 \ft Hebrew sojourns; twice in this verse\f* have lasted 130 years,” \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* replied. “My years have been few and hard, and they have not matched the years of the travels of my fathers.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Then \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* blessed \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* and departed from his presence. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 So \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* settled \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd* and \zwd brothers|Q1771963,Q1773005,Q215512,Q282220,Q460763,Q550869,Q614575,Q651256,Q851749,Q1775026,Q625661\zwd* in the land of Egypt and gave them property in the best part of the land, the district of Rameses, as \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* had commanded. 
\v 12 \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* also provided \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd* and \zwd brothers|Q1771963,Q1773005,Q215512,Q282220,Q460763,Q550869,Q614575,Q651256,Q851749,Q1775026,Q625661\zwd* and all \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd*’s household with food for their families.\zdate|Q289957:P569+P130Y\* 
\s1 The Famine Continues
\b
\m 
\v 13 There was no food, however, in all that region, because the famine was so severe; the lands of Egypt and Canaan had been exhausted by the famine. 
\v 14 \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* collected all the money to be found in the land of Egypt and the land of Canaan in exchange for the grain they were buying, and he brought it into \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*’s palace. 
\v 15 When the money from the lands of Egypt and Canaan was gone, all the Egyptians came to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* and said, “Give us food. Why should we die before your eyes? For our funds have run out!” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 “Then bring me your livestock,” said \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*. “Since the money is gone, I will sell you food in exchange for your livestock.” 
\v 17 So they brought their livestock to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, and he gave them food in exchange for their horses, their flocks and herds, and their donkeys. Throughout that year he provided them with food in exchange for all their livestock. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 When that year was over, they came to him the second year and said, “We cannot hide from our lord that our money is gone and all our livestock belongs to you. There is nothing left for our lord except our bodies and our land. 
\v 19 Why should we perish before your eyes—we and our land as well? Purchase us and our land in exchange for food. Then we, along with our land, will be slaves to \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*. Give us seed that we may live and not die, and that the land may not become desolate.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 So \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* acquired for \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* all the land in Egypt; the Egyptians, one and all, sold their fields because the famine was so severe upon them. The land became \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*’s, 
\v 21 and \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* reduced the people to servitude \f + \fr 47:21 \ft SP, LXX, Vulgate; Hebrew removed the people to the cities\f* from one end of Egypt to the other. 
\v 22 However, he did not acquire the priests’ portion of the land, for it had been given to them by \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*. They ate the rations that \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* supplied; so they did not sell their land. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 Then \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* said to the people, “Now that I have acquired you and your land for \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* this day, here is seed for you to sow in the land. 
\v 24 At harvest time, you are to give a fifth of it to \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*, and four-fifths will be yours as seed for the field and food for yourselves and your households and children.” 
\b
\m 
\v 25 “You have saved our lives,” they said. “We have found favor in our lord’s eyes, and we will be \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*’s servants.” 
\v 26 So \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* established a law that a fifth of the produce belongs to \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*, and it is in effect in the land of Egypt to this day. Only the priests’ land does not belong to \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*. 
\s1 The Israelites Prosper in Goshen
\b
\m 
\v 27 Now the Israelites settled in the land of Egypt, in the region of Goshen. They acquired property there and became fruitful and increased greatly in number. 
\v 28 And \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* lived in the land of Egypt seventeen years, and the length of his life was 147 years. 
\b
\m 
\v 29 When the time drew near for \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* to die, he called \zwd his son Joseph|Q145746\zwd* and said to him, “If I have found favor in your eyes, put your hand under my thigh and promise to show me kindness and faithfulness. Do not bury me in Egypt, 
\v 30 but when I lie down with my fathers, carry me out of Egypt and bury me with them.” 
\b
\m \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* answered, “I will do as you have requested.” 
\b
\m 
\v 31 “Swear to me,” \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* said. 
\b
\m So \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* swore to him, and \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* bowed in worship at the head of his bed.\f + \fr 47:31 \ft Hebrew; LXX Israel worshiped as he leaned on the top of his staff; see Hebrews 11:21.\f* 
\c 48
\s1 Jacob Blesses Ephraim and Manasseh
\m 
\v 1 Some time later \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* was told, “\zwd Your father|Q289957\zwd* is ill.” So he set out with his \zwd two sons|Q1056931,Q203251\zwd*, \zwd Manasseh|Q1056931\zwd* and \zwd Ephraim|Q203251\zwd*. 
\v 2 When \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* was told, “\zwd Your son Joseph|Q145746\zwd* has come to you,” \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* rallied his strength and sat up in bed. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* said to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, “God Almighty \f + \fr 48:3 \ft Hebrew El-Shaddai\f* appeared to me at Luz in the land of Canaan, and there He blessed me 
\v 4 and told me, ‘Behold, I will make you fruitful and multiply you; I will make you a multitude of peoples, and will give this land to your descendants after you as an everlasting possession.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 5 And now \zwd your two sons|Q1056931,Q203251\zwd* born to you in Egypt before I came to you here shall be reckoned as mine; \zwd Ephraim|Q203251\zwd* and \zwd Manasseh|Q1056931\zwd* shall be mine, just as \zwd Reuben|Q625661\zwd* and \zwd Simeon|Q1773005\zwd* are mine. 
\v 6 Any children born to you after them shall be yours, and they shall be called by the names of their brothers in the territory they inherit. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Now as for me, when I was returning from Paddan,\f + \fr 48:7 \ft That is, northwest Mesopotamia\f* to my sorrow \zwd Rachel|id="Q207389" t="2"\zwd* died along the way in the land of Canaan, some distance from Ephrath. So I buried her there beside the road to Ephrath” (that is, Bethlehem). 
\b
\m 
\v 8 When \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* saw the sons of \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, he asked, “Who are these?” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* said to \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd*, “They are the sons God has given me in this place.” 
\b
\m So \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* said, “Please bring them to me, that I may bless them.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Now \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd*’s eyesight was poor because of old age; he could hardly see. \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* brought \zwd his sons|Q1056931,Q203251\zwd* to him, and his father kissed them and embraced them. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 “I never expected to see your face again,” \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* said to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, “but now God has let me see your children as well.” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 Then \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* removed \zwd his sons|Q1056931,Q203251\zwd* from \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd*’s knees and bowed facedown. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 And \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* took both of them—with \zwd Ephraim|Q203251\zwd* in his right hand toward \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd*’s left hand, and \zwd Manasseh|Q1056931\zwd* in his left hand toward \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd*’s right hand—and brought them close to him. 
\v 14 But \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* stretched out his right hand and put it on the head of \zwd Ephraim|Q203251\zwd*, the younger; and crossing his hands, he put his left on \zwd Manasseh|Q1056931\zwd*’s head, although \zwd Manasseh|Q1056931\zwd* was the firstborn. 
\v 15 Then he blessed \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* and said: 
\b
\q1 “May the God before whom my fathers \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd Isaac|id="Q671872" t="2"\zwd* walked, 
\q2 the God who has been my shepherd all my life to this day, 
\q1 
\v 16 the angel \f + \fr 48:16 \ft Or Angel\f* who has redeemed me from all harm—
\q2 may He bless \zwd these boys|Q1056931,Q203251\zwd*. 
\q1 And may they be called by my name 
\q2 and the names of my fathers \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd Isaac|id="Q671872" t="2"\zwd*, 
\q2 and may they grow into a multitude upon the earth.” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 When \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* saw that \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd* had placed his right hand on \zwd Ephraim|Q203251\zwd*’s head, he was displeased and took \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd*’s hand to move it from \zwd Ephraim|Q203251\zwd*’s head to \zwd Manasseh|Q1056931\zwd*’s. 
\v 18 “Not so, \zwd my father|Q289957\zwd*!” \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* said. “This one is the firstborn; put your right hand on his head.” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 But \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd* refused. “I know, my son, I know!” he said. “He too shall become a people, and he too shall be great; nevertheless, \zwd his younger brother|Q203251\zwd* shall be greater than he, and his offspring shall become a multitude of nations.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 So that day \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* blessed them and said: 
\b
\q1 “By you shall \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* pronounce this blessing: 
\q2 ‘May God make you like \zwd Ephraim|Q203251\zwd* and \zwd Manasseh|Q1056931\zwd*.’” 
\b
\m So he put \zwd Ephraim|Q203251\zwd* before \zwd Manasseh|Q1056931\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 Then \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* said to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, “Look, I am about to die, but God will be with you and bring you back to the land of your fathers. 
\v 22 And to you, as one who is above \zwd your brothers|Q1771963,Q1773005,Q215512,Q282220,Q460763,Q550869,Q614575,Q651256,Q851749,Q1775026,Q625661\zwd*, I give the ridge of land \f + \fr 48:22 \ft Or one portion of the land; Hebrew shekem, which sounds like the town and district called Shechem\f* that I took from the Amorites with my sword and bow.” 
\c 49
\s1 Jacob Blesses His Sons
\m 
\v 1 Then \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* called for \zwd his sons|Q145746,Q1771963,Q1773005,Q215512,Q282220,Q460763,Q550869,Q614575,Q651256,Q851749,Q1775026,Q625661\zwd* and said, “Gather around so that I can tell you what will happen to you in the days to come: 
\b
\q1 
\v 2 Come together and listen, O sons of \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*; 
\q2 listen to \zwd your father Israel|Q289957\zwd*. 
\b
\q1 
\v 3 \zwd Reuben|Q625661\zwd*, you are my firstborn, my might, 
\q2 and the beginning of my strength, 
\q1 excelling in honor, 
\q2 excelling in power. 
\q1 
\v 4 Uncontrolled as the waters, 
\q2 you will no longer excel, 
\q1 because you went up to your father’s bed, 
\q2 onto my couch, and defiled it. 
\b
\q1 
\v 5 \zwd Simeon|Q1773005\zwd* and \zwd Levi|Q215512\zwd* are brothers; 
\q2 their swords \f + \fr 49:5 \ft Or dwelling places\f* are weapons of violence. 
\q1 
\v 6 May I never enter their council; 
\q2 may I never join their assembly. 
\q1 For they kill men in their anger, 
\q2 and hamstring oxen on a whim. 
\q1 
\v 7 Cursed be their anger, for it is strong, 
\q2 and their wrath, for it is cruel! 
\q1 I will disperse them in \zwd Jacob|id="Q289957" t="3"\zwd* 
\q2 and scatter them in \zwd Israel|id="Q289957" t="3"\zwd*. 
\b
\q1 
\v 8 \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd*,\f + \fr 49:8 \ft Judah sounds like the Hebrew for praise.\f* \zwd your brothers|Q145746,Q1771963,Q1773005,Q215512,Q460763,Q550869,Q614575,Q651256,Q851749,Q1775026,Q625661\zwd* shall praise you. 
\q2 Your hand shall be on the necks of your enemies; 
\q2 \zwd \zwd your father|Q289957\zwd*’s sons|Q145746,Q1771963,Q1773005,Q215512,Q460763,Q550869,Q614575,Q651256,Q851749,Q1775026,Q625661\zwd* shall bow down to you. 
\q1 
\v 9 \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd* is a young lion—
\q2 my son, you return from the prey. 
\q1 Like a lion he crouches and lies down; 
\q2 like a lioness, who dares to rouse him? 
\q1 
\v 10 The scepter will not depart from \zwd Judah|Q282220\zwd*, 
\q2 nor the staff from between his feet,\f + \fr 49:10 \ft Or from his descendants\f* 
\q1 until Shiloh \f + \fr 49:10 \ft Or until the one to whom tribute belongs\f* comes 
\q2 and the allegiance of the nations is his. 
\q1 
\v 11 He ties his donkey to the vine, 
\q2 his colt to the choicest branch. 
\q1 He washes his garments in wine, 
\q2 his robes in the blood of grapes. 
\q1 
\v 12 His eyes are darker than wine, 
\q2 and his teeth are whiter than milk. 
\b
\q1 
\v 13 \zwd Zebulun|Q614575\zwd* shall dwell by the seashore 
\q2 and become a harbor for ships; 
\q2 his border shall extend to Sidon. 
\b
\q1 
\v 14 \zwd Issachar|Q651256\zwd* is a strong donkey, 
\q2 lying down between the sheepfolds.\f + \fr 49:14 \ft Or saddlebags or hearths\f* 
\q1 
\v 15 He saw that his resting place was good 
\q2 and that his land was pleasant, 
\q1 so he bent his shoulder to the burden 
\q2 and submitted to labor as a servant. 
\b
\q1 
\v 16 \zwd Dan|Q550869\zwd* shall provide justice for his people \f + \fr 49:16 \ft Or Dan shall judge his people; Dan means He has judged or He has vindicated.\f* 
\q2 as one of the tribes of \zwd Israel|id="Q289957" t="2"\zwd*. 
\q1 
\v 17 He will be a snake by the road, 
\q2 a viper in the path 
\q1 that bites the horse’s heels 
\q2 so that its rider tumbles backward. 
\b
\q1 
\v 18 I await Your salvation, O LORD. 
\b
\q1 
\v 19 \zwd Gad|Q851749\zwd* \f + \fr 49:19 \ft Gad sounds like the Hebrew for raid and also for band of raiders.\f* will be attacked by raiders, 
\q2 but he will attack their heels. 
\b
\q1 
\v 20 \zwd Asher|Q1775026\zwd*’s food will be rich; 
\q2 he shall provide royal delicacies. 
\b
\q1 
\v 21 \zwd Naphtali|Q1771963\zwd* is a doe set free 
\q2 that bears beautiful fawns.\f + \fr 49:21 \ft Or gives beautiful words or bears fawns of the fold\f* 
\b
\q1 
\v 22 \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* is a fruitful vine—
\q2 a fruitful vine by a spring, 
\q2 whose branches scale the wall.\f + \fr 49:22 \ft Or Joseph is a wild donkey, a wild donkey by a spring, with his wild colts beside the wall\f* 
\q1 
\v 23 The archers attacked him with bitterness; 
\q2 they aimed at him in hostility. 
\q1 
\v 24 Yet he steadied his bow, 
\q2 and his strong arms were tempered 
\q1 by the hands of the Mighty One of \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*, 
\q2 in the name of the Shepherd, the Rock of \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd*, 
\q1 
\v 25 by the God of \zwd your father|\zwd* who helps you, 
\q2 and by the Almighty \f + \fr 49:25 \ft Hebrew Shaddai\f* who blesses you, 
\q1 with blessings of the heavens above, 
\q2 with blessings of the depths below, 
\q2 with blessings of the breasts and womb. 
\q1 
\v 26 The blessings of \zwd your father|\zwd* have surpassed 
\q2 the blessings of the ancient mountains \f + \fr 49:26 \ft Or of my ancestors\f* 
\q2 and the bounty of the everlasting hills. 
\q1 May they rest on the head of \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, 
\q2 on the brow of the prince of \zwd his brothers|Q1771963,Q1773005,Q215512,Q282220,Q460763,Q550869,Q614575,Q651256,Q851749,Q1775026,Q625661\zwd*. 
\b
\q1 
\v 27 \zwd Benjamin|Q460763\zwd* is a ravenous wolf; 
\q2 in the morning he devours the prey, 
\q2 in the evening he divides the plunder.” 
\b
\m 
\v 28 These are the tribes of \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd*, twelve in all, and this was what their father said to them. He blessed them, and he blessed each one with a suitable blessing. 
\s1 The Death of Jacob
\b
\m 
\v 29 Then \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* instructed them, “I am about to be gathered to my people. Bury me with my fathers in the cave in the field of \zwd Ephron the Hittite|id="Q6775384" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 30 The cave is in the field of Machpelah near Mamre, in the land of Canaan. This is the field \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd* purchased from \zwd Ephron the Hittite|id="Q6775384" t="2"\zwd* as a burial site. 
\v 31 There \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd his wife Sarah|id="Q194808" t="2"\zwd* are buried, there \zwd Isaac|id="Q671872" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd his wife Rebekah|id="Q40520" t="2"\zwd* are buried, and there I buried \zwd Leah|id="Q128847" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 32 The field and the cave that is in it were purchased from the Hittites.” 
\b
\m 
\v 33 When \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd* had finished instructing his sons, he pulled his feet into the bed and breathed his last, and he was gathered to his people.\zdate|Q289957:P569+P147Y\* 
\c 50
\s1 Mourning and Burial for Jacob
\m 
\v 1 Then \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* fell upon \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd*’s face, wept over him, and kissed him. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 And \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* directed the physicians in his service to embalm \zwd his father Israel|Q289957\zwd*. So they embalmed him, 
\v 3 taking the forty days required to complete the embalming.\zdate|Q289957:P569+P147Y40D\* And the Egyptians mourned for him seventy days\zdate|Q289957:P569+P147Y110D\*. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 When the days of mourning had passed, \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* said to \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*’s court, “If I have found favor in your eyes, please tell \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* that 
\v 5 \zwd my father|Q289957\zwd* made me swear an oath when he said, ‘I am about to die. You must bury me in the tomb that I dug for myself in the land of Canaan.’ Now let me go and bury \zwd my father|Q289957\zwd*, and then return.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* replied, “Go up and bury \zwd your father|Q289957\zwd*, as he made you swear to do.” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Then \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* went to bury his father, and all the servants of \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd* accompanied him—the elders of \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*’s household and all the elders of the land of Egypt—
\v 8 along with all of \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s household, and \zwd his brothers|Q1771963,Q1773005,Q215512,Q282220,Q460763,Q550869,Q614575,Q651256,Q851749,Q1775026,Q625661\zwd*, and \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd*’s household. Only their children and flocks and herds were left in Goshen. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 Chariots and horsemen alike went up with him, and it was an exceedingly large procession. 
\v 10 When they reached the threshing floor of Atad, which is across the Jordan, they lamented and wailed loudly, and \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* mourned for his father seven days. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 When the Canaanites of the land saw the mourning at the threshing floor of Atad, they said, “This is a solemn ceremony of mourning by the Egyptians.” Thus the place across the Jordan is called Abel-mizraim.\f + \fr 50:11 \ft Abel-mizraim means mourning of the Egyptians.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 12 So \zwd \zwd Jacob|Q289957\zwd*’s sons|Q145746,Q1771963,Q1773005,Q215512,Q282220,Q460763,Q550869,Q614575,Q651256,Q851749,Q1775026,Q625661\zwd* did as he had charged them. 
\v 13 They carried him to the land of Canaan and buried him in the cave at Machpelah in the field near Mamre, which \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd* had purchased from \zwd Ephron the Hittite|id="Q6775384" t="2"\zwd* as a burial site. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 After \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* had buried \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd*, he returned to Egypt with \zwd his brothers|Q1771963,Q1773005,Q215512,Q282220,Q460763,Q550869,Q614575,Q651256,Q851749,Q1775026,Q625661\zwd* and all who had gone with him to bury \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd*. 
\s1 Joseph Comforts His Brothers
\b
\m 
\v 15 When \zwd \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s brothers|Q1771963,Q1773005,Q215512,Q282220,Q460763,Q550869,Q614575,Q651256,Q851749,Q1775026,Q625661\zwd* saw that \zwd their father|Q289957\zwd* was dead, they said, “What if \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* bears a grudge? Then he will surely repay us for all the evil that we did to him.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 So they sent word to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, saying, “Before he died, \zwd your father|Q289957\zwd* commanded, 
\v 17 ‘This is what you are to say to \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*: I beg you, please forgive the transgression and sin of \zwd your brothers|Q1771963,Q1773005,Q215512,Q282220,Q460763,Q550869,Q614575,Q651256,Q851749,Q1775026,Q625661\zwd*, for they did you wrong.’ So now, \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*, please forgive the transgression of the servants of the God of your father.” 
\b
\m When their message came to him, \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* wept. 
\v 18 \zwd His brothers|Q1771963,Q1773005,Q215512,Q282220,Q460763,Q550869,Q614575,Q651256,Q851749,Q1775026,Q625661\zwd* also came to him, bowed down before him, and said, “We are your slaves!” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 But \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* replied, “Do not be afraid. Am I in the place of God? 
\v 20 As for you, what you intended against me for evil, God intended for good, in order to accomplish a day like this—to preserve the lives of many people. 
\v 21 Therefore do not be afraid. I will provide for you and your little ones.” So \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* reassured \zwd his brothers|Q1771963,Q1773005,Q215512,Q282220,Q460763,Q550869,Q614575,Q651256,Q851749,Q1775026,Q625661\zwd* and spoke kindly to them. 
\s1 The Death of Joseph
\b
\m 
\v 22 Now \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* and \zwd his father|Q289957\zwd*’s household remained in Egypt, and \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* lived to the age of 110. 
\v 23 He saw \zwd Ephraim|Q203251\zwd*’s sons to the third generation, and indeed the sons of \zwd Machir son of \zwd Manasseh|Q1056931\zwd*|Q85854026\zwd* were brought up \f + \fr 50:23 \ft Or born or placed at birth\f* on \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd*’s knees. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 Then \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* said to \zwd his brothers|Q1771963,Q1773005,Q215512,Q282220,Q460763,Q550869,Q614575,Q651256,Q851749,Q1775026,Q625661\zwd*, “I am about to die, but God will surely visit you and bring you up from this land to the land He promised on oath to \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*, \zwd Isaac|id="Q671872" t="2"\zwd*, and \zwd Jacob|id="Q289957" t="2"\zwd*.” 
\v 25 And \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* made the sons of \zwd Israel|Q289957\zwd* take an oath and said, “God will surely attend to you, and then you must carry my bones up from this place.” 
\b
\m 
\v 26 \zdate|Q145746:P570\*So \zwd Joseph|Q145746\zwd* died at the age of 110. And they embalmed his body and placed it in a coffin in Egypt. 
